(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2022-01-02T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 974 - Goodwood - Counters gate car park', N'It could be tropical weather in Chichester city centre and it would still be blowing a hoolie up at Goodwood Racecourse and today was no exception.

A good turnout (including a welcome back to Hornblower who told us he had nothing better to do) which could be something to do with the promise of mulled wine and nibbles - which hasher doesn''t love a freebie. But drumroll.......we had some VIRGIN HASHERS to welcome! Substandard had obviously promised Eddie and Andrew an experience to remember so let''s hope we didn''t disappoint.

Talking of disappointing, Slightly along with Sausage Lottery, Blindness, Snake Charmer and Bika had brought along their hounds who all decided to have their own noisy hash circle to drown out the human variety at 11am.

Substandard and Olive Oyl talked us through the hash - warm, short, flat, dry etc etc and we were off to find the on out in all directions. Bika went east toward Halnaker Park - Snake Charmer, Slighty Disappointing and Hornblower west toward the Trundle. Bika was the lucky one and surprised most of the pack for two reasons - one he was FRB and the other, we were heading where we were not expecting.
 Out of Counters Gate trees, across the field and down to East Dean Hill (South). A check here found Hornblower knowing the way and into the dry and sure footed (.....not) paths of Halnaker Park. It was here that Snake Charmer discovered Olive Oyl was not a hare to give any clues away.....even to her nearest and dearest as Popeye had not been forewarned to wear any shoes with grips and was last seen slaloming his way down to the bottom paths on his new invention......trainer skis. 

The quagmire that greeted us was inescapable so everyone waded through, with Hornblower being a gentleman and allowing Snake Charmer past to reach the fishhook for four first, with (I think) Bika, Slightly Disappointing and Pancsi joining her? 

Through the woods and toward Selhurstpark Road saw us cross this and over toward East Dean. Eddie and Andrew were doing a sterling job and joining in near the front - they just escaped a fishhook which again caught Snake Charmer, Pancsi and Hornblower.

Back toward East Dean Hill (North) and a bit of confusion about the way as most of the pack decided it couldn''t possibly be in the direction of East Dean as we could smell the chariots - however, Substandard and Olive Oyl had scouted the route well, so off away from Counters Gate we headed. A short skirt around the field found us crossing the road and heading back toward dry clothes.

Hornblower found a blob of something looking remarkably like a fishhook for four parallel with Selhurstpark Road but Snake Charmer had an out of body experience which saw her pick up a stick, channel Pablo Picasso and make it a reworked piece of art resembling an R for regroup. This was met with the call of "cowards" from Olive Oyl but she allowed us to carry on and reach the ON INN with resounding applause, food and drink from those waiting including Treefeller, Soapbox, Tigger and Substandard''s extended family.
 A brilliant and very enjoyable hash - thank you Substandard and Olive Oyl. A gathering (it''s not a party if there is cheese and wine) to eat and drink before the hash it was awarded to Bika for attempting to be posh and sophisticated.
 Although a slightly different Hangover Hash in terms of venue, Dr Blood and Pink Flamingo decided two years at the helm was punishment enough and handed the mantle over to Pancsi, Popeye and Snake Charmer, who in turn thanked them both for steering the club through rough waters of the pandemic and for agreeing to extend their tenure during that time.

Hasher of the Year was said, but it''s a secret until next time......

ONON

Snake Charmer.

 See: photos by Sausage Lottery

(Imported from https://www.chihhh.org.uk/run974.php)', NULL, NULL, NULL, N'United Kingdom', NULL, NULL, N'Goodwood - Counters gate car park', NULL, 50.894581, -0.723823, geography::Point(50.894581, -0.723823, 4326), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 974, N'The 3 Phils (Substandard/Van_Man/Hardcore) and Splasher - Hangover Hash', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, GETDATE(), N'Import chihhh.org.uk', @countryId, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2022-01-16T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 975 - Havant Thicket', N'You have to be crazy to want to go running around in Havant Thicket at this time of the year.
This must be why we gathered there on a bright but cool morning, prepared for a good dose
of shiggy, and welcomed by three Hares and a dog all caked in slowly setting slurry. 
Bika had brought along Lowawatha (Portsmouth HHH) with the aim of using the trail on the
next evening for a bit of shiggy shimmying in the dark, which sounds vaguely erotic, but is
not as good as mud wrestling, or so I am told. 
Popeye, a Hare, left before we started, claiming to have a "family event" that he
needed to attend, however we all knew that he just could not face another tour through the quagmire. 
Some of the usual words were said, I can''t recall exactly what, it''s just that the dogs were
making so much noise they became an embarrassment, so we had to start out in order to
quieten them down. I think that our behaviour is likely to reinforce the dog''s behaviour, i.e.
they bark and howl and we set out. If we got together, waited for them to start howling and
then packed up and went home without a run, they might stop howling, but we are not going
to do that, so each time the noise gets louder, I wonder what Barbara Woodhouse would
have said on this, "who?" you say, I apologise if you are too young to remember. 
Anyway we set out West into Furzy Plain with Spiderman in the lead, his long Nordic pole
glinting in the occasional sunshine. 
You see, if you try and run on the trail you are likely to go A over T on the slithery mud, so
you run on slightly firmer soil to the left or the right and get an eye poked out by a hazel twig,
even if it misses your eye it still leaves a little round bruise on your forehead. 
To add insult to injury Spiderman''s brief time at the head of the pack, came to an end when we
encountered a Fish Hook that caught himself, Olive Oyl, Kinky, and Bambi. 
Relief was at hand as we took the second turn on the right, North into Long Wood and onto
a mud free surface with Olive Oyl somehow back in front after a spirited sprint from the rear
only to hit another fish hook along with Lowawatha and Bika. 
Thus we reached the familiar Havant Thicket Triangle, where compasses go crazy, Hashers
disappear without trace, UFOs are seen and the trail is hard to find. After some rather reluctant
checking we found our way to the West past the pond where as a pup Aragorn nearly drowned
after making a mad dash to snap at a butterfly. 
Now another long drag on a path that gets progressively shiggier, culminating in pools of standing
water at the Western boundary of the thicket. The hares were making use of every shiggy part
they could find, as we now made a clockwise loop South towards Bell''s copse and then North
on the boundary path up Horsefoot Hill. 
With shoes that now resembled clods of earth and weighing a ton Bambi found this gentle climb
quite energy sapping, luckily Snake Charmer was at hand with words of encouragement,
"come on lads, tis but a small mound" she said, we looked up and she was right, it
was a small mound with a little copse on top, "you will have no trouble mounting it"
she added, and she was right, we had no trouble mounting it, although we all had a short
rest at the end of our exertion. 
From this relative height we could have just followed the boundary trail back to the chariots
and it would have been all over, but the hares had other ideas. They took us West again, all
the way back to the check at the end of the shiggyest trail in the thicket in order that we could
have the pleasure of running it in the opposite direction to reach the triangle again. 
Now it was almost all over as we made our way just North of Long Wood and through the
cluster of ponds to reach the On-In after one hour and fifteen minutes of some of the shiggiest
hashing available in Hampshire. 
At the circle, we thanked the hares (one …

