(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2022-12-18T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 998 - Boxgrove Village Hall', N'DID YOU REALLY WANT TO SEE RUN 999 REPORT? CLICK HERE 

What a perfectly ghastly day, caught between the arrival of tropical gulf stream
rain and the departure of deep freeze arctic frostbite, pools of water held in
place by hard frozen earth fed by a constant downpour. 
All plans to have a post hash al fresco feast were crumpled up and thrown in the
bin as we gathered for the run. By coincidence as we were arriving, a group of
runners with a running problem (Chi runners and AC) were departing after having
had their fix of ice skating/running, and looking happy without the
consumption of alcohol, hard to believe, I know. 
We sheltered like a group of penguins, without those eggs between our legs obviously,
under the porch of the village hall, a small group of sodden Santas, a tinsel toting
dog, and Splasher incongruously still wearing mid summer garb, hard as nails. 
To add interest and involvement, we might find a playing card near some falsies,
a prize for a card was promised. With that inducement we set out and busily checked
all the trails near the start without luck, ending up on a true trail North West, across
the A285 and then North towards Home Farm. 
Running in the rain with the wind at your back doesn''t feel too bad, just like someone
put a cold shower under your collar and not in your face, and lets you concentrate
on finding the small yellow clumps that used to be fresh white flour. 
Then we reached a fish hook that caught Snake Charmer, Bambi, The Ref, Red
Sonia and Icky Sticky Sauna. 
At the cross roads before Home Farm, Bambi chose the true trail East to Little
Halnaker and did not get a chance to find a card, very annoying, then turning South
on Park Lane the same thing again, double annoying. Oh, yes we stopped for a
regroup and say "Cheese" moment here, Bambi has to be quick with his
equipment here, whip it out for a quick flash and then stick it back
under the raincoat before it gets wet. 
The long slog down Park Lane to Halnaker was broken by spells of ice skating,
cleaning the mist off foggy spectacles and another fish hook that caught Snake
Charmer, Red Sonia, Popeye, Old Faithful and Bambi. 
At the crossroads the chariots were a mere wombat''s throw away, and a tempting
prospect, however the hares had other ideas, and now with the shower in our right ears,
we headed East into the Tinwood vineyards and then around the edge
of Boxgrove Common on Tinwood Lane. 
It''s at this point that Sausage Lottery, Blind Ness and Substandard ran past a falsie that
had already been pilfered for points by Old Faithful, they continued East past Oakwood
House before the lack of any yellow lumps of dough caused concern. 
By now the pack was homeward bound to the West with the shower in the left ears,
particularly awkward for Old Faithful, " I have picked up a Queen, is that
good?" he says, "Well that depends" Bambi says,
"What? Pardon" he says. 
Some rather odd trail marks, an On-In and Hare arrow left us marooned in the ruins
of the priory, built in 1107 by three guys out of their minds on Bénédictine liqueur
apparently. We got fed up running round the walls waiting for them to collapse, no
Kinky''s horn you see, and then headed over to the porch of the village hall,
to discover that three hashers were missing. 
In these days of modern technology, it was not long before Dr Blood was showing
Bambi where Sausage was, a little blip over there he pointed, and a search party
was sent out, well, just Pink Flamingo actually. 
While waiting for the blip to find it''s way On-In, an ad hoc committee decided to forgo
any circling and rush off to the Old Stables, where eventually we were all united once again. 
Here there was much rejoicing in the warmth, and thankful that no one had managed
to fall despite countless near moments living on the edge with little hope of an
ambulance should one come to grief. 
The …

