(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2023-01-15T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1000 - Hunston Uphill Way', N'T’was a cool 7oC winters day, with a westerly force 6 gusting 7 howling but blue skys.

Having distributed celebratory t-shirts, the JM’s welcomed the group of 36 people to the 1000’th Hash since its foundation in 1983. 

Bambi welcomed Ravinous Curls, the JM from Hazlemere H3, Dr. Pussy and Camping Gaz from Guildford and MJ as a Hash virgin. It was really good to see new faces and some faces that we hadn’t seen for a while including Sure Shot, Tigger a founder member, and her son Guy.

A celebratory photo was taken displaying the new t-shirts, Jackie was awarded the Hash name of Turkey Tail on account of the medicinal mushroom she had foraged.

The hares, Sausage Lottery, Slightly Disappointing and Blind-Ness explained the flour signs and invented a new triangular “danger road ahead” sign for free running dogs (not sure the dogs understood but their owners did).
 From Hunston it was north then east, across the main road and through a shiggy flooded track to a cabbage field. Northwards through skirting Hunston Copse, then east through it before skirting it south south west on a long slippy/slidy track. Sonja rescued a flying bobble hat from the midst of a swampy field for our visitor. 

From here we joined Church Lane heading north West and passed St. Leodegar’s church. Breaking south west on a shiggy track Dr. Pussy showed her mud break dancing skills whilst staying upright (just) before we regrouped a field edge & continued.

Having crossed the main road, we headed due north on the old Selsey tramway line, stopping at the corner of an allotment field for Slightly Disappointing to dispense a homemade bramble port restorative that was far from disappointing. It was then west north west into the biting westerly to the minor road and canal. Grass hopper displayed his mud skating skills and was photographed flat on his back but unharmed (we believe) as we skirted a flooded field. 
 Completing the loop we headed in a north easterly direction along the Chichester canal passing a brave person battling their stand up paddle board into the wind, frisky temperatures for aquatic sports. Then south back to the carpark and Hunston Community Club (HCC) for warmth, victuals and grog having completed 3.4 miles in 55 minutes. Excellently timing indeed, all credit to the hares.
 HCC had very kindly agreed to let us have the function room which took the group nicely, as we spread our feast on a well-protected pool table.

Having more than replenished the calories spent and rehydrated, Snake Charmer awarded the Hash-It to Dag for being outrageous and the Hasher of the year to Sausage Lottery, for enlivening the group, providing picnics and organising the HCC for us. The elaborate hinged seat was emblazoned with a plaque of current and past winners, recently bought up to date by Old Faithful, for which we are most grateful.
 The 3 joint masters then handed over the reins to the new Joint Masters, Bambi, Old Faithful and Splasher. Splasher offered his apologies for non-attendance finding Lanzarote warmer.

Bambi regaled us with a brief(ish) history of the hash, which had clearly involved many hours of web research to pull out the good parts of particularly memorable hashes to the merriment of the group. It was accompanied by a looped slide show of the photos from the website, which was great

A raffle was then completed awarding 50 or so prizes to the lucky winners and the feasting had come to a close. 14:30 was time for tidy up and departure.
 On-On,

Popeye
 See: Spiderman''s track 
... AND ... photos by Old Faithful et al 
 >

(Imported from https://www.chihhh.org.uk/run1000.php)', NULL, NULL, NULL, N'United Kingdom', NULL, NULL, N'Hunston Uphill Way', NULL, 50.809082, -0.779933, geography::Point(50.809082, -0.779933, 4326), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 1000, N'Sausage Lottery & Blind Ness & Slightly Disappointing - HHH 1000th Run !!!', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, GETDATE(), N'Import chihhh.org.uk', @countryId, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2023-01-29T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1001 - Droke, direction East Dean off A285', N'"Blow me down! this was a muddy one! T’was a grey winter day, 8 degrees C. and dry with the occasional light drizzle in Droke Lane car park.
 The 17 of us motley crew, were rounded up by our new JM, Bambi for the 1001 palindromic hash.

Bambi, welcomed Julia to the group. Sonja was given (Ed: provisionally) the Hash name of Bridget Kneels and clarified that Eddie’s name was Eddie the Eagle henceforth. We bid the name, “Rock Steady Eddie” a fond farewell.
 Old Faithful told us the course was smooth sailin'' without no roads. So we set off on the choppy waters of a muddy hill, churned up by them landlubbers'' forestry work to the west, checkin'' then southward. At this point, Can-man, Vixen and Prancer decided that the deep mud and steep climbs really were risking life and limb, and sailed a shorter course.

Having climbed the 40m hill, the track went east before descending though the woods back to road level east again. Having reached the extent of the forestry work the track, Snake Charmer found the route up again to the South. The whole group overran a kicked-out falsie and Substandard found the route south to the top of the hill near Selhurst Park Road where we regrouped (unofficially).
 We set sail northward, me hearties, and descended 40 meters deep. We kept straight on at the check and climbed 45 meters westward to the top of the 70 meter climb. But just as we were levelin'' off, we spotted a fish-hook we hadn''t seen before. But it were a fish-hook for no one, and we were mighty relieved. We regrouped as signaled and set sail again.
 At the crossroads, me mateys resisted the urge to head downhill to the carpark and instead sailed westward. We then veered northward on a long and gentle incline, headin'' towards the carpark. After checkin'' our course, we spotted the on-inn and soon enough, we could make out the carpark through the thicket, havin’ travelled 3.5 miles in 50 minutes ( Ed: 88min more likely).
 In the circle, Eddie the Eagle was awarded the Hash-it for advising the group that clothes and a meat sausage had been spotted in the forest, left by picknickers of uncertain intensions. We espied no such thing, t’was a myth, or a rumour to stir our curiosity?
 The gently sloping wet muddy carpark proved easy travel for the up-hill parkers but otherwise for down-hill adventurers. 6 of us pushed and pulled Spiderman and Spiderwoman’s car off the grass with its wheels spinning and onto the gravel of greater traction.

The Selhurst arms provided welcome grog, warmth and shelter from our refreshing ordeal.

On-on,

Popeye (arrrr)
 See: Bambi''s track 
... AND ... photos by Sonia & Bambi

(Imported from https://www.chihhh.org.uk/run1001.php)', NULL, NULL, NULL, N'United Kingdom', NULL, NULL, N'Droke, direction East Dean off A285', NULL, 50.906426, -0.686411, geography::Point(50.906426, -0.686411, 4326), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 1001, N'Old Faithful & Eddie the Eagle', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, GETDATE(), N'Import chihhh.org.uk', @countryId, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2023-02-12T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1002 - Stansted Garden Centre Overflow CP', N'T’was a cool 8 degrees, on a grey winters day. The drizzle came and went with little breeze as the troupe of 22 gathered in Stanstead over-flow car park.
 Old Faithful welcomed us to run 1002 and also to Caroline for joining us. The canine presence impressed us with totalling 8, a recent record perhaps?
 The hares, Kinky and Vixen explained the basics, for there were no roads where we were heading, only sheep and highland cattle. Like a wounded jack in the box we were off. Most headed north east, ha ha! for the one true way lay to the south. Having reached the wood edge, a hairpin turn took us north east on Park Lane track to Sindles Farm. 

A check deviated us across the rough grazing and sheep straight as a Roman road. A fish hook caught the unwary, Olive Oil, The Ref, MJ, Slightly Disappointing and Popeye, sending us to the back of the pack.
 Before long a check sent us West towards Stanstead house, followed by another sending us north north east on a path across pastures green. 2 deer decided to show us how running should be done as they bounded elegantly across the swathe. Vixens youngster attempted such an elegant gait for some distance, but shorter legs were no match. Having regrouped, with strength anew we set off and a check sent us east then another north west. Down past Lumley wood, across a small lane and then up past Long Copse though a field of really picturesque and docile highland cattle with their long horns looking completely at one with their lot. The route then took us south south west, past a fish hook and a westerly turn into a dell. 

Slightly Disappointing, noted the, “no dogs” sign and doubled back to take the alternative route south. The dog free fraction and those with dogs headed south on parallel courses before joining up on the Stanstead drive in. We completed the encirclement of Stanstead house as we headed for the chariots.
 In the post run circle, Old Faithful thanked the Kinky and Vixen for a lovely run that was and remarkably mud free for the time of year. 

The Hash-it was awarded to, Olive Oil for comments such as “I thought that we were coming for a run” as she bounded past the troupe from a fish hook in the reverse direction. 
 Grog at the Cricketers in Westbourne was the final destination having completed the 4.4 mile run in a steady 80 minutes.
 On-On,
 Popeye
 See: Spiderman''s track 
... AND ... photos by Old Faithful

(Imported from https://www.chihhh.org.uk/run1002.php)', NULL, NULL, NULL, N'United Kingdom', NULL, NULL, N'Stansted Garden Centre Overflow CP', NULL, 50.884084, -0.924542, geography::Point(50.884084, -0.924542, 4326), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 1002, N'Vixen and Kinky', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, GETDATE(), N'Import chihhh.org.uk', @countryId, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2023-02-26T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1003 - Eartham Woods', N'T’was a cool 6oC, on a grey dry winters day with little breeze as the troupe of 15 regulars gathered in Eartham Woods car park.

Splasher welcomed the group to run 1003, the canine presence being a more normal 3 after the 8 on the last run.

The hares, Bambi and Old Faithful explained the basics and we were off.
 On east was outbound with the tiniest piece of road work before re-entering the forest heading north east, with a left right kink. Spiderman was convinced that we were heading up Nore hill, as we hadn’t visited it for a while, but all his checking in that direction was in vain. Heading generally north west there was a fish hook for 4 people and 1 dog! Aragorn, bless him, shot back to the advancing pack as fast as his little legs would carry him and returned to the check on command.
 The sun came out as we bounded uphill through the rustling beach forest but Aragorn disgraced himself by watering a check.

Substandard met a furniture maker friend cycling through the forest doubtlessly hunting for raw materials, and so stopped for a chat, as you do, whilst the serious Hashers streamed past.

Having completes a rather angular heart shape loop in memory of Valentines day past, in North Wood, we headed down hill through the forest paths and rustling leaves to the chariots.
 In the post run circle, Bambi educated us in the art of singing traditional Hashing ballads and issued alcoholic and non-alcoholic “down downs” to Popeye and Olive Oil for not carrying the Hash-it around the circuit, Bica for Aragorn’s behaviour on a check, and the Hares for laying the trail. The Hash-it was awarded to, Substandard for chatting to strange men in the forest. Then we were off for grog at the Anglesey Arms in Halnaker, having completed the 4.6 mile run (including falsies) in a steady 85 minutes.
 On-On,

Popeye
 See: Spderman''s track 
... AND ... photos by Old Faithful & Bambi

(Imported from https://www.chihhh.org.uk/run1003.php)', NULL, NULL, NULL, N'United Kingdom', NULL, NULL, N'Eartham Woods', NULL, 50.887992, -0.666469, geography::Point(50.887992, -0.666469, 4326), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 1003, N'Bambi & Old Faithful', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, GETDATE(), N'Import chihhh.org.uk', @countryId, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2023-03-12T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1004 - Chidham Maybush Copse', N'T’was a bright/overcast 9oC, which felt warm-ish in the sun/wind shadow with a steady south westerly force 4 breeze as the group of 10 gathered in Maybush Copse, Chidham.
 Bambi welcomed the group (plus Aragorn and Belle) to run 1004 and regaled us with essential facts, such as the world wide web being invented and Uranus discovered on this day, at a random time in the past.
 The hare was Popeye. Olive Oil had helped lay the trail but had to disappear for a teaching commitment and co-hare Sub-Standard was unfortunately struck lame with an Achilles issue. The tracks, whilst superb the previous weekend, had been rendered really quite greasy by inclement weather, but remained incredibly flat with some small bits of minor road work. Popeye set off alarm bells mentioning a 6.8 mile route check - errors were made - actual route much shorter.

 On west was outbound through the little copse. Most of the flour had been eaten/removed causing confusion, but the true path was found zig zagging to the sea wall curling back towards Prinstead only to find a back-check. South along the sea wall the route was discovered, and on until a short back check indicated that the route lay east along the very roughly square course. A fish hook for 4 caught Bambi, Snake Charmer, Old Faithful and Kinky.

 Once back at Cot lane, there was a short south section before branching left onto a south heading path. A check straight on fooled the FRBs (Bambi, Snake Charmer and Kinky) who headed west too eagerly/early, a correct thought but west was at the next check on the road. With a check at the junction and falsies north and south, the trail past banks of primroses and huge green houses that had seen better days as it headed to the next check at Chidham Lane, but not before a fish hook for 3 caught Bambi, Snake Charmer and Old Faithful. It was then up on to the sea wall before descending onto the field edge path. Rather picturesque views of Bosham from Chidham Creek view point were photographed. Up again onto the sea wall north towards the main road, a third fish hook for 2 caught Bambi, Snake Charmer for the third time before branching left, through an extremely slippery bog and out onto Hamstead Medow. The trail joined Chidham lane and the On-Inn was back along the main road to the waggons.

 The post run circle in the shelter of the rather lovley, spring, Maybush copse, the birds were tweeting for all their worth.
 Substandard and Popeye laid on beer/cider/fruit juice, cheese, biscuits and fruit. Whilst Bambi handed out traditional hashing ballad hymn sheets and issued “down downs” to:-

•Popeye for breaking health and safety rules, jogging in a most unsteady fashion along the sea wall edge whilst chatting,

•Sonya for keeping her new t-shirt in pristine condition at the expense of a muddy Iphone/hands,

•Kinky for route advice from strangers.

•Popeye was awarded the Hash-it.
 A couple of us visited the BarleyCorn Tavern for warm drinks from really friendly bar staff, having completed the 3.7 mile core run in a steady 75 minutes.
 On-On,

Popeye
 See: Bambi''s track 
... AND ... photos by Old Faithful & Bambi

(Imported from https://www.chihhh.org.uk/run1004.php)', NULL, NULL, NULL, N'United Kingdom', NULL, NULL, N'Chidham Maybush Copse', NULL, 50.841377, -0.883313, geography::Point(50.841377, -0.883313, 4326), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 1004, N'Substandard and Popeye', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, GETDATE(), N'Import chihhh.org.uk', @countryId, NULL, NULL, NULL);
