(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2024-03-17T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1030 - Emsworth Dymoke St Car Park', N'Saint Paddie''s day and in keeping with Irish tradition the day started with a torrential
downpour that made laying the trail a distinctly moist exercise, spill a little flour on
a wet hand and things get very sticky as a white glutinous paste forms and transfers to
everything you touch. 
As well as wet it is unseasonably warm, and Old Faithful is wearing
too many layers, working up a sweat as we scamper thither and yon,
some runs down his forehead into his eyes, and instinctively he screws up
forefinger and thumb and has a good rub, leaving a thick glutinous mass on his brow. 
Oh! enough griping from the hares, by the time we were ready
for the main event the rain had stopped and Kinky was trying to get a laugh with some jokes
at the expense of the Irish but was only able to raise a small titter amongst the Hashers. 
We all said a "welcome back" to Wayne before Old Faithful attempted to explain that there would
be numbered sticks to collect en route indicated by a system of marking that''s far too
complicated to go into here. 
Then with great ceremony the JMs announced a new tradition for
the holder of the Ha-sh-it, in the form of a special tee shirt to be worn by the aforementioned
person. Just the one shirt to be passed on at the end of each run, was it said that it
should not be washed? ... surely not, that would be unsanitary insanity. 
Having run out of anything more to say, a dozen Hashers entered Hollybank Wood in search
of glutinous masses and prize sticks. What did they find first?, that would be MUD.
Hiawatha was an early FRB as we slithered and slid through the scrub North on the Western
edge of the woods until nearly in the roar of the traffic passing Southleigh Park House. 
The lure of a stick took Dr Blood to the limit in this direction while the pack made a one
eighty North East alongside Emsworth Common Road. 
We could go on like this describing every twist and turn, I,d be boring, you''d be
bored, lets cut to the highlights like "Hash Of The Day"

 The Regroup in the grove of ancient yews, where we consumed a bottle of Baileys, shot
by shot until I swear I saw a few of the little people dancing, "look, just over there!". 
 Then there was the competition for the stick on the other side of the big ditch that''s a main tributary of The
Ems, Wayne having a happy splashy time. Loosing Blind Ness for a while. 
The Ref takes a tumble, lets see that again in slow motion, was he offside?. 
 Sausage Lottery muttering something about something nearly being sucked off as we traversed
the squelchy stuff. 

There are no distant views in these woods, its lots of intersecting paths through bits of bog, copse and
streams, so when we reached the On-In it came as a surprise, after one hour twenty five minutes and
thirty four seconds of total shiggy trail. Looking around it appeared that the dogs were cleaner than the
Hashers, all really needed a good hose down however. 
In the car park, Pru arrived, set up a table and covered it with a veritable banquet,
for which we are very thankful; the cans of Guinness came out and we started on
down downs while munching. 
Due to the effects of Baileys and Guinness, I am afraid Bambi lost track of all the out of tune
tunes which were rendered while various Hashers glug glug glugged. Certainly The Hares, The Ref
and Dr Blood as fallers, Hiawatha with an ARMY tee shirt that obviously was missing
the first letter, B. 
Spiderman inherited the Has-sh-it loo seat and new tee shirt for telling us all to remember that the
clocks go back one hour just before the next Hash. 
And finally the sticks, remember them? very generously Old Faithful swapped each one for a prize,
the booby sort were cans of good old Irish stew, the star prize was a bottle of Baileys, I think it went
home with The Ref. Perhaps he will see the Little People around Selsey soon.

 On! - On! Bambi 

 Bambi''s track .....
 Walkers'' track 
... AND ... photos by Bambi

(Imported from https://www.chihhh.org.uk/run1030.php)', NULL, NULL, NULL, N'United Kingdom', NULL, NULL, N'Emsworth Dymoke St Car Park', NULL, 50.864542, -0.947103, geography::Point(50.864542, -0.947103, 4326), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 1030, N'Old Faithful and Bambi (St Paddy''s day green theme)', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, GETDATE(), N'Import chihhh.org.uk', @countryId, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2024-03-31T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1031 - Westbourne - Cricketers', N'It was Bright enough for us to cast a feint shadow outside The Cricketers on a calm day at the fag end of March.
Not many shadows at that. We were very happy to see two visitors, Night Rider (PH3) and virgin Kim
(London), with their arrival the average age of the pack was greatly reduced from it''s level near the geriatric
end of the scale. 
Bika drove past in a bright blue micro chariot, looking for a parking spot, and while we waited
for him to join us, Wayne got something off his chest. At the previous run, during the apres Hash feeding frenzy
Wayne had accidentally sent Bambi''s old coffee mug to the grim shard reaper with a sweeping movement of the
Hand. Now he was pleased to present Bambi with a fine unique replacement bearing an image of the famous
cartoon character created by Walt and some words "Bambi HHH Runner" just in case there is any doubt as to
who''s mug it is. 
Finally Bika arrived and we set off to the clamorous emanations from Kinky''s horn roughly West
on Commonside. In the usual general rushing about to find the start of a trail, Wayne and Bambi stumbled on
the "On-In" marking, as laid out in rule 42 of the Hashing Code they immediately stared into their Men In Black
Neuralyzer and forgot . 
The trail was found, not West on School Lane, which would have been too easy, but
deeper into the Westbourne metropolis on North Street and then West along Long Copse Lane. The reason
for this became apparent when we discovered that this potholed scraggy road was flooded. Most Hashers
enjoy a good splashy experience in horse poop infused water. 
By now our visitors and Wayne were well ahead
of the rest, and increasingly so because the Hares did not go into the parkland South of the lane as any sane
person might expect, but remained true to a long slog to the very end of Longcopse. Here at least was a Fish
Hook for four which caught Kim, Night Rider, Wayne and Bambi, and we were all together again. 
You will never guess what''s just North of the end of the Lane, it''s an entrance to Hollybank Wood, the very same
turf that we trampled on just a fortnight ago, and that is where we went next. This time however we did not
face the knee deep quagmire that this place offers in spades, because the trail pretty much went directly
North to cross Emsworth Common Road on The Sussex Border Path. Of course there were plenty of taken
opportunities for false trails to right and left, each one and adventure into shiggy culminating in the dreaded
three dots, and no sight of The Little People that were here last time. 
Across the road, in Shuffles Plantation
the same tactic was employed as we raced to Woodberry Lane along the border with a Fish Hook that
caught the same four fish bringing us together once again as we headed South East towards Monk''s Hill. 
We''d been warned of tarmac at the start, and this was a long stretch that revealed our relative fitness in a
very visual way, with the young fit things becoming distant dots while bringing up the rear the "less fit"
were deep in discussion about various infirmities and how hard it is to get a doctor''s appointment these days. 
Luckily, by the time the rearguard, including Bambi, reached the stile onto Monks Hill Road, the pack had come to a halt,
not sure why?, anyway, we had a "say cheese" moment before taking the obvious route down the hill, through the
playing field to find the long forgotten On-In after just on the hour of quick quick slow hashing. 
We dashed into The Cricketers, no contention with Sunday Lunchers here, there aren''t any, just don''t sit where the regulars
usually plant themselves and everything is dandy. Soon we were quaffing ale and our JM convened a small
circle, first administering a double dose of down down to Prancer (Hare), who took one for Cheesy Chips (Hare)
not present due to exhaustion and we are very grateful to them another challenging trail. Then of course Night Rider
and the virgin …

Full run report: https://www.chihhh.org.uk/run1031.php', NULL, NULL, NULL, N'United Kingdom', NULL, NULL, N'Westbourne - Cricketers', NULL, 50.868498, -0.924304, geography::Point(50.868498, -0.924304, 4326), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 1031, N'Prancer and crew (Vixen''s birthday) - Clocks go forward', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, GETDATE(), N'Import chihhh.org.uk', @countryId, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2024-04-14T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1032 - Stansted Park Sth CP', N'It was a beautiful day the day before, but hazy sun and 13°C as the 13 of us and Aragorn met in the Stanstead 5th carpark on Broad Walk. Having warmed up to 16°C by the end of the hash, it was one of those spring days that start cool but rapidly warm up. Kinky welcomed us to Hash number 1032 and regaled us with the wisdom that President Lincon was shot on this day in 1865 and the Titanic hit an iceberg on this day in 1912. Both incidents happened late in the day. President Lincon died the following day and the Titanic sank the following day. On that joyful note, he then handed over to the Hares, who were Old Faithful and himself, mentioning a fish hook and a regroup.
 The trail started out along one of the Stanstead rides heading west. We then ducked into the forest before a check caught us all out and sent us back onto the ride. The bluebells were in full bloom with a superb fragrance. The bees were buzzing and the birds tweeting. There were laminated signs in the forest explaining why branches were left on the forest floor (for wild life), selected oaks, other trees and a Norwegian airman who crashed in 1942. One of the most informative forests I’ve seen. There were no mushrooms to report but the wild garlic, hawthorn shoots, nettle, bramble and ground elder shoots are all tasty this time of year.
 An early fish hook caught The Ref, Hiawatha, Olive Oyl and Wayne. Whilst wending our way through the forest we saw 2 deer gently grazing. They looked up, then carried on grazing. Another 2 deer were spotted later on. Popeye spotted an Oil Beetle, with an oddly shaped blue/black body, about 25mm long. A recordable beetle apparently, the web says that it is, “a conspicuous, charismatic beetle that are so-called because they exude a yellowish oily substance from their leg joints when threatened”. Hiawatha photographed it and we left it to its sun bathing. I’m not sure that I have met a, “charismatic beetle” before.
 We came across the regroup to the north west of the forest with twin bench seats carved out of a large tree trunk, skirted a fenced off enclosure and headed back towards the car park using part of the north west ride.
 The trail was 3.6 miles, completed in a relaxed 77 minutes.
 In the post run circle, down-downs were awarded to:-

 The Hares for a jolly good run. 
 Hiawatha for not being at the front all the time. Excuses of sea swimming earlier that day and a half marathon race were overruled. 
 Popeye for complaining and Olive Oyl for reporting him! One of the most enjoyable quick drinks, I’ve had in ages. 
 Wayne and Bica, because they were thirsty! 

 The Hash-it was awarded to Old Faithfull for, “having no sole”, literally! The front half of his trainer sole was flapping independently of the rest.
 On the 16th June, the Isle of Wight have invited us over for a joint hash, The Wheatsheaf at Brading, Sandown. This is in between our normal hashes.
 We then departed headed for refreshments in the Cricketers, Westbourne.
 As I toil over this report, Olive Oyl commented that it was, “a very good Hash”, I wholeheartedly concur.
 On-On,

Popeye.
 Runners'' track ... Walkers'' track 
... AND ... photos by Oild Faithful

(Imported from https://www.chihhh.org.uk/run1032.php)', NULL, NULL, NULL, N'United Kingdom', NULL, NULL, N'Stansted Park Sth CP', NULL, 50.887759, -0.929663, geography::Point(50.887759, -0.929663, 4326), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 1032, N'Kinky & Old Faithful', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, GETDATE(), N'Import chihhh.org.uk', @countryId, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2024-04-28T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1033 - Goodwood - Counters gate car park', N'You can tell from the snaps, it''s the way everyone is a little hunched and clutching
their vitals, it was rather cool in the exposed Counters Gate car park on a grey
windswept morning. 
Kinky tried his best to cheer us up with a few merry quips and also something from
history, something about captain James T Cook discovering Bounty Bars in Australia
which caused his crew to mutiny. 
Unusually there was no sign of canine friends, so no barcophony to accompany the
Hares in their usual song of warnings of wet chalk, flint points and slimy roots. 
Kinky exercised his horn with gusto, and we all set off South West like rampant 
impala over the low grass of The Serengeti. Soon a Westerly trail parallel to
Racecourse Road was found, a twisty progress on wet gravel where those ahead
and those behind disappeared from view, a while of running alone with spring
birdsong and then a string of Hashers, the faces of Dr Blood, Dr Pussy, Popeye,
Wayne, Old Faithful and Slightly Disappointing, heading for the back of the pack
having reached a fish hook somewhere ahead. 
Our progress now brought us just South of the big car park where in August the
Glorious Goodwood tour buses will disgorge their occupants for a day boozing and
betting. 
Then continuing West to the edge of the woods below the grassy knoll where we
had an impromptu regroup and natter on various topics, comparing ailments,
news of recent runs and so forth. 
On the restart we wandered around, lost on the slope of the grassy knoll until the
Hares drew our attention to a massive arrow with enough flour to have made a
decent sized loaf. This pointed down the hill, South and back into the trees. Bambi
mentioned to Hiawatha (Hare) that it was to be expected that no one had noticed
such a large mark amongst the ankle high lush grass, she replied that she found
it hard laying in the grass, Bambi''s tongue started to bleed. 
Now a great downhill charge South East into the trees on slippery white chalk
infused with razor sharp flint, dicing with death until a fish hook caught Bambi, Old
Faithful and Wayne. 
Soon we found The Plantation on our left, this is a No Go area bounded by a tall
iron railing that must be worth a fortune in scrap value, it would be nice to go in
there, the forbidden fruit. Thus we descended to Appletree Bottom, from where
the only way is North up a broad path of soft pine needles and crunchy beech
nuts in the shelter of tall trees, lovely running at any time of the year. 
This path goes directly to the chariot park, but the Hares obviously did not
take us there directly, no, an anticlockwise loop to the East was laid, where
only the FRBs ran the whole way because the rest of the pack spotted them
at the top and made their way there directly. We arrived at the On-In after
about fifty two minutes of dancing on flint. 
Down downs for The Hares, Welcome Backs, and since Wayne had just
completed The London Marathon he had the Hash-It for being a Race - ist.
We then settled down to munching our way through the delicious buffet and
quaffilng the grog that the Hares kindly provided.

 On! - On! Bambi 

 Runners'' track ... Walkers'' track 
... AND ... photos by Bambi

(Imported from https://www.chihhh.org.uk/run1033.php)', NULL, NULL, NULL, N'United Kingdom', NULL, NULL, N'Goodwood - Counters gate car park', NULL, 50.894581, -0.723823, geography::Point(50.894581, -0.723823, 4326), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 1033, N'Sonia and The Ref', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, GETDATE(), N'Import chihhh.org.uk', @countryId, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2024-05-12T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1034 - Ambersham Common New Road', N'It was a beautiful day, blue sky with white fluffy clouds. 24oC with a light breeze as the 7 of us (and Aragorn) met Ambersham Common car park on New Road. 

Many regulars were off on other runs or holidays, hence the reduced number. Kinky welcomed us to Hash number 1034 and handed over to the Hares, who were Olive Oyl and myself. 

We mentioned the usual symbols, 1 road (twice) and some fish hooks. Also that we got lost towards the end and ran out of time, so we panicked, marked the trail with an on-inn and headed back to the carpark along the power lines. 

With that we were off - on up.
 Ambersham Common is lovely and sandy, so it drains well. Crossing the road we headed up the hill where a fish hook caught, Bika, The Ref. Kinky and Bika again! being such a small group with only 3 runners and 4 walkers. We circled around anti-clockwise and another fish hook caught Bika, The Ref. Kinky and Spiderman. We then crossed back over the road and headed along the old railway line before disappearing left into the woods towards Little London. After a fish hook caught Bika, The Ref. Kinky for the third time we circled clockwise back to the railway line and across it on a brick bridge before an anticlockwise loop back to the line again.
 The smell of pine needles was superb, with crunchy pine cones under foot. I do love this time of year, it is great to be out on the heath. After circling anti clockwise and a re-group photo, we came to the rather remote on-Inn and the power lines guiding us back to the car park.
 The trail was 3.9 miles, completed in a relaxed 84 minutes.
 We then headed for refreshments and down-downs at the Unicorn, Heyshott only to find it closed for a Rolls Royce rally, but the beer garden at the Partridge (Singleton) was open.
 In the post run circle, down-downs were awarded to:-

 The Hares for a good run. 
 Bika and Kinky for being FRBs. 
 Bika for running so many false trails, the hares kept advising, “don’t follow Bika”. 
 Spiderman and Spiderwoman for participating on such a lovely day. 

The Hash-it was awarded to Bika.
 On the 16th June, the Isle of Wight have invited us over for a joint hash, The Wheatsheaf at Brading, Sandown at 11:00. 

This is in between our normal hashes Sundays. 

2 Train tickets are suggested from Chichester (1 to Portsmouth & Southsea station and 1 from there to Brading), but if you leave from Havant you can do it in 1 ticket. 

The Portsmouth and Southsea ticket includes the bus trip to the Hovercraft terminal and train at the other end. 

It costs £33 return (but less with a railcard) and a 9:10 departure arrives at 10:25.

On-On,

Popeye.
 Runners'' track ... AND ... photos by Spiderman

(Imported from https://www.chihhh.org.uk/run1034.php)', NULL, NULL, NULL, N'United Kingdom', NULL, NULL, N'Ambersham Common New Road', NULL, 50.966949, -0.702468, geography::Point(50.966949, -0.702468, 4326), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 1034, N'Olive Oyl and Popeye', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, GETDATE(), N'Import chihhh.org.uk', @countryId, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2024-05-26T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1035 - Slindon Northwood', N'You can check in the annals if you want, after over a thousand runs we have never started from this venue
before, so full marks go to the hares for novelty. The weather was typical bank holiday fare, cool with threatening
clouds mixed with periods of blistering ultra violence. A smallish turnout mingled at the start with walkers and
equestrians who also have found this spot, in some ways a larger turnout might have caused chariotoparkophobia. 
We welcomed sometimes visitor Dick Head (NH3) and then our JM dipped into his vast repertoire of jokes to send
us all into fits of laughter, before reminiscing the past, something about him witnessing the last public hanging. 
The hares had little to say, unusual considering that there were four of them, a bit of too many cooks perhaps
when it transpired that the intention had been to have the "three dots and you are on" convention on trail, which
had been abandoned because not all were aware of this. Another run without canine attendance meant that our
rush out into the lush green scenery was unusually quiet in between blasts of Kinky''s horn. 
To start we went North on the Eastern side of the
wood South of Warren Barn and then South on the Western side of the same, during which a fish hook caught Dickhead,
Olive Oyl, The Ref, Old Faithful, Kinky and Bambi. 
When we came across the path going
North West towards Stane Street we took it, all the way to the top of the hill and the award winning round wood shelter
where the hares whipped out a round of sloe gin shots. Here we rested our eyes with the broad view of Northwood,
sipped out shots, mmmmmm nice, while The ref and old faithful had a discussion about the latter taking a shortcut
on the way up the hill, and Popeye started to climb up the internal frame, "because it''s there". 
On our way again, towards Stane Street, the next check saw us turn South West and enter "The Northwood" which
according to The NT is "the largest woodland restoration project ever undertaken by the National Trust nationally",
and we can go hashing there ! On-On!. Just after exiting Northwood via one of the tall wire gates in the high
fence that surrounds the project, the hares decided to take us "off piste". We are now deep in the trees on the
Eastern edge of Eartham Woods, and it''s gloomy, and there is much discussion about a false trail marking that
tests the boundary of what qualifies as a legal mark, how far apart can the dots be? is it ok to obscure one of the dots
behind a stone? make one tiny ? and the third large and obvious? ... the debate carried on as the hares were
forced to take us into deep leaf litter, low bramble, moss covered brush and tic infested damp ground elder in an
attempt to discover the meandering trail. It was a relief to find the Southerly path that leads to the foot of Noire
Hill. No we did not have to ascend the very steep chalk and flint path up the hill although the hares had expended
energy in laying a falsie a good way up, that left Bambi and Old faithful breathless. The pack were taken South East
from here almost directly home apart from a small detour to visit a commemorative plaque to the existence of
Slindon Airfield, which for a brief time in WW1 was used as a mooring point for non rigid airships that patrolled
the South Coast for U boats. The On-In was reached after one hour and twenty three minutes of educative and
very sober hashing. The hares now rounded off the occasion by providing a buffet lunch in the small meadow
adjacent to the parking, which ever hungry hashers descended upon like starved wolves. In amongst the feeding
frenzy, our JM''s administered some down-downs, Our Visitor Dickhead, The Hares, Kinky for not knowing who has
the Hash-It, Bambi for overrunning more than one falsie, Popeye for climbing up things all over the place. 
In all a great hash that will go down in the annals.

 On! - On! Bambi 

 Runners'' track ... Spiderman''s …

Full run report: https://www.chihhh.org.uk/run1035.php', NULL, NULL, NULL, N'United Kingdom', NULL, NULL, N'Slindon Northwood', NULL, 50.880603, -0.637436, geography::Point(50.880603, -0.637436, 4326), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 1035, N'Spiderman, Dr Blood, Spiderwoman & Pink Flamingo', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, GETDATE(), N'Import chihhh.org.uk', @countryId, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2024-06-09T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1036 - Lovedean - Bird in the Hand', N'Fifteen or so of us met in the vast chalky car park of The Bird In Hand, worth two in a bush if you prefer,
slap bang on Monarch''s Way on the edge of SouthDowns National Park. Sunny but rather cool weather
for June made for comfortable exertion and has done nothing to hold back the burst of bright green
foliage in all directions. 
Unfortunately, after making extensive notes on paper during the Hash, Bambi managed to loose the
pad later, which means that this account is derived from the few remnants of memory that remain. 
Kinky, our JM, once again had us all rolling around on the ground with laughter when he told us one
of his hand picked jokes and then told us about things that he remembered happening when he was a lad in
1870 when he went to Charles Dickens'' funeral. This was followed by Bika and Itchy (Hares) explaining
the hybrid hash markings which were to also serve for the Portsmouth Hash on the following day. 
Having run out of things to say, a pregnant pause was followed by a toot from Kinky''s shiny horn and
we flowed out into the surroundings. The obvious and attractive directions using Monarch''s Way and
the path to Catherington proved false, leaving us to take care of the traffic as we headed South on
Lovedean Lane. 
The, frankly rather boring slog, came to an end at the new brick building of the Bethesda Mission church
where our prayers for something soft underfoot began to be realised as we headed West into the fields
North of Honeywood. Bambi got well behind the pack here, looking for a non existent trail on the path to
Eastland Gate accompanied by two inquisitive blinkered cart horses. 
Meanwhile the pack, with Dancer way out front, was sailing through the green waves of waist high wheat
across Broadway Lane all the way to a regroup at Little Denmead Farm, Oh! yes a fish hook somewhere
here caught a few, Dancer, Old Faithful, Dr Blood, and more. 
We did a "say cheese" in the farmyard before exiting West again, to The Crossways where we noticed
that the road sign let us know we were a balls throw from The Bat and Ball, the birthplace of Cricket. 
The trails here, left and right, were false which meant the straight on West (again) was taken with
Dancer (again) front running, past a marked short cut, through more waving wheat and down the
runaway slope to Pithill farm, avoiding the slippy cow patties. 
Our flight ever away from the chariots stopped here as we turned North East along Harrowgate Lane,
and encountered some of the foulest slurry shiggy you would ever want to see, hedge to hedge.
After a pause at the edge, the pack formulated a plan, a cunning plan. A bit like good king Wensleydale
in the Christmas carol, we would go single file and tread in someone''s foot steps, and since Dancer has such
big plates of meat (probably from pounding the beat) we all followed him down the middle. This worked
surprisingly well. 
Thereafter a few more sticky bits were negotiated with ease until the pack made a sharp turn East and
up the steep narrow climb through many a nettle to emerge from the undergrowth on Denmead Hill Lane
and join Monarch''s way.
Now we were up high again, the run in, South East to the Chariots was delightful, gradually down via
Mill Copse to reach the On-In after just over one and a half hours of bright and breezy hashing. 
We had down downs as per usual, with our brand of out of tune crooning the age old anthems.
The Hares, bless em, Bambi, Bika again, Splasher, Cheesy Chips others, can''t remember why, and
the Hash-It went to Bika again, which is so many times that he thinks he owns it.

 On! - On!  Bambi 

 Track 
... AND ... photos by Bambi

(Imported from https://www.chihhh.org.uk/run1036.php)', NULL, NULL, NULL, N'United Kingdom', NULL, NULL, N'Lovedean - Bird in the Hand', NULL, 50.914998, -1.028855, geography::Point(50.914998, -1.028855, 4326), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 1036, N'Bika and Itchy', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, GETDATE(), N'Import chihhh.org.uk', @countryId, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2024-06-23T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1037 - Madehurst Houghton Forest', N'The day, almost the longest, opened with an azure sky over a heavy dew that sparkled in the long green green grass.
Seventeen or so eager Hashers assembled at the bottom of the small valley below Whiteways Lodge on the Southern edge
of Houghton Forest. 
Our JM once again treated us to a sample of his rib busting humour and reminded us of some events lost in the mists of time. 
He then welcomed back Bugle Bum who normally hashes in Spain with Dos Marinas H3 (Founded in 2020, just had 60th run) and
Tumble Tot who is now no longer a tot. 
No one listened to what the Hares had to say, and the circle disorganised itself into small chatting groups until our JM gave us
a blast on his horn and reminded us that there was a Hash to run, so we set off, as you do. 
To start with we headed South into the green gloom of the copse below Fairmile Bottom, but that was limited by the roar of the
A29 above, so soon we were heading North West into impossibly green vegetation of the main forest, a real mixture of trees and bushes
above lush grass, fern and bramble, all growing on a thin layer of soil atop hard flint and chalk. 
Some well meaning (no doubt) person had decided to save humanity from death from strange white powder by scattering the fourth check
and false trail marker, which led to the FRBs, Dr Blood, Dancer, Wain and Popeye going a long way towards The Denture before Bambi was
able to haul them back. Annoyingly a very very vocal cockerel at Lower Farm, Madehurst was calling "cock a doodle on on!" over
and over, which all led to more confusion. 
Meanwhile the rest had found undisturbed flour on a trail South East and thought that they were miles ahead until they encountered
a fish hook that caught Kinky, Olive Oyl, The Ref and Hiawatha, so all back together, and happy Hares. The check at the extremity
of this leg had short false trails because it was situated just on the bluff above the chariots, although not obviously due to the forest
density. 
Accordingly we now headed North, away from the chariots and via a challenging back check took a jink to the West before reaching
The Monarch''s Way. Cast your mind back to the previous Hash, from The Bird In Hand, Lovedean many miles away. Yes this is
the same Monarch''s Way, so we could have run here from there, maybe you should try it sometime. 
We just continued North across the Way and followed the contour of the valley that runs down from Westburton Hill, along the
way false trails led both Olive Oyl and Wain to the valley floor from which they had to return with much swearing in the case
of the latter. 
Most were now walking by the time we reached Wapelgate Corner, Hiawatha and Popeye seemed more preoccupied with
removing tics, not from each other you understand, although from a distance one could be forgiven for thinking that some
mutual grooming was going on, at least if looking through Bambi''s old prescription specs. 
It was all down hill from here, a charge South through Langham Wood with a falsie that lured only Olive Oyl and a small
obscure trail as we recrossed Monarch''s Way that took everyone by surprise. 
We continued South beside the overgrown almost dry brook until we reached the chariots and the On-In after about one
and a half hours of deep wood hashing. 
At the circle, we had down downs, preceded by what might be described as singing, we are not talking King''s College
here, more football fan. The Hares (Splasher, Old Faithful and Bambi), welcome back (Tumble Tot, Bugle Bum), Hiawatha
and Olive Oyl, can''t remember why and Kinky because there was a spare mug of beer. 
We then vamoosed down the road to The Old Stables where we quaffed into the early afternoon in the shade of an
umbrella, with talk of politics interrupted occasionally by the ear splitting howl of an overpowered motor bike piloted
by middle aged crisis.

 On! - On! Bambi 

 Runners'' track 
... AND ... photos by Bambi

(Imported from https://www.chihhh.org.uk/run1037.php)', NULL, NULL, NULL, N'United Kingdom', NULL, NULL, N'Madehurst Houghton Forest', NULL, 50.885838, -0.583304, geography::Point(50.885838, -0.583304, 4326), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 1037, N'Bambi & Old Faithful', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, GETDATE(), N'Import chihhh.org.uk', @countryId, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2024-07-07T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1038 - Forestside and Stansted Village Hall', N'The omens were not good, it''s England, it''s July, Wimbledon is on, and as night follows day
it is going to rain, and so it did, as Trump might say "it rained bigley". Thank goodness then
that our JMs had hired Forestside Village Hall for our midsummer Hash, a haven of dryness
in a moist moist forest. 
By the appointed hour around twenty five of us mustered to listen to Kinky tell us a selection
of jokes from the top drawer of his witty repartee cabinet, and some history snips like the
anniversary of the release of the famous Beatles track All You Need is Beer. 
The hares, Dancer, Splasher and Bambi told everyone how dry and dusty the trail would be
and other valuable information for the adventure ahead.
The pack then rushed North up the road outside the hall leaving a cohort of kind souls to
prepare hot food for their return. 
A long falsie West towards Warren Down drew half the pack, while the rest found the entrance
to Firtree Piece and headed East. Heavy rain not yet absorbed into previously very dry clay
produced an extremely slick and slippery surface, and the trail marks obviously had metamorphosed
from white flour into a yellow paste, yet still visible, just. 
Notwithstanding the environmental challenges the pack made good progress through a series
of testing checks turning South through Batty''s Park to reach a fish hook that caught Wayne,
Dr Blood, Old Faithful, The Ref and Olive Oyl. 
Basically we then continued down Rosamond''s
Hill before turning West at Forest Hanger with a long Falsie into the aptly named Hare Warren
taking Bika out as front runner. Soon we regrouped to cross Forestside Road and enter the
Western side of the forest, where the plethora of paths is a trail setter''s dream world. 
We will not bore each other with a turn by turn description from here, just say that the trail made
a broad clockwise loop North back to the Village Hall. For a brief spell the sun appeared and
transformed the look of the pack as the water proofs came off to reveal steaming damp tee
shirts. 
Then the rain started again with a new freshness as we shambled up the road to the on-in
after one hour and thirteen minutes of sodden hashing. Then we noticed that Wayne was missing. 
Dancer and Bambi re-girded their loins and set out to find him, which thankfully did not take long
as we spotted his grinning face bouncing up and down as he sprinted towards us from the final check.
He told us about his adventures after a sabotaged false trail mark left him way off the trail and
disoriented (no change there then) relying on the kindness of strangers to put him on the straight
and narrow. 
Meanwhile in The Village Hall, warming chilli con carne was being dished out by the ladle full and
snapped up by hungry hashers, and Tigger was selling raffle tickets galore ..oh! and yes beer was
being drunk. Pink Flamingo said "I was at it all day yesterday" as she sliced up some wicked cakes
fresh from her Kitchen, Dr Blood said that they were all gluten free and one was even Vegan. He didn''t
say whether or not he had been at it with her. 
Then the down downs, a small technical hitch, in as much as the cans of beer had one of those widgets
that produce shaving foam when a can is opened, the short time between pour and consumption
meant that participants gamely attempted to down a beaker half full of foam, oh well, leaving
a white Groucho Marx top lip. 
First came the Hares as usual , then the food providers, then four seldom seen (Tigger, Treefeller, Canman
and Soapbox) then Bika, Dr Blood and Slightly Disappointing (I can''t remember why? ... doesn''t matter
they all like a glass or two), finally the Hash-It was presented to Wayne the missing. 
The last item on the agenda of course was the draw for the luxurious array of raffle prizes, where everyone
wants the numbers first out of the bucket and then dreads the final few numbers. 
A little bit of clearing up and we were all on …

