(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2024-07-21T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1039 - Emsworth Horndean Rd Rec', N'With no Popeye or Bambi on this warm, muggy day, it was up to the JMs to organise a scribe for the run. 

Unfortunately, with only three possible choices, and two of those staring at me, I was volunteered. 

So, after rummaging through the car, I found a pen that worked and a scruffy piece of paper to take notes.
 After a few jokes and a mention about 55 years to the day from the walking on the moon and not forgetting the Ref’s birthday, we were sent off in the general direction of the On-Out.

Hiawatha leading the way across the rec to our first check. With her over running a sabotaged falsie we found the trail straight on through the meadow, looping across the Horndean Rd. and by way of Fairfield Rd. we arrived at Brighton Rd. 

Heading north into Westbourne and then back south along the River Ems we arrived at a fishhook laid sideways on by Wayne. 

No numbers were given at the start, so we treated it as a regroup for all four of us. 

After a long wait for Hiawatha, who complained about a long falsie, a photo was taken and we carried on over the M27 to Mill Lane. With a detour into the wild area, we came to the corner of Lumley Rd. An ideal place for another photo with the pond and ducks.
 As we were directed over the A259 Main Rd., we all were sure that the trail was going to take us into the centre of Emsworth. 

Eventually, after all other routes were checked and double checked, we found the concealed path along the south side of the Main Rd. that led under the road and along the boardwalk, through the corner of the wild area into the Meadow carpark.

The hare’s local knowledge had us running around. Looking back we saw our hare casually leaning on a lamppost smirking at an entrance to an ally that led past the library to Emsworth Church.
With Dave leading the way we headed down Bridge Rd. and with a few twists and turns found the On-In under the railway bridge after 1hour 40 mins. 
 Treefeller and Pru were still patiently waiting for us but our other J.M. had decided to go home.

With so few of us it was decided to forego down-downs as there wasn’t anyone to lead us in the singing. 
 Many thanks to our hare, Kinky and virgin hare Wayne for finding new paths in an area that we have visited so often.
 O.F.
 Runners'' track 
... AND ... photos by Old Faithful

(Imported from https://www.chihhh.org.uk/run1039.php)', NULL, NULL, NULL, N'United Kingdom', NULL, NULL, N'Emsworth Horndean Rd Rec', NULL, 50.854938, -0.940257, geography::Point(50.854938, -0.940257, 4326), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 1039, N'Kinky and Wayne', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, GETDATE(), N'Import chihhh.org.uk', @countryId, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2024-08-04T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1040 - Creech Wood Denmead', N'When we arrived at Creech Woods on a pleasantly warm morning it was being used as a fantasy location
for a group of Dungeons and Dragons enthusiasts. One of the characters, not sure who or what she was supposed
to be, let Splasher and Bambi play with her big plastic spear thing, such fun. 
Back to the hash, and real life, where we found Popeye asleep in the cavernous boot of his big black
chariot, exhausted from laying the trail presumably. 
A modest turnout of eager hashers gradually assembled to listen to another few side splitting jokes
from Kinky''s vast repertoire of humour. He also mentioned this day in history, something about declaring
war on Germany after capturing Calais from the French, what a belligerent nation we are. 
A new face, Ollie, his second Hash, the first having been in Taiwan, lets hope that his last is not with us. 
After waking up Popeye and hauling him out of his nest, he and Old Faithful were able to tell us very little
about the trail, there would be fish hooks, a regroup etc. etc. yawn yawn ... lets get on with it!. 
We were given no help as to where to start, so after we fanned out checking, a trail was found South East
into the lush green undergrowth beneath tall spindly pines and silver birch. This area was used for mustering nearly
four thousand soldiers prior to D day (camps A11 and A12 actually) so amongst the undergrowth we
stumble upon the foundations of the many small buildings that are scattered here and there. This interrupts
some of our progress as we examine the remnants and try to deduce the purpose of a building, this one
looks like a kitchen or perhaps a wash room and that one a latrine? etc. 
Back to the Hash, we continued South East, towards Furzeley Corner and could see one of the Greens
of the golf club ahead on our left through gaps in the tree trunks. This diverting prospect might have
been the reason why Ollie had a major trip followed by inevitable contact with the dark rich and thankfully
soft moist soil. He picked himself up more or less on the rebound and continued at a pace, no doubt hoping
that no one had noticed. 
Meanwhile the pack had turned South West at the corner and reached a fish hook that caught Kinky, Bambi,
and Wayne, before continuing what was now emerging as a clockwise tour of the woods. 
There are plenty of nicely surfaced gravel paths in these woods, however for sadistic reasons the hares had
laid the trail through several areas of wind damaged terrain, where splintered stumps threatened to spear
a careless foot, and clambering over birch trunks provided unwanted ham string stretches. Nevertheless, plenty
of flour on the ground kept us going and frequent verbal complaints about a shit trail relieved the hardships. 
As we reached the South West corner another fish hook caught Kinky, Bambi and Olive Oyl, before turning North
for more shit trail experience with slow progress, then by contrast a blast on the nice gravel trail North West to
a regroup almost at Bunkers Hill Road opposite Creech House drive. Here we found a log pile to lean against
while Old Faithful fumbled with a camera until he managed to take a snap. 
Now there was a choice. "Do you want another twenty minutes of this ?" said Old Faithful "or just head in now"
he added. It is to our credit that to a person, except for Splasher who is a lazy git, we all opted for more hashing. 
Thus we soon found more trail on a rectangular loop to the South even closer to Creech House before returning
to the log pile photo location. A lovely scamper on a nice soft pine needle bouncy trail with small changes
of direction that keep one going in order to reveal what lies just ahead around the bend. 
That''s it, the chariots were a short distance further North West which were reached after about one and a quarter
hours of laid back hashing. 
We circled up around one of the picnic tables (no BBQs or Fires) and drank beer, including down …

Full run report: https://www.chihhh.org.uk/run1040.php', NULL, NULL, NULL, N'United Kingdom', NULL, NULL, N'Creech Wood Denmead', NULL, 50.897563, -1.079, geography::Point(50.897563, -1.079, 4326), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 1040, N'Old Faithful and Popeye', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, GETDATE(), N'Import chihhh.org.uk', @countryId, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2024-08-18T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1041 - Eartham Woods', N'Eartham woods, a favourite stamping ground, so many tracks, so many possible combinations, for those that have come many times
a Hash here is more like a game of chess with the Hares. 
On this lovely sunny fresh morning things got off to a good start when the Hares
arrived carrying the three missing HHH signs that The Ref had made, which were given up as "lost" two years ago. They are high
quality oak framed examples which had been mistaken for picture frames and ended up in storage in Blind Ness''s garage apparently.
All''s well that ends well. 
Fifteen of us and enough dogs to have our own Crufts, make that seventeen as the Spider people turned up late and missed our JM''s
witty repartee which was something about Genghis Kahn catching Monkey Pox on this day in history because he wanted to give
women the right to vote, something like that, but hard to concentrate with all the howling that was going on. 
It turned out that we had four Hares, Sausage Lottery, Slightly Disappointing, Dr Pussy and Camping Gaz, the latter two referred
to themselves as "shadow hares". 
A convenient elbow pointed us to the East and out of the car park via all the horse boxes and horse crap covered path all eager to
get the show on the road. One thing that became apparent very soon was that the trail had been laid the day before, and in the
interveaning time slugs had had a feast, the no doubt ample dollops of flour had become a feint white perimeter surrounding
glistening slime, so the Shadow Hares had managed to lay a Shadow trail. 
Out in front at the start, Smokey Bacon and Ollie were lucky to choose the right way to get across Stane Street and over to
The Plain, where a small SNAFU with the flour led to Dr Blood going a long way the wrong way as the result of the Hares
getting lost while laying the trail, tut tut. 
With that sorted, we headed North East, and very shortly reached a fish hook that caught Smokey Bacon, Old Faithful,
Ollie, Bika, and Bambi, before hitting one of the trails that head North to the old seat under the old sign post that points
many ways. A majority of the pack thought that we would go that way, however Old Faithful and Bambi who are well
know for pondering on the exterior of the cubic enclosure chose to head North West back towards Stane Street. Unfortunately
it looked like they had made the wrong choice, no sign of a trail, until they noticed the pack charging towards them
led by the Hares. Yes, a slug affected trail by and by reached Stane Street. 
We had a bit of a natter here while the athletes checked it out. It was pleasant in the warm sunshine having just emerged
from a gloomy, humid and slightly chilly deep wood a pleasure made possible by the Romans. Apart from this what did
the Romans ever do for us?. 
Can''t remember who found the trail North and then East into North Wood and back into the gloom, along the way another
fish hook caught Dr Blood, Bambi, Smokey Bacon, Ollie, and Dr Pussy (I thought Hares are exempt). Now the old calf muscles
began to complain on account of the unfavourable gradient as we clambered up the slope towards Stubb''s wood with the
bionic Smokey Bacon showing no sign of tiring, I''ll have a bottle of whatever tablets he is taking please. The Hares had laid
a regroup half way up, and we made the most of it. 
Now all that remained was a charge South, down, down, led by Smokey, The Ref and Camping Gaz. After the odd falsie to right
or left we were soon at the On-In after one hour and fifteen minutes of breathlessly chasing a shadow trail. 
We gathered around one of the picnic tables in the dappled sunlight of the glade adjacent to the car park where the Hares
produced an assortment of tasty morsels and beverages for our delectation. Be in no doubt that we applied our attention
to the consumption of these victuals with a fervour born of famine and dehydration. 
Eventually as the hunger and thirst abated, our JM started …

