(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2024-09-01T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1042 - Greatham Village Hall', N'This Hash was a gathering of four local clans, just over fifty hashers including nine from CH3. The others from Haslemere, Hursley,
North Hants, and a handful of visitors some from as far away as Dorset. 
Four Hares, Bika, Zhepzy, Mud Runner, and A.N. Other ( + Turnup Tony, Alpha & Beta Blocker & Nutcracker? - SM) laid a trail in a
mixture of flour, wood shavings, and chalk the night before, no rain overnight, so it remained fresh although undecipherable,
being a melange of local customs. 
The day turned out to be as warm and humid as a weight lifter''s armpit, as we gathered to
try and make sense of the ground graffiti that we were expected to follow, some taking snaps for future reference as if it
would help. As we set out from the village hall it seemed sensible to head away from the pub just to the North East on account
of the tendency for hashers to see beer and settle in for a session. This took us South West to the church, the yin and yang
of English villages either the pub or the church, the profane or the sacred. 
A bit of dithering here, so soon, no one
seeming to find anything useful, hunching of shoulders, loads of "how are you?", then with a rush we were off North West
up Church Lane and behind Cam Green Cottage, at a full on pace that was to prove the order of the day. 
Past Great Wood, past Lawrence''s Copse a bumpy meandering shady path with moist hollows and low level brambles
just waiting to trip the unwary. All spread out now a long line and not a check in sight, then a much needed regroup
as the path emerges from the shade to reveal an intersecting footpath. The regroup was marked in red shavings, a colour
reserved for the walker''s trail, which some found either confusing, or annoying, or immaterial, or uninteresting. What is
the difference between ignorance and indifference?, I don''t know and I don''t care. 
After a nice breather, we continued our progress North to a check at Snap House. Here Ollie overran a falsie and took
some of the pack into Brockbridge Farm, those three dot falsies can be confusing especially if it''s only your fourth Hash.
Meanwhile the rest were making their way West and starting to feel the pull on the calves as the first small climb up
the tarmac to Holly Cottage ensued. 
Now the heavy breathing started to take hold, the front of the pack sounding like a choir of obscene phone calls as
we forsook the road to head South and steeper up on the edge of Lower Hanger to a regroup just inside the wood
near the top of the climb. Little did we know that we would be returning here later, not all though, some chose to
miss the trail beer and head for the pub while they could still walk. 
Thus a smaller pack continued South on the contour down stinging nettle alley, huge bunches some over hanging
at head height, no leggings, no feeling left in legs, everything and I mean everything below the waist tingling
and throbbing with the effects of histamine, acetylcholine, and serotonin, strangely invigorating. 
The masochism trip ended as we emerged at the rose garden monument to Group Captain Lord Leonard Cheshire
at Le Court. The original Cheshire Home was demolished years ago, the pack milled about here for a while not
smelling any roses, probably because of the many emanations of bodily odours on the breeze. 
Now another steep climb North West on the road beneath Le Court hanger, the FRBs being rewarded by a fish hook
a third of the way up, then a double whammy, another fish hook two thirds towards the top, one of us,
see later, managed to get caught by both fish hooks. 
A tired rabble, nothing new there then, found a mark which indicated that there was beer in the vicinity, this
perked us up no end as we laboured with new found energy North East over the dome of a stubble clad field
to the road at Yard Farm where we found the ale already measured out in paper cups, yipee! 
As you might imagine there was no rushing off …

Full run report: https://www.chihhh.org.uk/run1042.php', NULL, NULL, NULL, N'United Kingdom', NULL, NULL, N'Greatham Village Hall', NULL, 51.070249, -0.894685, geography::Point(51.070249, -0.894685, 4326), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 1042, N'Joint run with North Hants, Hursley, Haslemere and Chichester Hares: Bika, Zhepzy Mud Runner etc', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, GETDATE(), N'Import chihhh.org.uk', @countryId, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2024-09-15T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1043 - Selhurst Park West', N'Here we are at the nether end of another summer and enjoying what will probably be the last spell of warm weather before
slipping on our Long Johns. It would have been nice to have seen a bigger turnout for this run, a big contrast from our
four clubs together hash a fortnight ago. Nevertheless, the arrival of a new face, a second time Hasher Alexia was a welcome
addition to the pack. 
The early arrivals enjoyed the magnificent view from Selhurstpark over the Eastern Solent with Halnaker Windmill in the
near distance, until by the appointed hour a dozen hashers had amassed and Kinky called us together. 
His vast larder of humorous quips was once again raided for our benefit followed by the usual "this day in history"
spot. To summarise I think it was all something about the Battle of Britain for the Sun Newspaper. 
Just a few impatient woofs from the canine contingent and we were off, and soon found the trail North into the
gloom of high high beech on the flat crown of Selhurstpark Hill, little light, little undergrowth, a dark world beneath
the green canopy with soft leaf loam underfoot and a small maze of paths forged by the paws of dogs and the feet
of their owners. Several close checks in this area took us West and suddenly out into the pupil shocking glare of
a wide firebreak. 
The pack was proceeding apace, unusually little natter, and more than usual fleet feet, one was wondering how
long this could last. Back North and into the shadows and all the way to the path West that follows the contour
high above Droke Hanger, the sound of Kinky''s horn far ahead and Dr Blood catching up behind. Then the Fish
Hook that broke the spell of Olympic pace. It caught Olive Oyl, Alexia and Kinky and we were all together again. 
Now the pace slackened, more natter at the checks as we continued West on the contour before plunging down
to the main North/South bridleway that bisects the forest, using firebreaks with long dew wet grass underfoot
and hazel borders. Sadly no sign of any nuts here, except for the running nuts that is. 
In this valley beneath Astead Down Bika followed the flour South up the steep bridleway and several followed
until the sound of cursing ahead made them look up to see Bika''s annoyed body language as he returned.
Meanwhile, unnoticed, Olive Oyl had tried the unpopular downward trail and was also returning. This of course
left the western woodland that appeared to have been made inaccessible by the felling of a wall of
small brush. It took a while to find the obscure way through the barrier, while some had a natter. 
Scouring the open woodland beyond revealed a trail up West to a disused bike trail and then on to
Potcomb. All this time Kinky''s lips vibrated many times as his horn bellowed, the sound echoing between
the trunks and seeming to come from all directions. Ain''t some dogs peculiar, the one that The Ref had
on a lead, Ebony I think, was distinctly unsettled each time the horn sounded. On one occasion Kinky
was just a few feet away when he gave us a blast and the dog nearly jumped out of it''s skin. 
We were now heading East and inevitably up, but on the bright side heading for the chariots. Those
that know this area realised where we were as we passed under the power lines that come down from
the local road. Olive Oyl thought that she had the way in sussed so she charged off East parallel
to the road, which would have been right had not the hares taken the trail North again back into the
gloom for one last blast alongside a line of stumps to emerge at the On-IN at the edge of the
car park after just an hour of late summer hashing. 
The hares had brought tables, beer, and snacking grub, which was quickly deployed for
immediate consumption. After the crescendo pop and hiss as ring pulls were pulled and
crunchy crisp mastication took place, Kinky managed to drag us into a circle to mull
over the morning''s happenings. He had a …

Full run report: https://www.chihhh.org.uk/run1043.php', NULL, NULL, NULL, N'United Kingdom', NULL, NULL, N'Selhurst Park West', NULL, 50.898915, -0.682523, geography::Point(50.898915, -0.682523, 4326), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 1043, N'Old Faithful and Bambi', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, GETDATE(), N'Import chihhh.org.uk', @countryId, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2024-09-29T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1044 - Stoughton Village - Triangle', N'When is this global warming going to arrive ? with record breaking rainfall and puffs of wind from North Pole
we were lucky to get a dry day for this run. There is little sign of autumn in the woods thus far, it''s as if the
trees have been caught with their trousers down by the early onset of autumnal weather. All of us were doing
that self embracing jiggly dance and going "brrrrr" as we gathered in Stoughton village. There''s no such thing
as bad weather, just the wrong clothes, so they say, and we were just getting to the nitty gritty of this debate
when Kinky called us all over the chain link fence on to the green bit for the all important pre run briefing. 
Once again he had us rolling over and splitting our sides with a few gems from his vast store of witty quips,
before drawing our attention to things from the past on this very date, something about Hitler having an
agreement with a dating agency in Munich in 1938 I think, I was not really concentrating. One new person was
present, Steve, On-On-Cue''s male progenitor, he was suitably clothed, smiling and not shivering. The number
six was assigned to any fish hooks we might encounter, and just as the hares were wrapping up Bika arrived
better late than never, bringing the pack to a nice number of fourteen runners eager to get moving and get warm. 
Firstly and too quickly we found flour on the footpath North West beneath the church, no go, back to the green,
try again North up to the church loads of flour, then wandering around the churchyard, during which Old Faithful,
(being a Monumental Stone Mason) pointed out some examples of his very own work. Then we spotted a hare
hovering by one of the exit gates and made our way to him over a path made up of very old headstones, each stride
slowly eroding whatever inscriptions still remain, the past being disappeared. 
The field above the church held a way back to the footpath leading West into the very Southern end of Inholmes
Wood, a climb up a narrow white ribbon chalk path that had us puffing and panting in some order. A couple of
checks at the top kept us together nicely as we continued more less North all the way to Pitlands Farm, diving
out of the damp woods and down over a recently harrowed field that felt like running over the top of a Victoria
sponge cake with fresh cream and strawberry jam filling and a hot cup of strong coffee and ... , oh! my mind is
wandering, sorry. 
A few of us chose the way out of the bottom of this small valley long before the pack cottoned on and headed
North East up hill parallel to the private 600 metre airstrip reg no GB-0653 if you are interested in landing your
Cessna 172 when you want to return from France under the radar as it were. Although you would need a good
pilot to keep on this narrow grass strip after touchdown. 
The leading group then encountered a fish hook and a long way back to the back, thus On-On-Cue, Steve, Alexia,
Smokey Bacon, Bambi and The Ref sighed a collective sigh and headed back, and back, all the way to Bika
who for some reason had chosen to run up hill on the sponge cake rather than the nice firm path. 
Next, near the head of this valley, we reached the finger post that sprouts out of a cairn like mound of flints, with
a North/South choice, and most of the pack heading South because the alternative trail was, at that very moment,
being harrowed out of existence by the farmer''s massive implement. Down and South across the top of the airstrip and
back into Inholmes wood with another hard climb back to the top of the ridge lay ahead, followed by a North East
slog along the backbone of the woods, on a wide shale track passing many a beech log pile gently seasoning
before being fed to the many many wood burners now being installed by homeowners since the rise in heating
bills.... Oh! off on a tangent again, sorry. 
Time was ticking, a thirst was rising, people were using the B word, and still …

