(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2024-10-27T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1045 - West Marden Nore Down Way', N'Arriving at the designated venue at 10.45 I was surprised that not even The Ref was there on
this clear, seasonally pleasant day. Where was everyone? No hares in sight as I parked next
to the HHH board. Oops! The embarrassment, it then dawned on me. The last Sunday in
October, everyone else was having an extra hour lay in. I disappeared off before anyone could
see me and arrived back as if nothing had happened an hour later. 
A reasonable pack of 15 eventually mustered under the blue sky that had more checks than
any hash. We must have been standing under the main flight path for all the airports in the
south of England. 
At the circle Rob introduced us to his well-travelled daughter, Tali, an experienced hasher,
while Alexia had brought along Tony, a virgin hasher. Our J.M., with a better joke than normal
spoilt it by trying to tell us about the Sex Pistol battling it out in Newberry in 1644. That’s if I
can read my scribble. The hares warned us about yellow flour, roads, sweet chestnuts, hills
and a couple of fishhooks, before sending us up the road towards Forestside. 
A back check didn’t have Sausage Lottery fooled as she went diagonally up the field to the
left into the woods and coming out to the field which we have used a few times. Here history
was made as for the first time ever Sausage Lottery found a fishhook and was joined by Bika,
Old Faithful and Alexia. Although it was clearly marked with a 4, Tony and Dr Blood decided to
run back as well. It is accepted throughout the hashes that HHH cannot count much above 4. 
In the past hashes we went across the Oldhouse Road which tempted several hashers, while
a few went left down the road and over the road towards Fanny’s Hill. Just like buses come in
twos, if you find your first Fishhook you are destined to find your second. This time only 3,
Sausage Lottery, Tali and Old Faithful. With a break in the hedgerow a regroup was called
and a photo shoot opportunity was taken overlooking West Marden. 
Continuing over Locksash Lane, with a couple of checks, we headed in an anticlockwise
direction lead by Tony into Grevitts Copse. With Alexia and Tony leading most of the way
up and down the hills it was a relief to finally see them both coming back from opposite
directions after finding falsies at a cross check. Straight on, with more hills, to Locksash Farm
where a regroup brought the pack eventually together. Rob was now finding it very hard
going due to the hills playing his sciatica up. 
A photo shoot opportunity was taken as it gave a little longer to recover and then off again.
Finally, a downhill stretch brought us to the On-inn to the Queen Vic Pub in an hour and 34
minutes of tough terrain feeling harder going due to no exercise for 4 weeks. 
At the Pub our J.M., Kinky, thanked the hares for an excellent hash, that was met by the
usual enthusiasm from the rest of the pack, before awarding a down-down. This was then
followed by our virgin hare, Tony, newcomer Tali, Sausage Lottery for running her first two
fishhooks, Rob and the hares for not explaining our hash signs to Tali before the run and
finally after giving himself a down-down for turning 80 he awarded the non-existent Hash-it
and down-down to Old Faithful for admitting his early arrival. 
Thank you, Popeye and Olive Oyl for a good hash.
 On-On! Old Faithful 

 Runners''s track ... AND ... 
 Walker''s track 
... AND ... photos by Old Faithful

(Imported from https://www.chihhh.org.uk/run1045.php)', NULL, NULL, NULL, N'United Kingdom', NULL, NULL, N'West Marden Nore Down Way', NULL, 50.915967, -0.903678, geography::Point(50.915967, -0.903678, 4326), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 1045, N'Popeye & Olive Oyl', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, GETDATE(), N'Import chihhh.org.uk', @countryId, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2024-11-10T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1046 - Tangmere Village Hall', N'We can always find something to say about the weather, however we have been sitting under a uniform sky now for a long time, it''s as if
neutral gear has been selected, cool, dry, grey and going nowhere day after day. This run was on one of these days, when it''s oh
so easy to drift into that Seasonal Affective Disorder thing, so girding up one''s loins and getting out for a Hash is just the ticket as a cure. 
The hares, Hiawatha and Smokey Bacon had deployed as many as four signs to guide us to the overcrowded car park at Tangmere Village
Centre just in time for us to gather for the two minutes silence, this being Remembrance Sunday. Nearby two battling soccer teams froze
as a referee blew the whistle to mark the start of the silence, so what with a touchline of spectators, us, and the players there was quite
a crowd of contemplation going on, birdsong suddenly became more noticeable. 
We had a visitor, Comes In Handy, the Religious Advisor from Munich Hash and a virgin Terry who lives locally and wanted to sample what
goes on, being a curious open minded sort of person. They were treated to yet another walk down memory lane from our JM Kinky,
something about Livingstone being found by Donald Campbell in 1958 while water skiing in The Lake District ?, I was not paying attention
because the orange team were about to score a goal. 
A few lies from the hares and we were off North through the houses with the thrum of traffic on the A27 getting steadily louder until
there it was, at the ESSO petrol station, the roar drowning any calls of On! On!, if there had been any, but not the reverberation of
Kinky''s magnificent horn. Some hoped that we would now head South through Garland Square and away from the particulates but
No!, we had the fun of attacking the ramp climb to the top of the steel bridge and continuing North into Boxgrove, our stride causing
quite a resonance in the bridge span which started bouncing around, quite exciting. This climb, was the only deviation from the
horizontal for the whole of the run. Virgin Terry seemed to be having no trouble understanding what was going on, which in my
experience means that he might be too intelligent for Hashing, he was confidently out front on many occasions and hitting a fare
share of false trails. 
We could have gone straight up The Street to the village hall, but a falsie in that direction heralded a clockwise loop West on
Crouch Cross Lane into agriculture and then to approach the hall through the playing field where a small fun fare was being
erected for someone''s party, a little person I assume. When Bambi was young he was lucky to get a cake with marzipan on
his birthday, if there were enough tickets in his ration book, these days well!, what is the world coming to?. 
Exiting the hall, most of the pack realised that heading South would be a waste of time because you could see Crouch Cross
Lane in that direction.....except The Ref that is, who headed off that way despite the Hare''s calling him back. 
Meanwhile the rest of us found our way East into the Tinwood estate heading for the vineyards and not finding much flour, so
much so that if it wasn''t for the hares leading the way we would have given up. Just as the first stand of vines appeared we
headed South for a long slog past The Priory, over there on our right, with the ground getting more and more boggy, presumably
too wet for grape vines to do well. What a spot for a fish hook, forcing Kinky, The Ref, Old Faithful and Bambi to gather even
more clagg on their boots. 
Now that auto-mobile hum that had been slowly increasing in volume became a howl and flashes of colour could be
seen through gaps in the hedge, yes, we were nearly back at the A27, but not before stopping for a "say cheese" moment,
before teetering on the curb stones which said "LOOK RIGHT" and looking for a gap in the traffic. Patience and good timing
saw us safely across both …

Full run report: https://www.chihhh.org.uk/run1046.php', NULL, NULL, NULL, N'United Kingdom', NULL, NULL, N'Tangmere Village Hall', NULL, 50.851447, -0.717728, geography::Point(50.851447, -0.717728, 4326), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 1046, N'Hiawatha & Smokey Bacon', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, GETDATE(), N'Import chihhh.org.uk', @countryId, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2024-11-24T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1047 - Eartham, The George Inn', N'In global terms, just a short distance yon in central England and Wales, storm "Bern" was wreaking havoc,
trees blown over, floods, and landslides. Meanwhile in Eartham a strong mild almost warm wind was the
only hint of the mayhem not far away. Many of the "usuals" were in warmer climes which resulted in a rather
compact pack which gradually assembled outside The George attempting to keep their hats on or their
hair in place through the random gusts. 
Kinky had succumed to a seasonal bug and sweet talked Bambi into standing in as JM, thus very unfortunately
the Hashers were not able to hear any of Kinky''s vast repertoire of side splitting humour or rumination
about what happened today but years ago, most of which Kinky remembers. 
We had a virgin, Karen, to welcome before the three, yes three, hares tried to remember anything about
the trail, it seems to Bambi that these days the hares generally are being brutally frank about the trail. We
hear "there are fish hooks", "There is a regroup", "it was dry but now its
slippery", "beware roots", "there
will be steep bits",..... does this honesty spoil anticipation of the surprise just around the bend? one
wonders. 
At least no pointers to direction at the start, so we spread out to look for any flour that had not
blown away. The path South West by the church was clean and the nearby road was false, thus the
pack reformed to head East on Britten''s Lane and find the footpath towards Nore Woods. 
A sticky climb past the disused pump house at the bottom proved a leg sapping experience during
which Alexia''s top blew off, yes she was topless for a while. Don''t get the wrong idea, because at
the crest was a fish hook that took Bambi, Dr Blood, Popeye and Bika to the back, during which
manoeuvre the top of Alexia''s flour bottle was retrieved. 
When we reached Puck lane the choice is/was up-North/down-South, the false trail South was
suspiciously short, leaving the pack struggling a bit on the ever steepening trail up Nore Hill.
Here the autumn colours are on full display, the golden winter sunlight low in the sky throwing
the tree trunks into sharp relief and in the calm forest air the exertions of the climb soon had
everyone glowing. 
There is a fence alongside the path near the crown, it has one easy, built in gap to reach the other
side, where cleverly a check had been laid, Bika, Popeye and Dr Blood took the bait and decided
on the Eastern side, only to find the trail false and themselves cut off from the pack by wires. The
pack continued the climb on the main path, leaving them to either go back down to the check or
become super limbo dancers to get under the fence. 
We reached the crown of the hill, only down from here whichever way. Some of us have been here
before and were greatly relieved to find a short false trail down the North face of Nore Hill, which
is a precipitous and suicidal descent on wet slimy chalk this time of the year. This left the South
loop round to The Folly as the best bet, and Bambi was so keen to proceed in this direction with
alacrity that he failed to notice a trailing shoelace which once trodden on produced a headlong
descent into the scenery. Oh well, nothing broken, just embarrassment, silver lining
was a fish hook just ahead that caught Bika, Dr Blood and The Ref. 
Then suddenly on the South side, out of the trees and back into the howling gale coming up
from The Solent. Heads down into the wind we made it to The Folly where a "say cheese"
moment is obligatory. Then a charge down the jagged flint track, South, heading back,
hooray! and at the gateway no one choosing the path East towards Slindon College which
is resplendent on the ridge opposite. Westward ho for us, although a few were suckered into
taking the path that goes back up Nore Hill, not locals from these ere parts obviously. 
The rest were soon enjoying that big dipper path that slices the contours first down
into …

Full run report: https://www.chihhh.org.uk/run1047.php', NULL, NULL, NULL, N'United Kingdom', NULL, NULL, N'Eartham, The George Inn', NULL, 50.876926, -0.666945, geography::Point(50.876926, -0.666945, 4326), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 1047, N'Alexia & Olive Oyl', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, GETDATE(), N'Import chihhh.org.uk', @countryId, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2024-12-08T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1048 - Havant Thicket', N'Winter storm Darragh had just passed it''s zenith, natures way of pruning weak branches and whole trees
even taking the life of a few people in passing. Sane people would not go outside in such weather, but
we are dealing with Hashers here who were at the end of the line when the common sense was being
dispensed. 
As you might imagine, not a whopping turnout, gathered in Havant Thicket car park which one day will be
on the shores of a reservoir that will provide many recreational facilities including fishing platforms, so
we will be able to come here have our run, catch lunch and barbecue it. 
Coming back to earth, we had a short, "who has got the worst shoes?" competition which revealed
Smokey Bacon''s were brand spanking new, and Olive Oyl''s to be show winners that are in an advanced
state of decomposition. Then Kinky rocked up, so we formed a small circle. 
There were two splendid jokes, from Kinky''s vast store of side splitting humour, one about Captain Hook
visiting a "Second Hand Shop", the other, not from a Christmas Cracker was too complex to recount here
but was in the genre of "Ex Wives Jokes", always good for a laugh, are we allowed to say that?.
Moving on to this day in history, it transpires that in 1980 John Lennon opened the Clifton Suspension
Bridge, at least that is what it looks like I wrote down on my soggy note pad. 
At last a pair of seasoned Hares who returned to the traditional series of lies about the trail, it was going
to be hilly, bone dry and dusty, very short etc. and then they pointed us in the wrong direction to start.
Thus the pack jogged North to find a false trail, and then South on the true trail and smiling Hares looking
on. 
A bit like homing pigeons after release, we followed the flour in a broad clockwise circle, West, North
through Furzy Plain then East again back to within yards of the first false trail mark that was found.
Then a long meander North parallel to Manor Lodge Road, running on slick un-rutted mossy grass
topped with soft brown leaf litter, no wind here in the trees, luvverly. 
I''d like to claim that I know my way around the thicket, however very quickly, with a short distance between
checks I was soon finding many false trails where I was sure we would go, so full marks from me for this
route. Only now, looking at the GPS track I see that we made our way, via many twists and turns, gently
up Horsefoot Hill, and then back into the thicket using a craftily re-laid check. Yes as usual it was very
muddy in places, pools of water and fallen branches in profusion. 
After a while it became apparent that either Kinky is clairvoyant or he was taking advice from the Hares,
because several times he appeared at the front of the pack without passing any of us, like using a
time and space portal. 
Now we proceeded South to the shore line following one of the small brooks that will feed the reservoir
all the way to the point where it will eventually meld into lapping waves. Here gazing over the barrier
and probably dreaming of the future, Smokey Bacon leaned backwards into the spiky gorse bushes
and jumped out with many an expletive. 
West along the shore line followed and we hit a fish hook that caught Olive Oyl, Dr Blood, Smoky Bacon
and Bambi. We could have continued on this path to the chariots, except we were treated by a loop to the
North first. During which a small difference of opinion arose between Bika and a few of the pack.
Nothing serious, something about interpretation of the non existent rules, I''m not sure what, I could
hear The Ref exclaiming "you can''t do that" as I headed for home, which we reached after one hour
fifteen minutes of full on hashing. 
At the circle, down downs for the Hares, well done chaps!, Smokey Bacon, new shoes, Bambi whatever
it was he is innocent, Kinky, short cutting, and the Hash Shit for Smokey Bacon, bad language while
in a bush. 
We then rushed over to The Staunton Arms …

