(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2024-12-22T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1049 - Bosham Fairfield Rd', N'Oliver, Alex, Bev, Clem, Megan, Lara, Max, Maria,Toby and Susy were surprise visitors for this Christmas run,
some the fruit of Dr Blood''s loins,
and Vixen and Prancer not seen for a while, not to mention Dad Along Legs, so a quite welcome and
unexpected Hash swelling. A grey cool day with a blustery wind slowly working up steam from the West. 
Our JM had something to tell us about the abdominal snowman being on a sleigh and the Pilgrim Fathers
reaching the USA at 4.20pm, with no mention of what happened to the mothers, probably hanging around
waiting somewhere else as usual. As hare, he made a token attempt to tell us about the trail before we
disassociated our chat chat groups and drifted out to find flour. 
South first, past The Burial Ground, wait for it,...yes the dead centre of Bosham, sorry just had to, and
on down The Drive and Harbour Road to guess where? yes The Harbour of course!. 
Here we saw the big boys out in front, long legged strides eating up the damp salty tarmac of Shore Road
although several falsies here above and below the sea wall caused enough confusion to keep the
pack together. 
A clever little loop using an obscure path just south of the harbour brought us back to the shore to make
the northerly crossing that''s only possible at low tide. A bit of a Marmite path this, some skipping over with
almost dry shoes while others, notably Dad Along Legs managing to slip into the festering rotting seaweed
green muck left by the last tide. 
Now West into the teeth of a now howling gale and the constant percussion of rigging slapping the masts
of the boats at The Quay, where the hares had planned to have a drink stop by the seat on The Meadow.
"Pardon", "What?", mouths were moving but voices drowning in the cacophony, so we retreated to the
relative calm by the wall of Holy Trinity, where we were treated to many a sip of what appeared to be cream
sherry, but you never know with Thumper in charge. 
At this point, quite a few must have thought we had reached the On-Inn because, when we set off North
along the stones of the foreshore, the pack had shrivelled, not surprising because by now the wind blowing
across my face was making my cheeks flap like riding a motorbike without a helmet, and each stride held
the threat of a twisted ankle on a slimy rocking rock. 
A brief respite on the aptly named Windward Road and a fish hook that caught Bambi, Old Faithful, Smokey
Bacon, Ollie and The Ref, made the second foray on to the stones more cruel, until
after an eternity we turned for home, South on Brook Avenue and then East on Bosham Lane to Our Lady of
the Assumption and the ON-IN after just under an hour of hat clutching hashing. 
We quickly moved over to the nearby Berkeley Arms to compete with the Sunday Roasters and find a warm
welcome, on a bitterly cold wind chill day. Down downs were accepted by the hares (thanks), Bambi as best
dressed Santa lookalike Ho! Ho!, Dad Along Legs, muddy feet. Others I no longer recall, look at the snaps.
The Ref got the Hash Shit, no I still can''t remember.
 On-On! Bambi 

 Bambi''s track 
... AND ... photos by Bambi

(Imported from https://www.chihhh.org.uk/run1049.php)', NULL, NULL, NULL, N'United Kingdom', NULL, NULL, N'Bosham Fairfield Rd', NULL, 50.831675, -0.851083, geography::Point(50.831675, -0.851083, 4326), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 1049, N'Kinky & Thumper!', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, GETDATE(), N'Import chihhh.org.uk', @countryId, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2025-01-05T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1050 - Chichester, Castle Stross, The Avenue', N'In the gloom of a midwinter howling gale, with the rain sheeting down sideways
and armed with sixteen kilos of bone dry self raising, the intrepid hares set out
to conquer the universe. Well not exactly, however, a monumental effort lay ahead
that was to test both to a limit that is not normally reached when laying a trail. 
Two hours later the deed was done, the worst of the gale had passed, and the hares
were changing out of soggy clothes for the first time, annoyingly for him, the rain
had blown down Old Faithful''s lug holes and drowned the batteries in the aids which
were making a squeaking noise like R2D2, what? Pardon?. 
As you might imagine, despite this being the "Hangover Hash" with the attraction of
a raffle prizes and grub, the foul weather deterred all but the most adamant hashers
who slowly gathered outside Stross Schloss. 
The few circled up as usual on the strokes of eleven and held a silence to mark the
recent passing of Tigger, one of the founders of our Hash. On the 2011 Hangover
Hash (Run 702) Tigger had a heart attack on trail. This did nothing to dampen her
enjoyment of hashing, as soon as she recovered she was running (slowly) with us
once again. We will all miss the smile, and gentle, determined, karma. 
Our JM wheeled out the old "It''s not unusual" Tom Jones joke and dusted it down for us,
followed by something about king Edward confessing that in 1766 he bought the
crown jewels at Christie'' auction house, at least that was what I think he said, over
the squeaking from Old Faithful''s boom box head gear. 
The hares assured the pack that the going was bone dry and to take care not to inhale
too much dust or get dehydrated while on trail, before they set out tentatively in all
directions. Firstly we headed North via Chestnut Avenue and over to Lavant Road
passing the petrol station with The Ref and Smoky Bacon warming up out front. 
Both must have had their heads down into the wind and rain because they ran right
past a check at the new footpath West to Centurion Way, doggedly continuing toward
Lavant on the narrow pavement with the occasional drench of spray from passing
chariots. 
By the time that they realized they were all alone and returned, the rest of the pack were
already wandering around the boggy grass rings of the Roman amphitheatre remains
where the wide open aspect allowed the wind to exert it''s full force and carry away any
calls of on "On! On!" even if there were any, which there were not anyway, because of
all the gritted teeth. 
Finally after a little help from a hare, a soggy trail was found South on Centurion way for
a long slog all the way slightly down hill and in the relative shelter of the old railway cutting.
A fish hook had been laid just before the bridge at Brandy Hole Lane, however there was
no trace of it or most of the trail leading to it, the determined work of a saboteur, out to spoil
our fun or suffering from the poison delusion or the "burglar signs" tall tale, who knows what
motivation?, "there''s nowt so queer as folk" they say up norf. 
Obviously a big check here, with many possibilities, kept everyone occupied for a while, before
Dr Blood, who was wearing a thick fleece that was getting steadily heavier with each shower,
found the Southerly trail, in the field just above CW. This was the most unpleasant section,
with slip slide thin mud on uneven ground below and a suddenly invigorated rainstorm driving
blinding stinging rain into one''s face. 
This time the fish hook well into this torture was still
intact, so Smokey Bacon, Dr Blood, Olive Oyle and Hiawatha had to go tail to the wind for a
brief respite before a lee ho and about turn into the teeth once again. 
Salvation lay down a short precipitous drop over rock and slippery roots and back on to CW.
With great fore though the hares had purloined a length of rope, with which the pack were able
to abseil down, back into the shelter of the …

