(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2025-01-19T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1051 - Stoughton Forest', N'The temperature hovered above zero in the shadows below the dark yews on the Eastern slopes of
Bow Hill on this gloomy grey nose dribbly morning. We had early visitors, Yorkie and Yellow peril
(North Hants H3) who waited patiently for the congregation who appeared to be leaving it to the last
moment to turn up, except The Ref of course who is always a good half hour early for his appointments. 
Our new JMs egged us into a knee knocking tooth chattering sssscircle for a very brief welcome to
our visitors and hand over to the hares, Olive Oyle and Popeye who, thankfully were equally terse. 
There was news over the airwaves that Alexia and Tony were lost in the hinterland, duped by a misleading
postcode for a nearby house on an isolated access road, what did sat navs ever do for us eh?. With no ETA
for them and considering the temperature and after much debate (yea right) with deep regret we
decided to start without them. 
Hashes in these woods are a bit like getting out a well used jigsaw puzzle that fits together
differently each time we try to put it together, as Eric Morecambe said we know how to play all
the notes but not necessarily in the correct order. 
Apart from the yews all the trees are now leafless and the ground under is covered in a thick
carpet of as yet still crispy crunchy brown leaves that can hide annoying little twigs and noose
like brambles. 
To start, the pack spread out to the North East and luckily soon picked up a true trail, these
days we almost always adopt a "three and your ON" policy, which then took us North into Wildham''s
Wood with On-On-Cue being chased by Yellow Peril. No one was interested in looking West towards
Wildham Barn and when Bambi found the path to Ramsden Copse to be false the aforementioned pair
took the only other way South East on a field boundary back into Stoughton Forest. Now the
puzzle was beginning to fit together, a definite clockwise sense so far. 
To continue turning left and West from here would have taken us back to the On-Out trail which
could have been re-laid yes, however, thinking this to be unlikely we headed North East again
towards The Plantation and soon found a true trail bending South, the bottom of the long
climb to Blackbush House. 
Yes that is what came next, up and up, then more up, then up again, then more up until at
about three quarters of the way, the inevitable fish hook, those caught should see it
coming, possibly do, however are too caught up in showing off their hill climbing
abilities perhaps ?. Anyway the hook caught On-On-Cue, Yorkie and Bambi. 
Then, at the top, at Blackbush, who should we see shuffling leaves having come up the
even steeper climb from Greatdean Bottom but Alexia and Tony looking as fresh as daisies.
Obviously they had unlost themselves and been pointed "up there" and like Duracell
bunnies were still good to go from the top. 
We had a regroup anyway as the rest of us were a little limp, had a natter, took a snap
etc. before Comes With Daddy found the way out on the ridge path towards Bow Hill. The
normally inspiring view from here out across the Solent was lost in the gloom and
the IOW looked a million miles away almost the same colour as the sea and the sky. 
We couldn''t keep going in this direction for long, a deal of time had passed, so the
turn North over the ridge was a sure bet, what was a surprise was a big loop off piste
and well marked through the tall tall beech with the pack in a line snaking like
a spread out conga at the Christmas party, let the rhythm move your feet!. 
And so we arrived at the Tumulus criss crossroads, five ways to go, all but one
likely and needing a check. This took us a while, not helped by someone mistaking
a cross for a false trail mark, no more said, until at last we headed North over
the crown of Lambourne Hill on that path that really does snake around over all sorts
of trip hazard exposed roots that would give the …

Full run report: https://www.chihhh.org.uk/run1051.php', NULL, NULL, NULL, N'United Kingdom', NULL, NULL, N'Stoughton Forest', NULL, 50.907204, -0.84192, geography::Point(50.907204, -0.84192, 4326), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 1051, N'Olive Oyl and Popeye', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, GETDATE(), N'Import chihhh.org.uk', @countryId, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2025-02-02T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1052 - Burpham Car Park - George and Dragon', N'If you delve into the Hash anals then you will find that the last time we hashed from the
then "George and Dragon" (now just "The George") at Burpham was Run 689 in 2010,
hared by Malibog and Red Horse. So here we were 14 years and 7 months later and it
seemed just like yesterday, that’s what senility does for you, can''t remember what I did
yesterday, but every stride of run 698 is as clear as a bell. 
The big difference was the temperature, then a warm July morning, now cold and damp
and grey and February. Then, a meagre turnout, now a decent sized pack of honed
athletes jigging around trying to keep warm while we waited for ever for all to assemble
beside the village hall. Years ago the perils of the trail ahead would have been a mystery,
remember the infamous "Bridge Over The River Kwai" run near here (Run 640)? of course
not, but I do. These days the jungle drums of WhatsApp posts had already prepared us for
a great deal of shiggy somewhere ahead. 
We circled up, no newbies, no visitors, sparsely seen Bugle Bum was in attendance, foregoing
the warmer Spanish climate. Our Jms mercifully had little to impart, as did the Hares, although
I was more interested in the view from here where Arundel Castle is silhouetted just above the Arran
valley to the South West. 
There was plenty of flour on the footpaths of the recreation ground perimeter, all of it false, so
after a brief circling around we headed North towards the church and then continued in that
direction along Peppering Farm Road to the cross roads, then West down Peppering Lane to
the banks of a lazy loop of The Arran. 
Here, hemmed in by the river and the rail track it''s either North or South, and it was North,
and Smokey Bacon and Olive Oyl were getting quite far ahead of themselves. The very green
lush grass all around held a soggy secret, it was floating on top of ankle deep icy water. You
know those YouTube''s of "fire walkers", rushing over hot coals going "oo" "aah" etc, well we
were sort of "ice water walkers" rushing along going "ooo" "aaaaagh" as our feet got
progressively colder until that tingly feeling arrives where all sense disappears. 
The relative height of the lane just North of Peppering Farm gave us respite, a regroup,
and meeting with "The Walkers", and Oh! yes a handout of jelly babies by the Hares, do
you eat the head first?. 
When we restarted we took the left hand path towards Camp Hill which follows a strangely
dry stream bed over many, many a root and twisty turn with potters clay, slimy and slippy
underfoot. Two, yes two fish hooks along here were employed to keep us together, in all
catching, The Ref, Old Faithful, Comes With Dad, Smokey Bacon, and Bambi. At the foot
of the hill, sheltered from the breeze, the sun came out and bathed us in relative warmness
so we milled around for a while nattering while watching Hiawatha find the false trail to
the west. 
Turning East we now encountered the much heralded shiggy, OMG, what shiggy it was and
no mistake, essentially the wide path resembled a half drained canal. Pretty soon Olive Oyl''s
shoe was sucked off, as it was important to keep moving as lively as you like lest a moments
rest let the slurry pull you down to the centre of the Earth. Four hundred metres of this, AND
with total sadism and disregard for the European Convention on Human Rights the Hares
laid a fish hook that meant that Comes With Dad, Smokey Bacon and The Ref had a fair deal
of second helpings. 
Like in a TV advert, "But Wait!, there''s more!" as we now found ourselves at the foot of the
staircase up Camp Hill. Someone told me the number of steps, but I have forgotten, anyway
it''s far too many. The steps are fairly small, but a lunatic has spaced them out so as to prevent
anyone getting into a regular rhythm as one sprints up, Ha! that''s in my dreams, but not in
Smokey Bacon''s because he was up them like a rat in a drain pipe even with legs …

Full run report: https://www.chihhh.org.uk/run1052.php', NULL, NULL, NULL, N'United Kingdom', NULL, NULL, N'Burpham Car Park - George and Dragon', NULL, 50.870275, -0.524795, geography::Point(50.870275, -0.524795, 4326), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 1052, N'Alexia and Sausage Lottery', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, GETDATE(), N'Import chihhh.org.uk', @countryId, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2025-02-16T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1053 - Eartham Woods', N'Two degrees C when I set out – down to 1 degree by the time I arrived at the start, and there was hare Smoky Bacon in shorts and T shirt eager to get the hash going so he could warm up a bit. By the time Bika arrived – as usual with only seconds to spare (how does he do that?) – there were 9 hounds, 3 hares and 4 – erm – real hounds. And the hares informed us that the trail was 9 point something something kilometres. Crikey! That’s close to 6 point something something miles! “That includes ALL of the falsies”, explained the hares.
 So off we set, crossing Stane Street and following the zig-zags of the trail with the pack staying together well. Until the point where 6 of the pack and 2 hares found a falsie, but also some more confusing flour beyond it. “What can this mean”, the hounds asked the hares, “which way should we go?”. “Um, we don’t know” admitted the 2 hares (who shall be nameless). Fortunately, the third hare, The Ref , appeared at the far end of one of the side tracks, so that we 6 (whisper it) were able to short-cut and continue the trail.
On we went, the pack still keeping together, till we reached Stane Street again at the 7 way (8 way?) junction where we paused for a photo shoot.
 On again, and there seemed to be umpteen fishhooks, all for 3 hashers. I would list the names of the “hooked”, but with so many fishhooks, I think pretty well everyone was caught sooner or later. (Just an aside, but if you want a party puzzle, ask your guests if they can think of a word containing double “h”. We hashers all know the answer) 🎣
In the circle the JMs provided some liquid refreshment and some snacks for the grateful assembly. Then down-downs for the hares for a good trail, and also down-downs for the hares for getting lost occasionally on their own trail, and .... were there any more? I lost count!
 I started this report with a reference to Smoky Bacon , but I see in the inevitable après WhatsApp discussion an alternative name has been suggested – Streaky Bacon . Perhaps given his usual speed around the trail that might be an appropriate name. Votes for a re-christening on a postage stamp, please.
 On On, Kinky 

 photos by OLd Faithful

(Imported from https://www.chihhh.org.uk/run1053.php)', NULL, NULL, NULL, N'United Kingdom', NULL, NULL, N'Eartham Woods', NULL, 50.887992, -0.666469, geography::Point(50.887992, -0.666469, 4326), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 1053, N'The Ref, Hiawatha & Smoky Bacon', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, GETDATE(), N'Import chihhh.org.uk', @countryId, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2025-03-02T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1054 - Slindon Dukes Road', N'Since I was one of the hares I would say that this was arguably one of the best
Hashes this century so far. You will have noticed the escape word "arguably" which
like the phrase "up to" preceding the words "Sixty Percent Off" renders the sentence
more or less meaningless. 
For Tony and myself the day started at a crisp zero degrees
with the shiggy still hard and crackely underfoot, later as the pack gathered it was
eleven degrees warmer in the bright bright sunshine,"𝄞little darling it''s been years
since it''s been here" was earworming. 
A better than average turnout and a "haven''t seen you for a while" welcome to Blind Ness
and Splasher preceded some histrionics from our JM Slightly etc. Something about Arthur
Daley driving round Fontwell Park in a figure of eight looking for a hidden secluded
beach, at least that was what it sounded like, I was more interested in the warm sunshine
on my back, "𝄞little darling ...". 
Hiawatha brought the news that thatched roof of The Gribble Inn had burned out, ignited
by smuts from the chimney, say the fire brigade, although it''s been so wet recently
it''s ..... wait a minute this a run report ... mind wandering again, sorry. 
The pack set off West on Duke''s Road then had to come back because the trail started at the
car park footpath exit into the woods heading North parallel to Slindon Bottom road on a
narrow twisty turny rooty path that widened into lovely open leaf litter with brilliant
dapple light breaking through bare branches. 
At first the usual fit fit bods started to
draw away from the main pack, then a few unplanned events gradually slowed them. Rick the dog
needed plastic bag attention, Alexia slowed, then a rubbed out check slowed the remainder, and then
Old Faithful over ran a rubbed out falsie ... sometimes there
is a silver lining to the cloud of a sabotaged trail. 
Everyone was more or less together by the time we reached Top Road with the view up to the Folly
resplendent in the sunshine, "we was up there a couple or two of hashes ago" said The Ref, and
he was right for a change. 
A few wasted time on the falsies North and West while Alexia blasted East on Top Road, then ran out
of trail on the road up to the college, while Old Faithful found the turn South into the woods and then
up to the college via the very shiggyfied trail to join the walkers for us to all have a group snaparoony
at the Victorian brick walled bower and seat. Once upon a time this would have provided a grand view
South towards Middleton on Sea, however today it commands a view across the lane to the opposite
overgrown hedge. 
We continued to the college entrance to check out falsies towards Nore Woods and up Butt Lane and
then into the village and a switch to chalk markings on the tarmac at the top of Church Hill. Turning
down the hill we next visited the village pond, where two male mallards in their flashy plumage were
arguing over the rather dowdy female. We spent quite a while at the pond because the pack failed
to notice the footpath sign that is hidden in plain sight, just through the iron kissing gate. 
Once found the path headed South over open grassland to Park Lane. Dogs on a lead here, there
were sheep in the bottom field, which obviously noticed us because they organised themselves
into a tight protective group to one side as we came through. 
Continuing South, a blast down the tarmac to the small overcrowded car park followed while
Smokey Bacon and Hiawatha and Olive Oyl had to recover from heading North on the false. 
There are lots of options at the car park, and there was flour on some, so a minute or two
passed before the path towards Slindon Common was chosen. A nice soft path this meandering to
avoid the many shiggy pools just inside the trees towards the very busy Fairmile Bottom Road.
A fish hook was needed here, and it caught, Smokey Bacon, The Ref, Olive Oyl, Comes with Dad and
Alexia. 
We regrouped to …

