(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2025-03-16T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1055 - Ambersham Common New Road', N'It''s hard to over stress how clear the air was for this run, bright blue, yes, and also a bit chilly in the breeze with
absolutely no haze, a camera user''s dream, and Bambi was ready to whip his out at a moment''s notice
and a flash not needed. 
A small and enthusiastic group assembled, evenly balanced between runners and walkers, ready to see the
sights and ventilate their lungs with crisp clean air. 
Our JM of the female persuasion was busy weeding at her allotment, presumably putting her back into this
energetic activity having first selected a suitable hoe from those available. It''s odd
because we all know that our JM of the male persuasion has more experience with hoes.
In due time he called us to order and tried to tell us some interesting factoids about Ambersham
Common, but failed to add anything to common knowledge that it is a bit of sandy weald,
boggy, acidic, unproductive, and great for hashing. 
The Hares broke the news that for the first time we would be using a circle to mark checks, at least that was the
intention, which had been slightly muddled due to their difficulty in tossing an old habit, which had lead to a few
"hybrid" checks, marked as a circle with a cross, resulting in a false re-group. Then due to their usual total panic
during the latter stages of laying the trail, the part where they had almost run out of flour and time, they had
reverted to not laying any of the possible false trails. All in all it looked like we were going to have to
concentrate on this at the same time as running, and we can''t even tap our heads and rub our
bellies at the same time, not our own at least. 
No visitors or virgins, so we sort of drifted off at the start slowly working up the pace like a
washing machine limbering up to start a fast spin cycle, across the road from the car park and South
East up the sandy climb under the power lines straight to a hybrid check. 
No probs, Olive Oyl soon had us jammin North just beneath the ridge with the sun casting stark tree trunk
shadows across the trail, running down a never ending zebra crossing. Bye and bye we reached New Road again
just South of the old rail track which proved a tempting target which took a few of us away from the
less appealing scrub to the East which is of course where we went. 
If you have trod these parts before you will know the only path that gets you over to High Wood on the
edge of the common, it goes on forever, meandering in and out and around of large gorse bushes which
on this day were sporting a profusion of bright Hash Shirt Yellow flowers. We stopped for a snap in
the middle of all this which rather took the sting out of the fish hook at the trail end that caught
The Ref, Smokey Bacon, Old Faithful and Bambi. 
Now it was West to the trig point at the heart of the common, the pack getting used to spotting the
circles and finding short falsies that kept us moving apace despite the long stretches of soft powdery
energy sapping sand. Many paths present themselves at the trig point, most would have had us back at
the chariots too soon or heading for Graffham, accordingly Bambi and Smokey Bacon guessed the path
South to the Hoyle Lane cross roads where they were greeted by the walkers. Only one slight problem
being that they over ran a regroup somewhere just above the old sand pit, what’s done is done etc. 
After a decent time nattering and remarking on how sunny and bright was the day we all headed West
on the tarmac towards Polecats, then North West into Heyshott Common and all the way to the
brook on The Serpent Trail where the easy way over the bridge was false, requiring us to go off
piste to leap the banks to continue. No mishaps here, we are all fit and healthy, we laugh in the
face of such small challenges. 
We left The Serpent just the other side of the brook and headed North towards the aforementioned
old rail track. This area has been undergoing a bit of a …

