(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2025-03-30T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1056 - Rowlands Castle - The Castle Pub', N'This size of this write up is going to match the size of the pack that turned up for
this run. The End...... just joking, though a run on a glorious sunny spring morning
was no match for spending quality time with female progenitors. 
We will not concern ourselves with exactly where we ran because there is a distinct
possibility that this run will be revisited in the near future, remember if you don''t eat
all your sprouts you''ll get them again for supper. 
The pub car park, The Castle, is festooned with those intimidating signs that threaten
a million pound fine if you don’t scan a QR code in a pub that is closed. So perhaps
hashers were elsewhere looking for chariot room. Or had forgotten about the clocks
jumping forward, then the long series of WhatsApp
pings resounded from our pockets bringing news of a shrinking pack. 
At the appointed hour nine folk semi circled up, comprising two hares, two and a half runners,
and four and a half walkers. The half person being Sausage Lottery who is hard to
pigeon hole as one or the other. 
Our JM seemed to repeat ChatGPT''s view of Rowland''s Castle, won''t bore you with this
you can do it yourself, I do it myself frequently. It turns out that a big mound is involved. 
The hares were pleasantly brief, back to cross checks again, old habits, and a mixture
of wood shavings and flour, and laid on the previous evening, no comment. 
It will come as no surprise to learn that much of the run was in Stansted park woods where
a record dry March had left the usually shiggy trails set like concrete and full of ankle
wrecking ruts and hollows. Also during the night, the snails had feasted on the flour leaving
distinct patches of slime glistening in the sunlight, thus in parts we followed a slime trail. 
There was no rest for the two and a half of us that were running our hearts out. The smiling
faces to been seen in the snaps taken at the regroups belie the deep fatigue that progressively
set in almost from the first short climb into the trees. 
What aided our progress was the superb weather, blue blue sky throwing the leafless branches
into stark contrast before the buds burst, a cooling breeze for hot brows, spring and summer on
the way. After a bit over the hour we reached the On-In after an exhausting sun bleached run. 
Now, The Castle was in full swing, lots of mummy yummy at the tables. It was no hardship to occupy
the bench tables out front, facing South, basking. 
We had our down downs as per, for the hares god bless em, then on trail we had heard Sausage
talking about enjoying a joint and how Slightly uses his probe to make a good job of it, Bambi had
been shamed into NOT short cutting, Spiderman was thirsty and then pretty much everyone else
had a glug glug Ah!. Considering the size of the choir we managed a decent rendition of a
suitable chorus on each occasion. 
 On-On! Bambi 

 photos by Bambi

(Imported from https://www.chihhh.org.uk/run1056.php)', NULL, NULL, NULL, N'United Kingdom', NULL, NULL, N'Rowlands Castle - The Castle Pub', NULL, 50.8909, -0.957201, geography::Point(50.8909, -0.957201, 4326), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 1056, N'Old Faithful and Bika', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, GETDATE(), N'Import chihhh.org.uk', @countryId, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2025-04-13T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1057 - Graffham Recreation Ground', N'There are lies, damn lies and statistics, or so the saying goes, however it would be the truth
to say that we had 500% more runners for this hash than for the previous. Now you are thinking
of a mass of Hashers in the Graffham Rec car park, when in reality we were a modestly sized
bunch of athletes, honed to the peak of fitness ready to devour the trail on a sunny day with
a bitter breeze from the East. 
The JMs being part of the Hare team launched straight into tales about the trail, trying to educate
us regarding the difference between Alpacas and Llamas and their spitting habit, euchhchthwap. 
Also something about the trail going through the Southdown Airsoft park, which sounds a nice
gentle thing to do but is definitely NOT, look it up, I did, note that they have defined a "safe word"
to use in case you feel uncomfortable with what is happening, the word is "CUCUMBER". 
There was a welcome for virgin Lorna and a hi to regular visitors Dr Pussy and Camping Gaz from
Guildford. 
Without any general direction indicated, we issued forth into the hinterland in all directions and
circled around until Bambi found something looking like slimy flour behind the pavilion. It turned
out that the trail had been laid on the previous evening (again) and of course the news had been
published in The Slug and Snail Gazette the readers of which had flocked to the area for the
feeding fest. 
Thus with a little help we found our way round a loop to the South via Calloways and Chestnut House
and the West on the path to Marsh Farm and then Woodcote Farm across lovely soft level meadow and Spring
has arrived. Birds in chorus, buds bursting into vivid green all around, a warmth in the suns rays,
feeling fine, and a fish hook that caught Dr Pussy, The Ref, Smokey Bacon and Camping Gaz.
We regrouped at Woodcote Lane and Sausage Lottery whipped out her eggs and passed them round
she is so generous. 
Several paths emanate from the farmyard, it''s a great place for a check, and it took a while of finding
nothing before we found the path North West into Jay''s Copse. Here the path is almost un-runnable.
deeply rutted by some big heavy wheels in clay soil set hard by the driest March on record. Duly
another regroup in the copse, more of Sausages eggs, and worryingly the Hares are consulting
their maps. 
So now North into Hoyle Copse, and Oh! the path has a warning notice, "Simulated combat games
are being played" and then two seemingly mutually exclusive instructions, "Please DO NOT enter
this area" followed by "Please keep to the public footpath"..... and a number... so..
good old Sausage whips out her phone and had a chat with a real person, yes a real person, and
let them know we were coming through, while we had a few more of her eggs. 
Smokey Bacon, Dr Pussy and Old Faithful were keen to test the trigger happy chappies ahead
because they rushed off ahead of a more hesitant pack, someone muttered something about
cannon fodder. We looped to the East, right through the middle of the battleground, keeping
to the path, in the calm of a temporary truce, observed by silent camouflaged combatants. If
we had known the safe word we would have been shouting "Cucumber!", "Cucumber!", "Cucumber!". 
Then a rush down the steep slope through Round Wood Copse, over the stream and a short regroup
on the other side, more of Sausage''s eggs, before the lazy climb back to Woodcote Lane, where
Thumper had set up spiritual stop. Here we had a chance to natter about the Airsoft experience
and knock back a few shots of god knows what? along with more of Sausage''s eggs, chocolate and
alcohol, mmmmmmm. 
Legs getting a bit stiff now, too much standing around, so a slow restart North on the lane before
an almost U turn South on the path back towards the chariots. We have used this path before in
the not too distant past. Then, it was through open fields and reached The White Horse pub. Now
the path is …

