(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2025-04-27T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1058 - Droke, direction East Dean off A285', N'A very very sunny and warm day, and April not quite out yet, no noticeable dew or breeze either.
The small car park at Droke was in no danger of over fill, why?, well so few people are turning up
for our runs these days. Thankfully we had three visitors from Hazlemere, Chastity Belt, Head Boy
and His Knees are buggered who almost doubled the number of runners. 
Bambi was a one man band for this run, he was stand in JM, he was the single hare, he took the snaps,
he deployed the signage, he provided down downs and crisps and finally is writing this scrawl. 
For the first time, at least for us, we tested a trail with no falsies, just blobs, circle checks and some
fish hooks, from a hare''s perspective it takes less effort than the traditional ways. 
We set off East under Droke Hanger with a check near the entrance North to Deep Coombe which
tempted Head Boy away from the pack which found flour continuing East. He soon caught up
because very shortly a back check was a long time problem. Ages were taken to find the climb
South East up Selhurstpark Hill, lots and lots of brown dry leaves atop soft musty soil, not that
we are soil sniffers, well not normally. 
Just below the summit, running West on the contour a fish hook caught Dr Blood, Old Faithful
and Smoky Bacon. Then anticlockwise loop around the top pf the hill, wandering around on the
flat in the shade of the high canopy of beech, circling the branch stacked teepees built by children
last summer. In the warm and here on the level, some of the pack were even running after
loosing all their energy during the climb. 
West next, parallel to Selhurstpark Road but using an old forest ride, just far enough away from
the tarmac to silence the sound of traffic except for the odd motorbike, the ones that sound like
a wounded wasp having what must be a straight through exhaust. When we crossed the main
bridleway back to Droke, Smokey Bacon ran way down the hill in that direction, big mistake,
coming back up one could hear him panting like a madman, he is a masochist and no mistake.
Having said that, he still made it back in time to be caught be another fish hook that also caught
the same pair as the previous. 
When we reached the famous pair of dog graves on the edge of Chiseldown Wood we turned North
and it was all down hill from here. We dropped into Potcomb, skirted Astead Down and reached
Bonny''s Hanger all in the shade, and at a cracking pace between the checks as you would expect
when putting one foot in front of another takes no effort. We reached the ON-IN after just over an
hour of very pleasant summer hashing in spring. 
At the circle, there were down downs for Chastity Belt (repetitive shortcuts), Smoky Bacon, Dr Blood
and Old Faithful (repetitive fish hooked), visitors, Splasher (He owns a share of a race horse but
cannot remember it''s name, sounds like "salmonella", and the hare of course. 
Some of us called in at The Anglesey Arms on the way home, enjoying some amber nectar in the
popular garden under the warm afternoon sun.
 On-On! Bambi 

 Bambi''s track ... AND ... 
 photos by Bambi

(Imported from https://www.chihhh.org.uk/run1058.php)', NULL, NULL, NULL, N'United Kingdom', NULL, NULL, N'Droke, direction East Dean off A285', NULL, 50.906426, -0.686411, geography::Point(50.906426, -0.686411, 4326), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 1058, N'Bambi', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, GETDATE(), N'Import chihhh.org.uk', @countryId, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2025-05-11T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1059 - Rowlands Castle - The Castle Pub', N'A feeling of déjà vu as I arrived at 8am on a bright, warm and promising to be hot, Sunday morning. 
 Today my assistant was virgin hare, Comes With Dad who was volunteered by her Dad and Becky at the last run here. Yet again only a small pack formed for the welcoming circle. Most of these being walkers. Luckily our running numbers were doubled by the return of Lorna, Ollie and his dad. It was nice to see Treefeller, whose mum has turned 100 four months ago and Can Man, the last of our founders. 
 With the J.M.’s still on honeymoon and no one else taking their place, Tali and I explained the markings we had used, including a couple of photo opportunities and a fishhook with checks as circles, except when Tali laid large squares. After laying nothing but crosses for 38 years this has become very hard for me to adopt! If it’s not broken then why try and fix it? 
 Olive Oyl and Ollie were the two front runners as we started along the road and into the forest. Even though Bika, who should have known where we were going as he laid the previous trail with me last time here, did not give much away. I was attempting to use the same trail again as the only runners last time were either on honeymoon, Becky, running elsewhere, Bambi, or haring this run, Tali. 
 The trail was mainly clockwise starting with a steep up hill climb. The walkers who thought that they might keep up with the “runners”, I use the term loosely, soon gave up and with Spiderman in charge did their own thing. This very quickly reduced our numbers to six. Stansted Forest is laid out on a grid pattern so there were many opportunities for checks. 
 With two photo opportunities taken sitting on tree trunk seats, we reluctantly thought about why we were here on this very hot, dry, sunny day and eventually waited until the two “O’s” found the trail before setting off. The Ref having to be crowbarred off his seat. After an hour Lorna decided to take a short cut and unknown to the rest of us Ollie’s dad decided to follow her. At this point the pack over ran a falsie and started complaining, in humour, about the lack of a check. Whilst Ollie went looking for his dad, Tali and I directed the remaining three onto the trail waiting for his return. After a phone call and chat with his dad the four remaining “runners” continued. The fishhook was reached. With a smile on their faces they looked down at the number 4 and circled each other then carried on. 
 After a few more checks a hidden route through the trees, over crisp dry leaves, brought them to the last check and finally the on-in over the fields back to the start in 1 hour 35 minutes. 
 Drinks were taken in the pub garden where we all chatted away not bothering with the hash-it, down downs or mentioning any misdemeanours. 
 Our heartiest congratulations to our J.M.’s for now being Mr. and Mrs. Hamilton-Day or whatever they decide on and to Comes with Dad on her engagement in April to John. 

 On-on O.F. 

 Walkers'' track 
... AND ... photos by Tali et al

(Imported from https://www.chihhh.org.uk/run1059.php)', NULL, NULL, NULL, N'United Kingdom', NULL, NULL, N'Rowlands Castle - The Castle Pub', NULL, 50.8909, -0.957201, geography::Point(50.8909, -0.957201, 4326), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 1059, N'Old Faithful & Comes With Dad', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, GETDATE(), N'Import chihhh.org.uk', @countryId, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2025-05-25T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1060 - Buriton Queen Elizabeth Country Park', N'Early showers had given way to a light bluster with intermittent blasts of ultraviolence
from a sun that is only four weeks away from it''s zenith. The car park was packed with
pennywise punters seeking to avoid the five plus quids needed to leave their chariots
on the other side of the hill in the QECP. 
Our JMs, still celebrating their nuptials in Asian climes, were represented by Kinky, who
in his unparalleled style told us a joke that sounded a bit sexist, which I have already
forgotten and then something about Charles the second sinking HMS Coventry using
a Star Wars weapon. I''m not sure about this, I was not paying attention, I was wondering
why Old Faithful had failed to yank his lever hard enough to prevent his van from creeping
down the lane and blocking the road. 
We set off West on the SDW after the hares let us know that it was going to be a nice
flat run (gazing up at the hills all around us), with fish hooks and mostly circles for
checks. 
This is the steepest way to leave the car park, on a hard flinty unyielding track, you can
try keeping closely to one side on the narrow strip of weedy soil, however you end up
with a gait that looks like you are trying to prove to The Fuzz that you are sober.
A fish hook three quarters of the way up that caught On On Cue and Bambi proved
annoyingly humorous to the rest of the pack. 
The climb flattens out and the SDW splits into a plethora of possibilities, and our lungs
are on fire, and more is asked, as the true trail heads North up War Down, and up and up,
and bugger another fish hook, this time add Dr Blood to three disgruntled back pedlars. 
Now we are on top, really, nowhere higher in the park, trail a bit sketchy, bit of sabotage,
but not a problem, and then we bump into On On Que''s dad, out with his pals on their
bikes, probably not a coincidence, he refused to tell us if he''d seen any flour ahead, quite
right too. 
No one wanted to even look at the false trails to the West on account of them disappearing
down a precipitous drop, and there were a few false trails to the East as we headed South
down the backbone of War Down. Running was now delightful, gently down, soft grass
available on each side of a wide trail, a few adventures on falsies into the dappled gloom
of tall tree cover what could possibly go wrong. 
The walkers route brought them close to us just as we had reached the tree covered slope
just above Hangers Way. There are lots of exposed roots here, and one of them leapt
up and tripped Spiderman who nose dived into the unyielding chalk below a tree. A split
lip, loosened teeth, and a boxer''s nose job were evidence of the violent impact. We waited
for Dr Blood to come and wrestle Spiderman to his feet, he is the expert after all. After getting
over the initial shock, Spiderman was all "Tis but a flesh wound" and appeared stable in a
standing position. It was obvious that for him the Hash was over, with Spiderwoman and Pink Flamingo
at each side he retreated for an early beer anaesthetic. 
The pack continued South through the busy children’s play area, chattering about the remembered
disasters of the past, top of the pops being a toss up between Hairbrush''s busted knee and
Tigger''s heart attack, both of which involved a blue light ride. 
Soon we reached and Shipwrights Way and through the car park wot we use when doing the Parkrun,
that''s the boring Hash, the one with no flour and no falsies and no beer, as Bika might say
"Why?" but then he is one the Parkrun directors so he knows why, ask him when you see him. 
Now we turned for home with a long slog up the gradual slope of Shipwright''s way, back in the open
on a hard arid track. Only a couple of checks, and I think I remember a fish hook that caught Old Faithful,
Comes With Dad, and On On Cue, before we arrived back at the very first check of the Hash half way
up War Down. 
I think we might have been stumped for a long time here, …

Full run report: https://www.chihhh.org.uk/run1060.php', NULL, NULL, NULL, N'United Kingdom', NULL, NULL, N'Buriton Queen Elizabeth Country Park', NULL, 50.972798, -0.956408, geography::Point(50.972798, -0.956408, 4326), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 1060, N'Alexia & Olive Oyl', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, GETDATE(), N'Import chihhh.org.uk', @countryId, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2025-06-15T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1061 - Whiteways Lodge Car Park', N'Sausage and Slightly, still displaying to glow of newly weds, had baggied a spot at the top end
of the very busy car park at Whiteways. Luvly warm wevver that had brought out clouds of tiny
tiny small black beetles intent on crash landing on our bright yellow shirts not before circling
our bonces once or twice just to annoy us. 
Before anything we had a moment or two of quiet reflection, at the sad news of the demise of
Bodyshop in tragic circumstances while on a Surry HHH run. 
Then the Hares, Blind Ness and Sausage Lottery, told us the usual lies, and then Kinky arrived late
and missed their warning about the presence of the dreaded ticks, one of which had just been
spotted creeping around on Sausage''s back. 
Soon we were off , North West down hill on Monarch''s way with a few falsies to the left and
right to keep things interesting. Kinky was finding the warm mouthpiece of his horn far more
appealing than it''s frigid winter condition, consequently he was able to provide us with
frequent blows along the way, attracting the pack into a tight unit as we reached the bottom
of the valley below Langham Wood. 
Here much aimless wandering about occurred, falsies everywhere, everyone avoiding the elephant
on the trail, namely the arduous climb up to Waplegate Corner, through some of the most tick
infested knee high grass on the planet. Yes, of course, that''s where we went, Bambi first,
ho hum. 
Up at the top, and a quick check of any skin where we had exposed ourselves, you know what
I mean, not what you just thought. The mercy was no fish hook that would have meant some
of us exposing ourselves for a second time. Time for a short natter while Olive Oyl
discovered the trail South West back down the contours. There, hiding in the shade of a
stout oak, were the walkers. No sooner had we engaged them with hashing pleasantries, who
did we see coming along from the East but Prancer and Dancer, better late than never. They
looked well wrapped up having just returned from somewhere really hot. 
More hill climbing next as we made a Northerly loop all the way up to The Denture and into
the steady flow of mountain bikers making the taxing journey to Bignor Hill. A long falsie
into Trot Row split the pack, leaving Old Faithful to take the rest back South on Monarch''s
way again. The re-joiners had some catching up to do, and making good time downhill until
Tony stubbed a tiny bit of proud flint which sent him cartwheeling into the side growth.
Pick yourself up, dust yourself down, grit your teeth and carry on was the order of the day
for him, surprisingly unscathed despite quite a spectacular tumble. 
It seemed for a while that we were going to go South down to the Madehurst road, but the hares
had found a twisty turney over the rooties duck or grousey little pig of a trail West then
towards the forest entrance off the Madehurst Road. Now down in the valley below the chariot
park the rest of the trail steeply up into the gloom back to Whiteways Lodge CP, by the way, where
is the lodge?, all one can see is the cafe. Anyway we reached the ON-IN after one hour of
quite challenging hashing. 
The Hares produced a feast for us from their tailgate, which we attacked with great relish, amber
nectar of course and also a shot of something stiffer in memory of Bodyshop whom some of us new. Sausage found a ring of ticks around an ankle OMG!, tiny tiny
examples, loads of advice, don''t squeeze them, do this do that OMG!, I think she
smeared them with Vasaline in the end. 
We formed the circle and there were Down Downs, The Hares of course, god blessum, Tony, falling
and wearing new shoes, AND torn shorts, Hash Shit and the shoe sup for him, Dancer and Prancer, late, Sausage and
Slightly, the newly weds..... actually the list goes on.... it was Father''s Day, so all the guys
who at least were aware they were fathers.. etc...etc. 
Then we continued the party until bit by bit hashers …

