(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2025-06-22T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1062 - Iping Common', N'This Hash, coming at the nether end of a short heatwave was a hot one which brought the benefit
of a nearly empty car park as most sensible people did not want to be out and about in the mid day
sun, "𝄞only mad dogs and Englishmen" as the well known song goes. 
Iping Common is at the heart of the Western Weald, a landscape quite different from our more
frequent downland experiences. Masses of open heather, sandy trails, prickly gorse, head high
bracken, gentle undulations and a lack of visual keys on the horizon to aid a sense of direction. 
The aptly named "Serpent''s Trail" runs through the heart of the common with it''s twists and turns
and the hint that one might spot the odd Adder or three on a sunny day such as this. 
Our JMs were doing something with a Hasher''s Ball on the IOW, so Bambi was in charge, well
at least as much in charge as anyone can be when faced with pack of eager Hashers. 
We were two minutes late circling up because Kinky arrived just not in time and needed to do
up his laces while a reasonable number started to sizzle under the sun grill. 
A welcome to Loiuse originally from South Africa, probably at home in the sun, then a
few humorous quips and no useful information from the Hares. Moments later and the pack spread
out looking for the on-out, and found it West on the Serpent''s Trail, straight across the Roman Road,
all the way to the Trig point, no longer used of course and now buried in a gorse bush, I can''t see anyone
deploying their theodolite here again. 
There are lots of ways to go from here so there was quite a gathering of non checkers while the
more energetic sorted it out. Old faithful whipped out some Jelly Babies, getting a bit sticky in
the heat, and needing a bit of finger licking after popping in the gob. 
Trail found, South, off we rushed and Rick the dog only just failed to send Alexia on a trip into
the gorse. While on the subject of tripping, she did show us snaps of the massive bruise on
Tony''s leg (see 1061 trash) and told us that he had a mildly broken right elbow, now strapped
up. 
At the bottom of Trotton Common on the edge of the pines we were joined by Rob, fruit of Old
Faithful''s loins, who had been wandering round Iping Village looking for and not finding us, good
thing that OF had been assiduously marking the checks with an arrow. 
Now, like day and night we had a brief excursion into the Sweet Chestnut trees of Goldring''s
Warren, where the paths are gloomy green tunnels, on this day, a welcome shelter from the
ultra violence from above. There was little time to cool off here because some idiot very quickly
found the trail out of the maze. 
Next, East, out into the harsh shadeless heather of Fitzhall Heath, not blooming yet, but when it
is blooming heather it''s like running through the lush pile of a thick psychedelic carpet, a trip
of a different sort. The meandering path also winds around various ancient Tumuli before
crossing the Roman road, where we turned left, North, and now heading for the chariots. 
Soon we reached the On-In after a very dehydrating one hour of hashing under a midsummer
sun. 
There was a postage stamp of shade in the car park where we circled up to consider all
things. This included a Down Down for the hares (OF and Bambi), Alexia (almost tripping),
Comes With Daddy but not today , Kinky, and Rob (had a hard time getting here),
𝄞Just like a virgin Louise, Splasher (nice to see you), Bika (I''m thirsty), Kinky again
plus Hashit (moans "I''ve done all the falsies"). 
After a little more rehydration and nibbling crisps some of us retired to resume at the nearby
Hamilton Arms where we sat in the shady part of the garden, sipped cool cool beverages and
caught a hint of Gaeng Ped Yang curry on the breeze from the Thai restaurant.
 On-On! Bambi 

 photos by Bambi

