(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2025-07-06T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1063 - Angmering Park - Dover Car Park', N'So far it''s been a hot dry summer, hose pipe ban on the horizon, "Phew! it''s a scorcher" headlines,
so no sign of shiggy, just dusty deep dust bowl craters at the entrance to the car park at The Dover. 
The Hares were there to greet us, not entirely tickety-boo, Tony still recovering from a Hash Tumble
 (see 1061) 
and Alexia sporting a grubby left haunch having found that walking backwards into a
horizontal tree trunk while sipping cider doesn''t go well, even if you don''t spill a drop.
 Quite a nice turnout gradually assembled, Alexia brought Linsey, a neighbour and Hash virgin,
Comes In Handy (Sometimes visitor, Munich HHH) had walked the 9k from Littlehampton, with
raincoat and brolly, because the Met Office was predicting rain, which turned out to be a hot
sunny day, what do they know?, might be better off examining a piece of dry sugar kelp. 
After again waiting for Kinky to join us our JM let us know what he had found of interest about
this area, which amounted to a tale of how the Germans had tried to bomb Tom Oliver a famous
local jockey during the war, trying and failing to stop him winning The Grand National, or at
least I think that''s what he said, I was distracted by the antics of the dogs that were milling
around, they appeared to be engaged in "water sports", say no more. 
So off we go, into the local maze of woodland paths that surround the car park, in all directions
at first, but then settling on East along the edge of Hammerpot Copse and a very early
fish hook that caught?, unfortunately I was still playing catch up to notice even though
another fish hook almost immediately caught Dr Blood, Dancer, Comes With Dad and Kinky. 
Now the pace picked up, continuing East along the edge of Kitpease Copse and at last into the
shade and massed log piles of Selden Fields, where we had a regroup and said "Cheese"
many times, and Alexia handed out Jelly Babies, and we discussed their nibbling niceties, do
you go for the legs first? or do you prefer to slowly suck off the head? 
North now, through Stonyland and Barnstake Copses to Buckmans, where we left Dr Blood
behind for some time while he made a detour to examine the Nazi bomb crater, a
big one, was intended for London, but the bomber crew had a dummkopf navigator, got lost, then
suffered a premature bomb bay evacuation, verdammt!. 
Continuing North into Michelgrove Park and another fish hook for CWD, Dancer,Linsey and Kinky
who is to be commended for his liberal use of the horn on this Hash, at his age it takes
stamina to run and blow at the same time. 
All this time we''ve been in predominantly coniferous surroundings but now we turn West into the
aptly named Oaken Copse for a change of flora and somehow denser and more humid and
less birdsong or at least more sound absorption, like running under a duvet. This is on The
Monarchs Way, here a tarmac lane, and we all felt that we had to be heading South sometime
soon. A check at the head of nearly every Southerly path that then proved false got more and
more annoying, CWD got quite vocal, she was doing more than her fair share... etc. 
Eventually we got to Upper Wepham before finding the true trail South to Wepham Ball and
then the ON-IN after taking about one and a half hours of fast action hashing to make a
complete circuit of Angmering Park. 
Wow!, what a spread lay before us. There on a chequered table cloth lay a feast provided by
the hares. After a slight polite hesitation the pack set to devouring the victuals and glugging
the grog, with small pauses for the odd recount of something from the morning''s shenanigans. 
We did find a moment to circle up, and shower the hares with our profound thankfulness, before
"Like a Virgin" for Linsey, more Down Downs for Comes in Handy (Visitor), Dancer (Hash Shit),
Dr Blood (Cratering), Spiderman (why not?), Bambi and CWD (whatever). 
"Get on your knees Tony we have a name for you!" quoth Sausage, he …

