(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2025-07-20T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1064 - Pagham Nature Reserve Visitor Centre', N'As our number 1 scribe was off on another of his club runs the notes this week will be a poor
example against his talents, for which I apologise. 
With threats of being struck by lightning and being washed away, we circled at the car park
after paying for parking, eager to hear what the J.M.’s had to say whilst all wanting to get off
before the rain. Learning that the freshwater entering the marshlands was only run off
from the fields, it is classed as a saltwater marsh, which at the moment was at low tide, so
no wading expected this time! 
With warnings by the hares of fish hooks and restricted dog areas ahead we set off along
the north side of the dog restricted area to the shoreline. Ahead was the Crab and Lobster,
an area we all know too well. Some running down the alleyway, others along the shore
but the trail went past the pub on the road. 
The Ref led the way to the right from a check that took us through the bushes reeking
of celeriac, to an open field that, surprisingly, contained the only shiggy on the trail.
Through a cow field, luckily they were all laying down, and back to the marshes. 
After trotting our way over another long grassed field we were due for a “Jelly Baby” stop.
The trail took us over a bridge, where the stream was full of duckweed, and along Halsey
Farm House and Barn. 
Over halfway and no sign of fish hooks. Was this another ploy by the hares to keep us
from running too far ahead? Just like buses as one fish hook comes then another follows!
Catching The Ref, Old Faithful, Olive Oyl, Popeye and Doctor Blood out, some twice. 
Heading back to the familiar area of the Crab & Lobster at the far end of the alleyway we
hit the third fish hook, catching The Ref, Old Faithful and Doctor Blood. 
Once running, I use the term lightly, we started coming across a number of falsies that
caused only slight confusion as we were all convinced of the way back including the front
running Ref who short-cutted, beating Comes with Dad to the ON-INN. 
It wasn’t until then that the very first of a couple of fine drops of rain were felt. Thinking
how lucky we had been, the heavens then opened up and within the last 50 yards
(I don’t believe in the European metric) we all got soaked. 
It was impossible after a 1hour 20 minute run to circle at the car park so we convened
at The Spotted Cow.
All very civil as we just enjoyed a chat and drink before dispersing. 
Thanks to the hares Kinky, yet again Alexia and Lindsey for a great hash.
 On-On! Old Faithful 

 photos by Old Faithful and Alexia

