(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2025-08-03T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1065 - Arundel Crossbush Lane', N'Why have we not hashed from Crossbush Lane before?, beats me, an omission, overlooked
since the dawn of Chichester Hash, now brought to us by Alexia, Linsey, and Caught Short
featuring Rick the dog. 
A bright dry morning saw a decent gathering with quite a few visitors and welcome backs.
Nicko (Brighton H3), Bugle Bum (Dos Marinas H3), Ollie and Max (son of and son of son of Dr Blood). 
Our JM started rabbiting on about how nearby Arundel Castle was used by Dr Who? to turn
king George into a madman, I wasn''t concentrating, more interested in trying to understand why
the goats in the adjacent field had turned Rick into a raving, lathering mad-dog. 
After a short bit of messing around at the start we set off due East on Crossbush Lane, which is
a cul-de-sac created by the Arundel Bypass that only got as far as Crossbush because of NIMBY
activists who have saved Binsted Woods on the other side of the river, leaving massive tailbacks
either side of Arundel every day of the week, pumping out CO 2 as the cars creep haltingly along,
causing climate change, that gives rise to global warming, that is going to create wild fires
that will destroy millions of acres of woodland elsewhere.... oops, sorry I''ve drifted off the
subject, any way Binsted Woods are great for hashing if you can find a place to start. 
In the time it takes to milk a goat, Ollie and Max had gone out of sight at the cul of the sac and
North into Poling Copse, where a plethora of possible paths posed a positively perplexing
puzzle for panting hashers. 
No worries though the pair out front had chanced on the true trail and Kinky was in hot pursuit
blowing for all his worth, even finding a third higher note from between his moist lips, soon
he will want us to rename him Sashmo, it''s a wonderful world. 
We reached Blakehurst Lane and headed North on the winding up hill tarmac, would have been a bit
boring, however the hares treated us to a series of three fish hooks in the space of the one
kilometres to the sharp left hand bend at the top of the road. Could list those caught, however
can''t be asked, there was a need for constant alert on this part of the run, due to the continuous
stream of petrol heading North using this narrow lane as a rat-run to avoid the long tailback
on the A27 at Crossbush, caused by the premature end of the by-pass, caused by the NIMBYs, causing
climate change ..... wait .... stop ... don''t get me started, we have already vented this spleen. 
Here we took a regroup, Alexia whipped out her bag of icing sugar coated jelly babies and we chatted,
some picked blackberries which are ripe very early this year, probably due to global warming caused
by NIMBYs ... stop it, some watched more cars raise the dust as they headed South towards Clay Lane
and the front of the queue. 
We, though took fresher air as we left the tarmac and continued North to join Monarchs Way,
taking the steep decline into the deep valley beneath Warnincamp Hill. Sausage Lottery was lured
down a falsie at the top of the drop, I couldn''t see what was down there, whatever it was it
induced a series of expletives that we all heard, where did she learn those words?, I blame the parents. 
Once in the valley, the pack favoured the lazy way West, after yet another fish hook that caught Ollie,
Kinky, Old Faithful and Dr Blood, gently downwards following the stream
on it''s journey to the Arun, and there was a good trail, and running was lovely on soft grassland, and
the birds were singing, and the temperature was just right, and the hills were alive to the
sound of music, and I was about to burst into song when, suddenly we needed all the breath in our bodies,
some have bigger breaths than others, in order to make the almost vertical climb South to Common Barn.
They say every cloud has etc. and here there was a reward, because after the leg cramps and red mist
induced by the climb had subsided, behold! a wonderful …

