(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2025-08-17T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1066 - Appledram Lane South - Church Farm', N'A large crowd gradually gathered at the Spider people''s gaff at Apuldram, bringing food,
cash and raffle prizes on a literally cloudless blue day which coincided with run number
1066 an important number in the history of this sunny sceptred isle. 
There were virgins, all grown up and "is it far?" Karen, and lots of growing to do, twins
Ella and Storm the progeny of Alexia. There were welcome backs for long time no see
Vixen and occasionally seen Tumble Tot, Rob (Grasshopper''s dad) and Louise. 
There was a brief refresher course on hash marks, memories of cross checks now fading
into obscurity, before we set off using the time honoured back door into Spiderman''s
rear entry, which involves cocking a leg over a fence and then ducking in a dark thorny
tunnel to emerge on Saltern''s Way. Immediate drama, as the weakness of Spiderman''s
mortice and tenon joints yielded to the inertia of Alexia''s bodily motion according to
Newton''s first law and a bit of his second for good measure. The result, fence on ground,
Alexia on ground and no leg cocking required for the rest. 
Immediate check on SW and the pack took ages to find the flour on a trespass path North
across the brackish Lavant to the public footpath which runs West over towards a suicide
crossing of the A27, we of course didn’t go that way, it''s certain death, no, the Spiders,
god bless em had been out with the shears and cleared the path back over a rickety
bridge to SW. This turned out to be walled with blackberries, about fifty yards of ripe black
fruits glistening seductively in the bright sun, impossible to pass by without emerging
purple handed and a mouth full of those annoying seeds that lodge between the teeth and
yet a smile on the blue lips. 
Continuing North on SW we followed it across the tarmac of Apuldram Lane and very soon found
another trespass path West towards Fishbourne creek over the hard bone dry stubbly dusty
earth left after the recent harvest, till we reached the retreating tide at the bridge at the top of
the boardwalk. Lots of possibilities from here, lots to check, and the true trail North towards
Fishbourne church checked and found last. Even so, a bunch of tail enders took out a big
chomp of trail on a blatant shortcut. 
Then we are wandering around the church car park, it''s nice and shady, no rush then, time
for a natter while someone else looks for the white stuff, then it''s found, South now, past a big
water trough, ideal for Rick the dog to use as a plunge pool, to reach the mill pond stream
with the two wooden bridges. Here a real splash fight broke out amongst some of the pack.
The water here is crystal clear and running freely, drought?, what drought?. A bit of a wet T
shirt moment then, although the sight of Bika in a wet clingy T shirt might make some feel a bit
queasy. 
South again around the high tide path and back to the board walk, where Old Faithful had been
busy relaying flour to confound the pack anew. This time it took the deductive powers of
Dancer to expose the way on the other side of the hedge adjacent to the board walk and then
on over the sewage treatment outflow. Many a quip in poor taste here, "is this your turd run?"
"Two reasons not to drink the water here, number one and number two", etc. AND a fish hook
that caught all the FRBs Dancer, Rob, Smokey Bacon, Olive Oyl, Popeye, and Caught Short. 
A hundred yards further, and we are out on an open hay field, and a problem of sorts, because
the walkers, on their own route appeared at the far end of the field, somewhat spoiling the
lure of the upcoming falsie towards the pub at Dell Quay. Thus it was that we were soon all
gathered in the cool gloom of another church car park, that of Saint Mary The Virgin at Apuldram,
you didn''t know that she was a hasher did you, yes her son also, apparently he took a long
false trail and spent forty days in the wilderness with the Bethlehem HHH. 
After a good chat and …

