(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2025-09-14T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1068 - Emsworth Sussex Brewery', N'So there we were, gathering outside The Sussex Brewery on a cool grey fresh morning of
dryness sandwiched between two days of autumnal monsoon. A welcome visit from Chilly Willy
, Dr Pussy (co hare) and Camping Gaz
and a virgin Hash for Maxine who, if you remember, gate crashed our BBQ apres for a few
hot sausages and a down down. 
Our JM had a short babble about Emsworth''s maritime history, although of course we were not
in Emsworth Hants, we were in Hermitage West Sussex, the pub name being a big hint.
This did not prevent him going on to mention oysters, but he got away with it. 
Sausage and Dr P then refreshed our short term memory with a squirt or two of flour
on the tarmac, and we were about to set out when Bika arrived, so we spent a short
while berating him for his tardiness, he nearly always comes last, or so I am told. 
Then we set out, and like so many times in the past assumed the slightly obscure path
down the side of the pub would be the way to go. Not so, not even a false, leaving quite
a crowd wandering around the boggy, thorny pastures tip toeing between piles of horse ordure. 
Meanwhile, the first of the few had made it West to Lumley Road and made a loop in the marshes
next to the Ems before crossing the ricketty rackkety wooden bridge across the Ems and into
Hampshire, without any sign of the expected passport control. 
What a contrast!, the gentle rural vibes of Sussex give way to the mean streets of Emsworth
and the throbbing bypass ahead, can''t even hear myself think, maybe I wasn''t thinking anyway,
yes that''s it, not thinking, Hashing. So down the steps and into metro graffiti of the
gloomy underpass and up out in uptown Emsworth. 
Passing Loretta''s Coffee Bar, which is worth a visit before you kick the bucket, and a long
long stretch of suburbia South West on Warblington Road and the South to Western Parade via
Beacon Square. Suddenly we hit the harbour, what a contrast!, now the smell of drying weed
brought up on the morning''s tide, the jangle of halyards, the eyes relaxing as they set
their focus to infinity on the wide horizon. 
A shot vessel thrust into my hand, promptly filled with Cockburns by Cock Burns, probably
celebrating the recent naming event at Nash Hash, aH! that hit the spot, more please while
Old Faithful took a snap. Then before any giddiness sets in we are off East, crunching over
the pebbles to the sailing club and then North on the oyster bed wall, The Promenade, to The
Quay. Oh! now a fish hook half way there, caught Old Faithful, Olive Oyl, Camping Gaz, Chilly Willy,
Cock Burns and Bambi. 
A shot vessel thrust into my hand, promptly filled with a Sausage potion, and a dip into a bag
of licorice all sorts, nice combination, leaving bits of black on the teeth and a long warm
heady after glow. Now we ARE giddy, need to pull into the pit lane at South Street car park.
Guess who comes screaming into the next bay?, Smokey Bacon, so we have a deeply philosophical
conversation about the meaning of life before a slightly wobbly unsafe exit from the pits and try
to catch the back of the pack West down Queen Street, where we find a regroup. 
A nice natter by The Lord Raglan, not sure what about now. We could have just easily cantered
on inn from here, however who could resist the draw of a belt down the top of the narrow dyke
on the West of Slipper Mill Pond. Concentrate, concentrate, concentrate, a stumble or wayward
step along here, salty mud on one side, brackish water on the other, bits of flint sticking up,
port and potion giving false confidence and impairment at the same time.
Phew, made it, turn back and watch others making the same trade, before it''s over the small
weir and back into Sussex. 
Can''t be far now, can it?. Well yes actually as we slide South into the boat yard and jetties
of Emsworth Marina where there are plenty of paths to weave a trail. We ran along Mill Quay and
then Heron Quay before a …

