(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2025-09-28T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1069 - Midhurst - Grange CP', N'Gathering at The Grange car park we competed for spaces with those attending the
"indoor car boot sale". Is that not just a jumble sale? I didn''t see any cars inside the
leisure centre hall. Anyway, there was plenty of room outside on a gloomy morning of
high humidity, it felt like it should be raining but just couldn''t quite manage it. 
Slightly DHN had genned up on Midhurst, apparently Henry VIII came to nearby Cowdray
House quite often to help Boris Karloff look for the bride of Frankenstein, at least that is
what I thought he said, I was not concentrating, I was remembering the hangover I had
the morning after we celebrated the matrimonial coupling of Pink Flamingo and Dr Blood
here many moons ago. 
We had newbies, as Lindsay Lindsey had dragged along Wayne and Kate, and would you
credit it, we set off without explaining the flour glyphs to them. At first no one seemed to
want to leave the car park, the get up and go had got up and gone, with the pack milling
about swapping views about the statue of Dante and the chance of spotting Egyptian
Geese on The South Pond. 
Then the chase was on as Dr Blood found a trail South West through Spring Meadows
besides the stream and to the main road opposite The Fire Station, where the pack took
a long time to find the obscure path that is squeezed between the perimeter fence and
the deep gully that leads to Mead Way. This is real suburb territory, if you could class
Midhurst as "urb", with tarmac and paving underfoot, so it was with some relief that we
soon found that the trail went into Holmbush Play Area, but not after Cock Burns, Ella and
Storm had taken in the long falsie round Holmbush Way. 
The play area has a small rear exit (or entrance depending on your approach) that goes
through a remnant of Pitsham Wood and out on to the Bepton Road, where we continued
South West, not far to the crossing with The Serpent Trail. Lots of checking here, and it''s
where we realised that Wayne had not been educated in the art of trail markings as he
took a load of the pack on the "Head" route (who said head?) which was false. 
In the brief confused hiatus, Sausage took the opportunity to step aside for a bit of behind
the bush relief and hope no one would notice, while a few found the "Tail" route West
into the woodland of South Midhurst Common. 
Unfortunately, this area is well known for hash saboteurs, who had been diligent in removing
a few essential hundred yards of our trail. If you are thinking of laying a trail (Please!) then
bear this problem in mind if you use paths that are popular with walkers. In this case
the hares provided a nod and wink in the right direction, which lead North and down into
the disused sand pits with their pair of small lakes, still plenty of water here even though
we are having a very dry year. 
Here a regroup and natter, with the Jelly Babies that are becoming a habit, and now a bag
of those gummy worm things that look like a Star Trek Earworm (cf. The Wrath of Khan),
I popped one in my mouth at the risk of sucking off a crown, being sure to keep it well away
from my external auditory meatus. 
After a good chat and throwing a few sticks into the pond, we set off again continuing North
on lesser used paths and old bike tracks, up hill to the dead centre of Midhurst, yes, the
cemetery (old chestnut that). This part of the common is criss-crossed by many public footpaths
which served well as the pack were all over the place amongst the Rhododendrons, even
marking a check with a false trail direction at one stage, oh yes, and needing another break for
wormy things. 
At very long last, we happened on the trail down the Northern slope to the main road opposite
The Half Moon. No stop for a swig or two here, time was running out on the parking ticket and
anyway we were eager to go up June Lane (don''t!) and down the permissive path round the rear of
the college. A sudden fish hook here caught …

