(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2025-10-12T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1070 - Eartham Woods', N'With only one JM, Rob, present we were summoned to circle on an overcast, cool Sunday awaiting to be
informed of some interesting facts about Eartham Woods. No such luck. 
Apart from being part of the Monarchs Way and Stane Street we were told of the birthday of Comes
With Dad now with Jason in Canada. If you are reading this Tali, then hope
you enjoyed the first snowfall of the skiing season. 
The one and a half hares, Popeye twisting an ankle yet again, explained that there
were no falsies so if you found flour you were on and 3 for any fish hooks we came across. 
Relying on Cock Burns and Dancer to do the running and lead the pack we set off north
where Rick did his normal trick, one of many, and delayed Cock Burns letting the Ref take the lead. 
Although there was a fair turnout, the majority were content to amble along right from
the start. Old age and injuries holding them back, nothing to do with the long up hill
stretch. Dancer, Dr. Blood and Kate were caught out at the first and only fish hook
just before we turned east and headed downhill to a 3 way check. 
With the Ref charging up hill sharp eyed Cock Burns spotted flour off piste on a tree
in the woods to the left. Coming out of the trees and heading downhill we came across
our JM looking as surprised to see us as we were to see him. 
After a short regroup, with customary Jelly babies, we were off again heading to the
furthest point of the woods and to a check where the pack was waiting for confirmation
of the correct way from Dr. Blood and Dancer who were a couple of hundred yards ahead.
Our JM got inpatient while waiting and accused them of chin wagging rather than looking
for flour. At the next 4 way check I remembered Bambi telling me not to bother carrying
straight across as it only went into a long loop, so I headed downhill to the right.
A little knowledge is a dangerous thing! Yes, I was way behind after retracing my steps
up hill and heading straight across. Thank you Bambi! 
Still it turned out to be a lucky break as way up ahead the pack were struggling up the steepest
of hills to a check at the top. Kate had anticipated this and taken a path to the right just before
the climb giving me a chance to catch up. A couple more checks led us to the infamous 5 way
sign post and the walkers relaxing on the bench.
Not only Jelly babies, good job it was a large packet, but Cock Burns port were handed around,
with seconds for some. With us all gathered the JM decided this was a great opportunity to video
us wishing Tali happy birthday. With some calling her Comes With Dad and others just Tali we
seemed to finish singing at different times. This was sent to her later which she really
appreciated or she pretended to. 
We said goodbye to the walkers but Rob came with us until he decided he would take a
shortcut back. Cutting our way through the woods and over paths we became stretched
out with our two front runners out of sight. As with all good hashes they went wrong at
the next check and the hash was back together again. We reached the point that
we often used when coming On-IN but we had to go the longer way round. Here it was
reported that Dancer had been checking Strava on his phone seeing where the chariots
were and which way to go! 
We were back at the chariots in 1hr. 28mins after 4.1 miles of very enjoyable hashing.
Rob eventually arrived back after his short cut and awarding himself the Hash-it for getting
lost yet again. 
Refreshments were taken at The Anglesey Arms where we enjoyed the company
and gossiped forgetting about down downs.
Thanks to the hares for a great hash which worked well keeping us together without falsies.
 On-On! Old Faithful 

 Spiderman''s track (did most!) ... AND ... 
 photos by Old Faithful

(Imported from https://www.chihhh.org.uk/run1070.php)', NULL, NULL, NULL, N'United Kingdom', NULL, NULL, N'Eartham Woods', NULL, 50.887992, -0.666469, geography::Point(50.887992, -0.666469, 4326), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 1070, N'Olive Oyl and Popeye', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, GETDATE(), N'Import chihhh.org.uk', @countryId, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2025-10-26T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1071 - Hilsea - Coach&Horses', N'Even with the clocks going back the pack of a dozen still turned up on time across the
road from the pub. Dressed up as witches, skeletons and I don’t know what, and didn’t
like to ask, we attracted the attention and bewilderment of motorists and the puzzled
public who smiled politely and moved away quickly. It was difficult at first to know who
was who the only giveaway was by seeing which dog was dragging them along. 
The J.M’s informed us about some Irish man called Jack who thought it was a good
idea to put a candle in a potato in order to save souls, which later became our tradition
of lighting up pumpkins. The hares spoke of fish hooks and regroups before letting the
pack free to search and find the trail that went down Perron Road. At a check,
nearly at the closed bridge, we were delayed by a couple of witches trying to find a
quite spot to relieve themselves. No such luck, as that was the true trail heading east
at the foot of the Napoleonic defensive ramparts. A long, curving climb took us to
the top where Rick was Rick not only delaying Cock Burns with his usual no. 2’s but
managing to soak his costume coat and himself with his number 1’s. 
After a few well placed checks the true trail went down a fairly short steep drop to the
right to a parallel path halfway down. Rick, making up for lost time, sent the witch Cock
Burns flying and both nearly ending up at the very bottom of the mound. Up the slope
again over and under fallen trees to the top. 
Eventually we were altogether again. With Dancer returning up the slope from the
northern side, we were led down the south side to the entrance of the rooms in side the
fortifications. Cock Burns was heard shouting from behind us that she had been assaulted.
Turning around she had a floury hand print on her backside. Only one person was
carrying flour and he was in disguise. Mind you, with the smile on her face she obviously
enjoyed it (See photos). The entrance was through a very small gate into the spooky
darkness. Exiting we thought it was about time for refreshments. Jelly babies and Cock
Burns’ port were handed around with many photos being taken of the very decorative graffiti. 
Off again through the narrow path to the steep steps which led again to the top and the
path heading east. Coming first to the fish hook were Kinky, Dancer and the Ref but as
the path was so narrow they had nowhere to go so just stayed where they were until
the back markers caught up. At last we left the defences and ran along the road
towards the Hilsea Halt railway station. Up onto the bridge where eager beaver Miss
Wednesday and Dancer carried on running missing the steps down on the other side of
the road. With a right at the roundabout we were in the industrial estate, going left, right
and left again until we were back at the bottom of the defences. Pirate Dancer, with all
his running, became sweaty and hot, and stripping down to his vest caused Lindsey to
nearly have a heart attack. Through the tunnel where vocal shouts were sent echoing
and on into Fox’s wood and a regroup. The port and jelly babies again made an appearance,
just giving all who participated enough energy to run along the side of the Hilsea moat. Rick
was helped into the water by Cock Burns’ boot to wash the number 1’s off which did improve
his smell. Up and along the main road and back to the chariots in 1hr. 28 mins. 
A quiet corner was found in the Coach and Horses for a hash gathering. As there were many
families eating their Sunday roast, down-downs were kept to a minimum. Nominees included
Miss Wednesday for her eagerness in checking out trails, Kinky for forgetting the beer but
the hash-it was awarded to Dancer for his enthusiasm and stripping off his shirt among other things. 
Sausage Lottery brought her bag out with a wonderful variety of home made jams and pickles,
which I can highly recommend trying.
 On-On! Old Faithful 

 photos by …

