(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2025-11-09T10:55:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1072 - Halnaker Anglesey Arms', N'Everyone arrived in good time for this Remembrance Day run. Coincidentally on this day last year we started from Tangmere
(Run 1046 Hiawatha and Smokey Bacon) and half the route was over the same flat terrain, supping ale at the Gribble Inn
which shortly after burned down, so much has happened since then, a year''s worth actually.
This time thanks to climate change, it was much warmer and drier, although the same shade of grey. 
As we waited for the big hand to reach eleven our JM entertained (I use the word loosely) us with some
local trivia gleaned no doubt from AI''s answer to the question "whats special about Halnaker?", unfortunately
it had not mentioned that the pronunciation is "Hanaker" with a silent L, lets not get started about the difference
between Cosham and Bosham. 
So many times this account includes a mention of the noise made by barking dogs as we wait to start. Not on
this morning, as we stood in our silence there was a tiny bit of whining which seemed to add to the poignancy
of the occasion. 
Ready to go then ?... No!, pub not open and Linsey Linsay needs a wee, no jokes about finding a bush please! as
we ran off and let be what will be, don''t look back it might make you blind. 
In no time at all we dealt with the six way junction at the crossroads, mostly because of many short falsies,
and were soon embarked West on Redvins Road with the endless but pretty high flint faced wall on our left.
On and on and on with Wayne (#theotherwayne), who was resplendent in his Findon Cricket Club touring
shirt, in our kind of yellow, and The Ref getting well ahead of the rest. 
The flour density along here could be described as "light", so much so, that the further we went the less
confidence we had, and the rush slowed to a trot and then a walk. To be fair, much of the flour had fallen
down into the long grass verge or been sucked off by the vortex behind passing vehicles, which do
belt down this long straight country road.
There just had to be a check at the turning to Goodwood Home Farm, and there was, faint, but there. 
The obvious choices were either "Private" or false, leaving the only direction to the East, back down one
of the Permissive Paths that the Goodwood Estate has created recently
 Goodwood Paths .
A run halfway back to from whence we came, on soft, lush, green green grass, with a fish hook that
caught Old Faithful, Kate and Kinky, before heading South East
across Stane Street to Boxgrove Village Hall and it''s many possible onward paths. It served as a good
opportunity for us to regroup as hashers wandered around a bit clueless, until the obvious trail South
down The Street was found and then the inviting Easterly progress along Church Lane, past The Priory
and on to the undulating flatland of the Tinwood Vinyards with Sausage Lottery out front. She has mixed
feelings about these parts on account of getting lost here on run number 998, on a seriously miserable
wet day at the fag end of 2022. 
As we passed by the corner of the priory grave yard we had a regroup, where Cock Burns dished out
some heart warming shots of liquid that might have contained alcohol, and Caught Short asked us
to raise the aforementioned in remembrance of
 Billy Fiske 
the 1932 Olympic bobsleigh champion, the first American to be killed in action in WW2, who''s
grave is right by the footpath. 
Quiet moment over we continued East into the vineyards to The Keepers House cross paths and then
round to Cockshot Dell T junction, including a fish hook that caught Wayne, Kate and Bambi.
The hares were muttering a bit about how slow was our progress
a message that was immediately followed by an up in our pace (not) as most of the pack headed North
leaving Bambi and Wayne to take the "it won''t be down there" trail and find good flour all the way to
Eartham Road near the power substation. This was a lovely soft run on hard sandy soil across fields
showing the first day …

