(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2025-11-23T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1073 - Rackham Old School House', N'The Ref said that he remembered starting a Hash at Rackham in the days of
Doc Freeman, the Hash AI bot has revealed that although another similar
venue exists in the anals, no run numbers coincide with it. So apart from
himself this was virgin territory for the rest. 
The hares had missed an early deluge and avoided the "we got soaked laying the
trail!" headline by the skin of their teeth, aided by an alarm clock malfunction
situation that stalled their arrival somewhat.
Nevertheless clear skies and a cool cool breeze, that we would have loved on July
1st, had arrived along with as fine a bunch of Hashers as you could ever want to
encounter. 
We had newcomers, Cliona, Edie and James, who along with the rest of us were treated
to interesting historical information about a mention in the Domesday Book,
followed by an outbreak of steel pan and spoon clanking from the kids, apparently to make sure that Slightly was fully awake, because he has a
habit of dozing off in fits of narcolepsy, frequently on soft couches after
a bevvie or three. 
First off the brat pack of Storm, Ella, Edie and James disappeared North, up the
contours and into the Tumuli like rats up a drain pipe with amazing ease, leaving
the rest of the pack to moan and groan their way up the slippery oak and beech
litter atop slimy roots. At the top there was plenty of flour with no long falsies
due to the smallness of the copse, and some bramble ramble was needed before we
broke out on to the Greatham Road heading North West in a big all together group
charging down the tarmac with James and Olive Oyl at the front, "Tally-Ho!". 
This all ended when we reached the path South West into Rackham Plantation and
found a tight anti clockwise loop that started at river level and wound it''s way
up the very steep edge of the ridge to a flat top with a commanding view of the
Arun flood plain and Amberley Wild Brooks. Needless to say the brat pack got there
first, which caught Bambi out, as he was implementing catch up plan A, which
was a frontal assault without going all the way around behind. 
Hooray! reward for being there as Cock Burns whipped out a flask of invigorating
Ruby and we soon found a medicinal dose nestling in hand as we gazed out over the
wetlands below, were we soon heading there?, a question on many a lip. 
We restarted, no takers for the falsie West straight back down, and soon we had
wandered around long enough on the crest to find a wiggly path through bracken
and bramble North, a craftily re-laid trail that brought on a bought of deja vue.
It took us back close to the chariots before turning West to stop for a "say cheese"
grouped around the fallen trunk of a massive oak. 
Continuing West we literally joined the West Sussex Literary Trail, try saying that
after a draft or three of Cock Burn''s medicine, as it turns South through the reeds
towards Rackham Mill. The young uns were the first to find that the nice green field
leading up to the mill was ankle deep in freezing water, for some reason known only
to themselves this induced fits of screaming, I wish I could still remember a time
when I would have done the same. Amongst the pandemonium Ella managed to have a
shoe sucked off, an even colder foot, and no sympathy from others, mirth in fact. 
We passed by the Mill House which is currently on the market for a cool two million
of your finest, if you are thinking of a purchase bear in mind that Rachel from accounts
will want to put her hand in your pocket at some point. Moving on from that thought
the hashers were led up the long sapping deep grass climb to the road at Cross Gate
by James, only a small chap (at the moment), however he is like a Duracell Bunny while
the rest of us are running on cheap zinc. We needed a regroup at the gate before spilling
out on to the road looking for flour. 
It was found East along the road and then on the footpath to Rackham Village, then …

