(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2025-12-07T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1074 - Patching Worlds End Pub', N'Bonkers - Adjective-Definition-mentally unbalanced; mad; crazy; enthusiastic; excited;
lacking reason; very eccentric; foolish; or irrational. NOT 
to be confused with the verb starting with same four letters. 
Yes that''s the word that fits as a description of the Hashers that turned up
for this Hash at The Worlds End. There was a chap in the garden of the pub
building a boat to save humanity from the coming flood. He wanted a pair of
Hashers to help breed some civilisation into the survivors, no volunteers,
after all we had a Hash to run. The boat had floated away by the time we got
back. 
Yes, you guessed, this Hash was driving rain from the laying of the trail
to the sinking of the last down down and beyond. 
The diehard core of regular hashers were about outnumbered by visitors
and virgins, firstly from Brighton HHH was Angel and Bouncer, lured because
the venue was on their doorstep; and then Karen, Pete, Sue, Alan, Sheila,
Alex and Sophie, I''m not sure, but it seemed that they all said that the Hares
had made them come. Sheila said that she was not Australian but had been
down under. 
I would like to have had my usual pencil scribble to aid my memory of this
Hash, however I am sure that you will understand that the notebook turned
into papier mache within the first five minutes. I know that only one JM was
present, the one of the female persuasion had been on the ten mile Guildford
Hash (Nativity pub crawl actually) the day before and was feeling tender all
over. So from under the cover of his monster rainbow umbrella our JM told us
about the local history, while I watched the steady flow of water that was
oozing out of Old Faithful''s hat, down his nose and splashing on to his boots. 
Bambi found the trail, which miraculously was more or less in existence, at first
heading West up the footpath alongside Arundel Road, sploshing along head down
and bemoaning the fact that he is a four eyed git and needing spec wipers from
Santa. This is the reason that he missed the footpath North over Orchard House
Farm, so that when he looked back he was all alone, Aww! 
Meanwhile the pack, having found the FP, were squelching along on soggy grass
through acres of newly planted vines and reaching the large new galvanised
gate at the entrance to Jewshead Wood. Here we were meant to continue North
through the wood, but the Hares considered the descent to be too dangerous
in the exceptional conditions, no one disagreed, we are not that bonkers. 
Luckily Wayne, was able to take us on a detour up Selden Lane, right at the
farm and back on track to continue North through a big, big field full
of very nervous sheep to reach a blocked off stile in the corner at the
entrance to the woods at Barnstake Copse. 
We were very spread out by now, as predicted by the Hares because a regroup
was marked. This was the nicest spot on the Hash, the shelter of the trees
reducing the velocity of the sheets of rain so that it fell vertically in
large consolidated blobs, almost feeling warm. And then out came the shots,
large consolidated blobs of a very different sort were dished out, downed
and definitely did feel warm. Not sure if it was Cock Burn’s Cockburns, you
know what they say "any port in a storm". 
What followed next was a tight clockwise loop around Patching Rough, some Hashers
love a bit of rough I hear and this did not disappoint. The hares, probably
worried that we might cut East across the hairpin shaped loop, had marked
all the trails to the right with a no go line, thus when we reached the top
of the loop and no more lines, it was an educated and correct guess to
head South. The only thing that held us up for a short while was Old Faithful
mistaking some soggy tissue paper for the trail, one wonders what goes
on in Patching Rough to leave so much soggy tissue paper around. 
Just before reaching Patching Copse we found the first fish hook, an artists
impression of a …

