(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2026-01-04T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1076 - Angmering Casa de Cock Burns', N'You need a better writer than this humble scribe to do justice to the account of what occurred at this "hangover" hash,
nevertheless, here we go. 
Cold, not just a frosty morning, the cold had been going day and night for the best part of a week beforehand so everything outside was cold to
the core, the soggy bits now crunchy, the tarmac a mixture of still wet brine and slick ice. I arrived super early at chez Cock Burn''s to ensure pole
position on the drive only to find it already occupied by The Ref''s chariot, somehow not surprising. 
The race to get the boots on and laced up before the ends of the fingers get all blue and tingly was on,
"no gloves?" I hear you, but I''ve tried and
always end up with them neatly bound in to the top of the boot. Concentrating on this and trying to make small talk with The Ref was a
challenge when things got more complicated as Caught Sort bounced up after having refreshed the trail he had laid the previous day and his smiling
face was all a glow as the first rays of bright sunshine burst over the horizon putting him in the spotlight. "It''s
not that cold once you get going" he said
with a broad smile, ever the optimist I thought, and then he added "I''m afraid it''s going to be a bit of a
long run", ever the sadist I thought, at least
no mention of wading as in some previous years. 
Lift the latch with dead fingers, open the door, and straight up the side alley to the backside, to see a garden and large patio with a whole set of
chairs and tables with cloths just like for a summer garden party with a totally blue sky above, only the leafless trees giving the game away.
I was clutching a firepit kindly loaned by Snake Charmer, "where do want me to stick this?" I announced to nobody in particular, hoping for a
reply from an empty garden, when I noticed another looking lonesome, so deposited a partner, can''t have too many firepits on a day like today. 
Hashers started to arrive now, quite suddenly, like starlings when they decide to condense on a favourite tree and start chattering. Some emerged
from the warm of the kitchen, others clutching raffle prizes, from the alley, "happy new year!" everywhere. Soon around thirty were gathered for a
"say cheese!" moment. Hooray! Dancer returned with the Hash Shit, another visit from Just Louise down from
the smoke, and a virgin, Dean, all watched our outgoing JM, Slightly, trying his best to form a circle out of a rectangle and failing. 
We were all enthralled by his
histrionics of the locality, talking about the legend of Colin Cowdrey who ran a Bath House here in the nineteen thirties, at least that was what
I thought he said, my brain was starting to freeze. Caught Short then did the usual trail warnings, as expected, then we settled on the number six
for any fish hooks and oh yes, apparently he had hidden some of his balls en route, "how many does he have?" I hear you ask (answers on a
postcard please) anyway these were for young eyes only. At long last we were rushing round and out to the street and start running,
start metabolising, start burning calories, get feeling back in the toes, squinting into the mellow glare of midwinter sun. 
North it was, up Arundel Road, NO! not west on Palmers Road, lots coming back from there, YES! left at the sports club for a tour of the outer
edges of the sports field, up, across, down, while too many to name shortcut on the hippopotanuse (Pythonagoruss''s theorem). We then meandered
North West towards the Vinery Industrial Estate near the A27, via the slippery edge of the pond in Steyne Wood where we hit the first
Fish Hook that caught Ella, James, Bambi, Smokey Bacon, and Dancer who objected and demanded VAR. AND shortly after another one
that caught Smokey Bacon, Wayne, Bambi, Ella, James and Dancer, this time no objections, it was a fair cop. 
At the estate few of us ventured towards the roar on the tarmac but didn''t find any flour on the …

Full run report: https://www.chihhh.org.uk/run1076.php', NULL, NULL, NULL, N'United Kingdom', NULL, NULL, N'Angmering Casa de Cock Burns', NULL, 50.833793, -0.48577, geography::Point(50.833793, -0.48577, 4326), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 1076, N'Cock Burns & Linsey Hangover Hash', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, GETDATE(), N'Import chihhh.org.uk', @countryId, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2026-01-18T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1077 - Iping Common', N'On the eve of "Blue Monday", "the most emotionally difficult day of the year ", the weather
did nothing to help dispel an impending gloom, a dribble day, no wind, no sun, the odd
spot of rain, a damp cornflake of a day. Despite all this, a good few hashers were motivated
enough to try and find a trail amongst the weald of Iping and Stedham commons. 
We shuffled together into an almost perfect circle, indicating that hashers have a refined
spacial awareness of their relative position and a balanced understanding of their societal
standing with respect to the centre of attention, this is well covered in my book "Sigmund and
the Hashers'' Dreams", available in all good bookshops. 
Apparently in 1077 Scott of the Antarctic arrived at the south pole carrying a chamber pot that
an Australian convict had stolen from William The Conqueror, at least that''s what I thought the
JMs said, I was too busy squinting at the psychedelic pattern on Kate''s shorts, doing that
focussing thing where you look beyond and suddenly see a three dimensional dolphin
or something, no joy though, except for .... no don''t go there!. 
The Hares (Old Faithful and yours truly) then gave a masterful and eloquent address to the
circle, as usual, most informative and edifying, before an elbow indication of the general
direction of departure, and we did depart. 
West, out on the open heather of Fitzhall Heath and South down the Roman road to cross
the Elsted road at the multipath junction at Fitzhall gate house. Pleasant ground feel on the way,
slightly spongy peat, gentle slopes, big sky, and an unusual sombre pack, quiet enjoyment,
like stretching just after getting out of bed. 
All change then, in the dark paths amongst the rampant, towering rhododendrons on Mitchell�s
Common, lots of calling, a sense of urgency and the short seemingly tireless legs of James, Tawney
and Any Port, taking them way out ahead as we made our way South around the deep waters
of the sand pit, unseen behind the green curtain. 
By the time the main pack reached the open area at the South West corner of the pit, the mini
pack of three had sorted it all out and were well on their way East alongside the still unseen
Southern shore, until they reached the devilish fish hook, which caught them and an equally
sprightly Smokey Bacon. 
Together again, the pack found it''s way to a regroup at the Minsted Road, via Andrews
Lane, managing a mass trespass South of the farm cottages on the way, bit like sheep,
one goes, they all follow, I''m humming that tune from The Messiah, 𝄞 All we like
sheep have gone astray. We have turned etc..... 
At the regroup a medicinal dose of cherry brandy was approved by Dr Blood and jelly babies
made available to take away the horrid taste, holding the nose didn''t work, I found out after
several attempts. 
Now we waved, "see you later", to the Walkers with Pink Flamingo clutching the return map,
while we set off over the flat open slithery fields of Minsted Farm, James in front again. Something
was smouldering in the yard and casting an earthy slightly acrid smell into the still air, probably a
mixture of straw and horse dung from the stables, maybe something else. 
If you know this area and you are Spiderman, you know that we must be heading for one of
only two places to cross a major tributary of The Rother, which means that he was amongst
the group to reach the bridge first and enter the tall tall pines of The Warren. This area was
cleared of rhododendron some years ago, now it is regrowing from the small pieces of root
that remained and is just above head height, perfect for obscuring the many paths here, perfect
for hashing. 
The posse of youngsters was lured down a long false towards Tyland Farm, and the rest
bamboozled by a back check before discovering the big stream leap North towards The Severals,
The leap was a test of confidence, looking at it it''s not far, and the water not deep …

