(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2026-02-01T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1078 - Hill Brow Jolly Drover', N'Including Horrors, Ten CH3 hashers turned up for our visit to Haslemere''s run where a virgin hare
just Sam was due to be named. This was a very popular run, with hashers from North Hampshire
H3 and Deep Cut H3 as well as H4, although several familiar faces here run with all these Hashes. 
Without a word of a lie, the rain that had been persistently pissing down all morning, stopped on
cue at eleven and started again just after we finished running at about twelve thirty, and it had been
raining hard, as the small lake at the entrance to the Jolly Drover car park attested. 
Once we had loosely gathered together on the shore of this lake, Chilly Willie began her address of
welcome, not exactly your epistle to the Corinthians, all over before I''d had a chance to whip out my
little jotter, something that doesn''t happen very often, honest.
" Good morning, welcome, over to Sam, the Hare". Then Sam, just as quickly to the point,
with a review of what he had laid, without prevarication. So we circumnavigated the waters edge
and hit the road without knowing anything about this day in the past, or the history of the area, or
anything unimportant. 
South East on Rogate Road to start, along to the Serpent Trail car park and into the woods and into
the gloom of Long Bottom. There is nowhere flat in this bottom, the trails would be great for a walk, but
for running they demand constant concentration. The main hazard are those roots that run diagonally across
the path, slimy, and threaten to take your feet from under you to the right or left without warning.
Although Bambi holds the 2025 tumble tot award, I am glad to say that this time he remained corporally
upright throughout. 
With such a large pack, and it being "one dot and your on" the progress was rapid, check loiter time
short, only slowed by the frequent gradients and descents as slippery as The Cresta Run. 
For a while after the start, James and Bambi found themselves to be FRBs, unexpectedly, as
we headed East across Tipsall Lane, being lazy and following the contour, and finding the magic
spot, time after time. 
This all came to an end when we nearly reached Rogate Road again, and the trail headed South
and steeply down into the heart of Rogate Common, only just able to remain upright. The sound
made by someone who is battling with gravity and a lack of friction is hard to describe, you know what''s
happening when you hear it. Sort of "WOOOOOOEEEEEEEEEAAAAAAAGH!!!!". Sometimes this
sound is followed by that of "PHEEEEEEEW!" and occasionally by that of snapping twigs and brambles
and "OUCH", "OUCH" and > Redacted by Ed 
Sam produced a big packet of FRUIT PASTILLES , yes I know, no Jelly Babies, sorry if this is ungrateful
but well, I mean, how can you suck the head off a pastille, and anyway they pull out your fillings.
Salvation for Sam was all right, all right he had a little bottle and it was all right. It was full of
German hazelnut schnapps, but not for long. We all thought that it had an immediate rusch of
spirit followed by a long and complex finisch, "Ahhh, Haselnussschnaps" as they say. 
At the restart we parted company with those desiring a shorter slog, who waved cheerio as they
disappeared into the trees, North, up Tipsall Bottom. The masochist pack were now treated to
a long loop, on undulating terrain, open fields with a surface like ripe Brie, some slick tarmac,
flat lanes with massive pools of floodwater, and the most literally "in the groove" running you
can get, where streams have found a path of least resistance down the footpaths, turning them
into a deep Vee shape, every muscle and tendon below the waist getting a good workout here. 
All this took us firstly West to Tipsall farm then South
and West around the pumping station, all the way to The Sussex Border Path. Then North to
Carrols and back to Tipsall Lane and re-join the shorter route. 
Back into the trees, and all uphill from …

