(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2026-02-15T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1079 - Stansted Park Sth CP', N'Any more runs like this and we will be developing webbed feet and quacking.
At first it looked like no one had turned up, but no, everyone was waiting in
their chariot until the last moment before emerging into the cold wet deluge,
like that moment when you decide to bend your knees and take the plunge
when wading out from the shore. 
Also a whole bunch washed up on the way back from the Worthy Winchester
"BlackTie" St Valentine''s dooo, they needed to sober up, so this was like an
unwelcome bucket of cold water 
Eventually,of course, we were all outside in it, and Bika was telling us a whole
load of day in history stuff, apparently Galileo had driven the Russians out of
Afghanistan, at least that is what I think he said, I was wondering why there
were no dogs around, and how the hares, who''s sodden skin was very pale
and even more wrinkly had managed to lay a trail today. 
Considering everything a decent turnout, most intending to walk covered in
rain gear, holding umbrellas etc. and only a few planning to have a more
enjoyable unprotected Hash. Fish hooks were out there, could catch a real
fish today, and would mean only two of us would swim further. 
To start we all spilled out on to The Avenue and made a right pig''s ear of
finding the trail, I remember thinking that if we don''t find a trail soon I''m
going home. The flour had not washed away, it was there, we''d have had
the same problem on a dry day, and it led us North East across Broad Walk
through the pearly gates and along the main drive to the big house. 
The rain was heavier as we turned North through Cooper''s Wood and Hare
Warren (yes imagine a warren of hares), and left the squelch squelch squelch
of wet daps on tarmac for the splosh splosh splosh of ankle deep mud on
top of hard slimy flint. Oh Yes, a fish hook here caught Wayne and Bambi. 
A swift turn left to West was forced on the hares because everywhere else
here seemed to be a "Keep Out" conservation area, we are short of rainforest
after all. 
Back across the road at the North car park and into Stansted Forest, a vast
wilderness of criss cross paths that was once brilliantly used to create a GPS
track that spelt "888" on
 run number 888 
obviously, as Bika informed us. 
You know the adage, "do as I say and not as I do", well at this point Bambi, totally out
of character managed to go a long long way West on no trail, and the pack started to
follow. A false trail mark hove into view, but for a trail from somewhere else in the maze,
so it was all back to the last check, splosh splosh bloody splosh, South to try and catch
up the walkers who had already started to check out the wide open space of the
Northern Ride. 
Here in the open you can run on virgin grass, off the quagmire path, easier going, yet
looking North West to an horizon of infinity and beyond, with possible trails to right and
left, and the cold rain having found a way past your collar and running down the middle
of your back, well, it is hard to see how this could be enjoyable, yet somehow, the
masochist hidden inside finds a perverse pleasure. "Speak for yourself" I hear you say. 
Someone found a trail, Old Faithful ?, taking us North to the big log seat at the confluence
of many paths. Bambi whipped out his snappering equipment as there was an easing in
the downpour, everyone said "Cheese", having their souls captured and imprisoned in
a digital box for eternity. 
If you get the chance you really must visit this seat with some friends, walk up to it, sit down,
have a chat. Then stand up and pose the question "can anyone remember by which path
did we arrive here?". I''m betting that there will be confusion over which it was, and you
yourself will be none too sure. Thus it was with us, even the Hares could not remember,
which was okay because at least they had laid a trail, like Theseus. 
Our way through was South West on the PFP towards Rowland''s Castle and then …

