(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2026-03-01T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1080 - Slindon Park Lane', N'Saint David''s day, and guess what? Welsh weather, rain, although we must have been very good little Hashers
since the last run because it cleared up as we arrived at the Park Lane car park, and drove through the two
lakes at the entrance. 
All things considered, a good turn out, helped by another visit from Alex and Sophie who are on a hunt for
running opportunities during March, hoping to total 50Ks in aid of charity. Also visitors Margaret, Shyra and Jim,
all the way from Harare HHH, they were well wrapped up against the cold damp day, obviously. 
We started off with a debate about the birthplace of cricket, Slindon, just over there on the Common, or Hambledon
near the Bat and Ball. Somehow Yellowstone park got involved but I think that this might have been something
to do with this day in history rather than cricket, the dogs were taking more interest than me. 
There is a whole bunch of directions that emanate from here on Park Lane, and with no help from the Hares
we started out with Hashers scattered thither and yon, moving quite quickly, getting warmed up, finding the
many falsies that to their credit the Hares had spent some effort to lay. 
T’was Old Faithful that happened upon the true trail, South West into Slindon Wood and then North West
as the trail meandered in that narrow band of scrubby woodland that is sandwiched between Slindon Park
fence and the Park Pale. Plenty of opportunity to come to grief in here, roots, brambles, twisty, turny, tiny,
trail, flour on right or left, top of stumps, dropped in obscure holes. It was a relief when we at last headed
West "beyond The Pale". 
I noticed that The Ref had an everlasting daffodil pinned to his cap, for St David, and we had a short conversation
regarding whether a daff or a leek is better which did not reach a conclusion, rather like the earlier cricket debate.
Then I mistook Bika for someone who knows where he is going and followed him on a long long false trail.
Meanwhile everyone else had reached the footpath that comes up from Duke''s Road and were heading North
towards Slindon Bottom. There were several well laid checks as we proceeded up Slindon Bottom, so we were
all back together when we reached tarmac at Top Road with the prospect of Nore Folly ahead, built for
a picnic by some rich bird in 1814, why not take your hamper up there when it''s warmer and the bluebells
are in profusion in Nore Wood, you won''t regret it. 
Oh, yes! back to the Hash, where we mixed it a bit with the "petrol" on this usually very quiet road as we made
our way East to the slopes beneath Slindon College, and then took a very shiggy trail up the slope, back inside
The Pale to reach the Pale path heading North again. This time I didn''t follow Bika when he thought he knew
where he was going, a wise choice because he was going nowhere except along the path that leads towards
a pond, which becomes progressively more shiggified as a logarithmic function with distance, who would have
thought that he knows such swear words?. 
Others also had a surprise as the true shiggyless trail had a fish hook that caught Dr Blood, The Ref, Wayne,
Alex and Sophie, leaving us all together to meet up with the walkers at the bower bowl seat where we always
stop for a flash when in the area. Then there were shots. Not the bang bang pheasant popping feathers fluttering
sort of shots. These were some sort of port and blackberry infusion, produced by an alchemy that would have
had the Hares declared as witches in the middle ages, which they are, but we don''t burn them anymore, at least
not in this part of Sussex. Then more shots of the photogenical sort, no need to say "cheese" due to the effect
of the previous shots. 
Empties collected, nattering over, and we were off again towards the college with Wayne''s team in front until another
fish hook caught them, Old Faithful and Bambi. Good actually because we all arrived at the entrance …

