(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2026-03-14T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1081 - IOW Nodes Point', N'Memorable visit to the Isle of Wight and the Nodes Point Caravan Park. 13th March 2026.
 Chi HHH smuggled ourselves into this event with Worthy Winchester hosting the Friday and Saturday
and IOW HHH the Sunday.

There were three levels of athlete: runner, walker and Spiderman with a bad knee.
 Friday Evening was a night Hash. Torches glistened as the words spread that the route was along the coastal path towards Ryde. A muddy track lead in that direction. We had just eaten a hearty meal of Chilli con Carne and it inhibited athleticism greatly! 

Dr Blood reported that he tried to follow the trail and the early self proclaimed ‘slow’ runners who started early. Then there was a cryptic mark pointing inland. But the coast was clear and unambiguous despite the dark. Opinions varied as to where we were heading. but in dark he arrived at The Solent as advised, but the Landlady told him to go away, also to the surprise of the skeletally dressed Friday hare!

The pack eventually got to the Simeon Arms in Ryde and although not expected (a busy band evening) we were given a back room where we could shout lots.

The walkers got very strung out but they managed to rendezvous with the runners. Meanwhile Spiders and Pink Flamingo arrived much later thanks to mobile phones
.

Then you either got a 21:05 #8 bus back to the caravan, while those with stamina got some gory entertainments, having moved to Harry''s Bar and opted for the 23:05 bus. 
 Saturday morning 60 hashers lined up for the On-Out.

Dr Blood reported that he tried to follow the trail but it was too bright but he got to The Vine pub to find the beer stop.

The walkers seemed to head off in the same direction as the previous night at a gallop.

Spiderman gathered up 6 walkers and six dogs who could not keep up with the pace and had a gentle stroll to the beach for a hot chocolate.

Then we all got together for protracted down down''s, we had the usual crimes and rubbish singing before we got to a naming ceremony.

Our Karen stalwart nibbles provider (& nothing to do with the USA slang version) is now to be known as DipsinDipsout, unfairly for supposed patchy attendance.

Eventually, lunch of soups and sausages appeared at 15:00.

Then we all tried to get into Cock Burns caravan to drink cider or elsewhere to BeerPump''s for nice real ale.

I gather there was a Pimms party and a late night for the youngsters. Chichester revelry was rather muted.

Sunday was IOW HHH''s turn. As it was nearly St Patrick''s day we had green flour and a few silly outfits.

The walkers were given a route and once again managed to throw off Spiderman who went back on his tod. Dr B. was called on to pull the previous skeletal hare out of a thorn bush and a ditch. We hashed for ages through bogs and fields helped by back checks and regroups. 

The walkers missed the beer stop by miles and thus their hare gained the Twat Hat. But in their efforts to find it, they broke into an unsuspecting van looking for drinks, to the surprise of a great big Alsatian dog.

Thankfully we rejoiced in the clement weather with the rain storm perfectly timed to miss us as we recovered indoors.Things got a bit hazy after that; Guiness, Cider and Vodka Jellies were to blame. Rugby was watched. 
 All in all despite a lot of whinging about communication and marking of trails, all agreed, the squirrels were red and it was REALLY GOOD FUN.

On-on

Spiderman, Dr Blood and Pink Flamingo

 photos by IOW photographers

(Imported from https://www.chihhh.org.uk/run1081.php)', NULL, NULL, NULL, N'United Kingdom', NULL, NULL, N'IOW Nodes Point', NULL, 50.704608, -1.1025265, geography::Point(50.704608, -1.1025265, 4326), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 1081, N'Worthy Winchester and IOW Weekend', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, GETDATE(), N'Import chihhh.org.uk', @countryId, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2026-03-29T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1082 - Whiteways Lodge Car Park', N'To begin at the beginning. It is spring in the grey windswept Whiteways car park.
The alfresco, chip butty, breakfast in a bap cafe, is long gone, as are the clumps
of rattling, revvy, chit chatting bikers, deterred by the lack of diet busting, belt busting,
lip smacking, grub. 
Hush!, Bika is speaking, the rolling bowling Rs flow over Henry and Wars of the Roses and
President Andrew and procuring Alaska, while the whirly, wavy, psycho deli crazy pattern
of Kate’s nethers consumes our attention. 
Listen!, we have a son of Haslemere amongst us, a boy of sorts, a bright and smily boy, a
lanky boy, a Head Boy, Hurrah! 
Look!, these are our Hares, this is "I may have been down under", Sheila, and there is Kate
and they are the architects of our morning''s delights, and they are pointing, yes pointing,
to the chalk white, flint firm, friction free, mouth open path, down into the damp wood.
And we let it swallow us whole. 
And then I woke up, to find that no matter how many times I clicked my heels I was still in
Houghton forest and following Wayne, North, down the stony Denture path to the triangle
at the bottom, where we had a good old run around finding nothing but the check. 
Now us old timers have bobbed about in these parts many times before, and therefore
think we''ve got it covered. Not so. Evidently the Hares had spent quite some time before
hand in a forensic examination of old bike trails and deer tracks and possible even used
a compass (shock! horror!) to lay a trail from the triangle North East up the
 Mill Ball , where we meandered around
ducking under branches, tripping over roots, on lovely soft mossy soil with that woody, mushroomy,
composty, almost tobacco scent in the thick misty musty air, and the warm breath of the person behind,
and the psycho deli pattern ahead and ..... 
Oh! ... where was I?, Ah yes, somehow we came out at the top of Langham Wood for a lovely
re-group. Someone said we were waiting for the walkers, but they never showed up, and anyway
the jelly babies were nice and we recognised where we were, felt safe again, and had a chat
about something and nothing as usual, by the way the state capital of Alaska IS Juneau, not
what Bika said, which means that I could have told the joke where you ask "Whats the state
capital of Alaska?" and the other person says "Juneau" and you say "yes, I know, but do you?",
which is a rattling good joke as long as the other person does know the answer. Wait a minute!
perhaps Bika said the wrong answer because he didn''t want to hear the joke.. doh! ...
I need to speak to my therapist. 
There were four ways to go from where we stopped, Bambi chose a falsie, as did The Ref before
Wayne never came back from the North, and we all followed him up past Langham Buildings
towards the SDW. This is lovely running, tall airy pines above, soft pine needle loam beneath
gentle slope and even though a grey day, you could hear birdsong as presumably the boasty
males were sounding out the territory, "over here ladies", "listen to this". 
Just shy of the SDW the hares had spotted an unmapped track North West right along the
Northern boundary of the forest which Old Faithful took up, glad to be following flour rather
that the lumps of soggy tissue that he has found of late. 
Again nice, fast, easy running, took us to the turn South and down the valley that has the
"Bomb Crater" on the right where we often find an impossibly steep trail, but not today, we
passed it by to reach a fish hook that caught Head Boy, Wayne and Bambi. And then another
almost immediately, not sure who, I was getting tired, almost an hour gone, then another
re-group, and another "say gorgonzola", more jelly babies, just in time. 
Now we were back at the triangle, no one wanted to check in any direction that we had been
before, leaving the down hill access road South East as the favourite. A nice graded gravel
path this. Once you get a rhythm …

