(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2026-04-12T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1083 - Graffham St Giles', N'I had barely exited the chariot, the retracting seat belt rattling behind when I heard a Hare saying
"Do we look like people who want to run up hills". Let''s leave that there for the moment. 
A combination of "Gran Duties", injury, and pestilence meant for a meagre turn out aside a
narrow lane at the foot of the Downs on a dry grey April day, brightened by the presence of
larger than life Sir Malibog. 
Some last minute Googling by Cockburns turned up that it was National Cheese Day and on
this day the first Shuttle was launched to the moon, piloted late at night by Dave Letterman
to check if it was Cheddar or Wensleydale, which was a waste of time because everyone knows
its Emmental my dear Watson, you can see the holes. 
The magic Fish Hook number was set at two, before we girded up our loins and issued forth
into the countryside. A quick check up past West Lodge proved fruitless, while good trail was
found North past the lytchgate of St Giles which has seen the last journey of many a soul since
1066. We on the other hand are very much alive, and none more so than Sausage who streaked
off (not literally) West on the lane past Tagents Farm with The Ref in pursuit, a relationship
that would blossom later. For now it all came to and end as they reached the first Fish Hook and
we were all together once more. 
A few checks North and South, and we continued West at a good pace on soft grassland just
waking up from winter. Then we got to Dirty Lane and the trail flirted with the foothills by turning
South up Golden Coombe Bottom on a track that had recently been hit by a flash flood,
washed out boulders, and fallen trunks clad in impenetirable ivy.
Still we like a challenge don''t we?. 
Led by Dancer who seemed not to notice the obstacles we fought our way to a rest at a landing
of sorts carved into the slope. Then to our relief, a false trail on the upward continuation and
good flour on the contour path East, apparently homeward bound, all easy peasy lemon squeezy
from here. How wrong can one be. Let me tell you. 
Soon, a cross paths, Dancer ahead, calls "ON" South West UP Long Bostle, we follow;
some hikers on the way down say "good luck mate", all I can see, a yard in front is a wall of chalk
and flint, I sprain my neck looking up to see Dancer skipping upward, probably humming "Oh!
what a beautiful morning" or something. 
Up and Up and Up and Up no let up in the Up, until unbelievably I see Dancer leave the trail by climbing
the vertical slope to our left, your avin a larf I thought, I look behind and see Bika, hands on hips,
It looks like he''s shaking his head, but my eyes are stinging with salty sweat and its all blurry. 
Still we like a challenge don''t we.? 
By clinging to roots and kicking toes into moss we managed somehow to "Summit" on the SDW at
the top of Broad Walk, three at first, then Sausage who looking back at The Ref says "I have had to
drag him up that last bit" which although you might doubt, was probably true, because The Ref
did not contradict her. 
When finally Caught Short arrived we wasted no time with the verbal abuse, there was no energy
left for any other sort. He placated us with port shots, and a refill or three and mentioned that this
was the second time today that he had been here, first carrying flour, second with a bottle, so
in the end we were all merry and thanking him for this opportunity to commune with nature. 
There now followed a much easier (obviously) canter East more or less on the SDW, during which
we encountered a Fish Hook that caught Dancer and Bambi, the former using the run back to
practise his sprinting, yes. Then the descent. Caught Short said "beware, sometimes going down
is almost as hard as getting up", so true I thought, as we gathered speed on the path back to West
Lodge. Here, the slightest stumble could lead to a headlong hard contact with razor sharp flints,
all treated this surface with great …

Full run report: https://www.chihhh.org.uk/run1083.php', NULL, NULL, NULL, N'United Kingdom', NULL, NULL, N'Graffham St Giles', NULL, 50.94191, -0.678589, geography::Point(50.94191, -0.678589, 4326), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 1083, N'Caught Short & Cock Burns', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, GETDATE(), N'Import chihhh.org.uk', @countryId, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2026-04-26T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1084 - Arundel Torton Hill Rd', N'There was a traffic incident at Crossbush, "just over there" from where
we were gathered at Park Bottom. Where we waited, and waited and waited.
Only fifteen minutes in the end, and it was a very pleasant, sunny, end of April
day to be exchanging pleasantries. 
No expense had been spared on research into the historicals of the day, that is
to say there had been no research, so we were spared the need to concentrate
on anything important apart from welcoming a newbie Nikki before the get go.
We had a decent turnout with a good bunch of walkers, which meant quite a lot
of running was up for grabs as we ventured forth to explore. 
Dr Blood and Bambi found themselves wandering around the nearby cemetery
a bit distracted by the Royal Sussex Regiment war graves and not finding any
flour before being called West across the road and down by the riverside and
under the bypass into town. 
There was an attempt to lure us up King''s Arms Hill, yet the trail which had been
laid the previous evening had survived well tucked beside lamp posts and doorsteps
or sunk in cobblestone cracks, taking us as close as possible to the river bank to
reach the Queen Street bridge. 
Visitors, everywhere here, not going anywhere, sitting on anything flat, leaning on
anything upright, outbreaks of selfies, sarnie munching, dodging the traffic, a
British, reserved, version of the sort of chaos one sees at a busy tourist hot spot
in Italy say but without all the honking. 
As far as trail finding was concerned, well, a bit of a nightmare really, running around
staring at people''s legs while shouting "checking" or "how are you?" might seem a little
perverted to some. Thankfully Bambi did not get arrested before he spied that the hares
were leading a bunch of walkers past the heritage centre. 
Now out on to the flood plain along The Klondike dike where a fish hook caught Old Faithful,
The Ref, Slightly D, and Bambi. Then along Pecky''s lane East to the Arun and following it
up-stream. No checks possible on this long curving stretch of the bank. No tourists out here.
A nice firm dry path, lined by tall reeds, the light breeze making them hiss, the sun on ones
back, and as we progress around the arc, a broad view of the castle basking in the
brightness to the West. What a lovely spot on the planet on this spring day. 
The inevitable fish hook caught She Wee, Dr Blood, Old Faithful and Bambi, before we
arrived at a Sip Stop on the wooden bridge in the Wetland Centre. Here the magic Porty
potion was very generously dispensed by Cock Burns while we chit chatted and regrouped
and remarked at the clarity of the water that was flowing from Swanbourne lake
causing the healthy looking weed to wave gently in the current around the bright orange discarded traffic cone. 
With a newly glowing interior, we resumed, the walkers making a circuit of the lake and the
rest running along the North shore and then under Box Copse, where Old Faithful and Bika
discovered that it''s not entirely true that what goes up must come down. Having climbed
a very steep false trail off, they turned to return, the vertigo struck. Great entertainment
for the pack as the pair awkwardly extricated themselves, bit like having two cats
stuck up a tree. 
The path up this valley is indeed steep sided, and the short legged lively Horribles
had made use of many of the trails off and up for falsies, any of which to the West
would have to be used to get home. Checking these without success proved very
tiring for the pack, believe me. 
Eventually one of these was a goer, and it took us South up the trail to the Hiorne
Tower folly, which was used by Dr Who in the 25th anniversary story "Silver Memesis".
If he had not rid the planet of them we would now be ruled by Cybermen, far worse than
politicians don''t you think?. 
We were now very very tired, so the down hill romp back to the chariots from here was
most welcome. On the way we …

