(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2026-05-10T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1085 - Selhurst Park West', N'As old Billy Shakespeare wrote in "As You Like It", with an "On" and an "On" and a
sweet "Nonny non" when birds do sing "Hey ding a ding" the Hashers love the Spring.
We all love the new green in the woods and the fauna busy regenerating, but what
tests us is the rain, the light drizzle that floated down throughout this run.
At first the trees act as shelter, the leaves intercepting the wetness, then later
they act as lenses, concentrating the mist into large droplets that thunder down
at the least breath of a breeze. Sir Old Faithful and Sir Bambi mused on this
duality while sucking the odd extra strong mint and dispensing the requisite quantity
of Tesco''s budget self raising along the chosen paths and tracks, thither and yon.
We used to suck Fishermen''s Friends but can no longer handle the ensuing laxative effect. 
The Ref arrived a million years before everyone else as usual, and by the appointed
hour a good size mob had gathered, including the fresh face of Róisín, the long long
unseen face of Two Ferrets Fighting, and the odd times seen face of Splasher. 
Cock Burns didn''t do the this day in history, she did this place in history, as waves of
deja-vu brought recollection of her first run with us, same place, same hares, but not
raining. Sometimes "The Past" seems to always be a sunny place. 
Off we went then, West, straight on a long falsie, led by Dancer, who was wearing a
stab vest, or as the Old Bill prefer to call it, "Covert Body Armour", weighing around
three kilos, all part of the master plan to loose weight. Although at first sight one
could be forgiven for thinking it was a ladies corset, to help make it look like
he had already lost weight. 
The pack returned to find the true trail, parallel but in the woods, where the high
green canopy was still acting as an umbrella making the progress on a bouncy blanket
of brown pine needles a true delight. From then on all the running was predominantly
in the green gloom of deep woods, and importantly dry, the occasional emergence onto
bright open spaces only served to confirm that it was still raining. 
We continued West, loosing contact with the walkers, led by Sir Spiderman and went North
down into Chiseldown, into a new clear felled area, here we all got wet, no cover, and
having to pick our way over the discarded brush, plenty of flour here, all following
the trail in single file like a Centipede. Q:- What goes "99" "Bonk" ? Ans:- A Centipede
with a wooden leg of course. Here we were going "100", no bonking here. 
We broke out on to the old path half way up Wallerdean Hill, Alex, Sophie and Róisín in
front, all three checking the South option, close together in a compact group of three,
like the maids from school in The Mikado. They were on a false trail however and were
called back, good, otherwise they would have been even further ahead. 
The pack meanwile continued North and then South East more ore less on the contour
around Potcomb and then under the power cables on Selhurstpark Hill and up to the main
footpath that goes to Adstead Down. A fish hook on this section caught Dancer and the
three maids who were back out in front again. 
Here we met the walkers, well done Sir Spiderman! we always knew that you would find
the rendezvous, we never doubted you would be here, no not for the briefest moment,
no not at all, honest. We broke out a bottle of Ruby and bags of Jelly Babies, so with
sticky, sweet, fortified smiles we attempted a snapperoony with fill in flash. 
A short while later, and after a bit of a hunt, we found the trail, well off piste
on more crinkly brown beech leaves and tons and tons of the light yellow beech blossom
that looks like sawdust, making flour hard to spot as we careened down the slope East
to reach the trail to Droke. 
Dancer, who had not yet been stabbed by anyone, decided to add calories to his exercise
app by choosing to check up the steep way South, towards …

