(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2026-05-24T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1086 - Iping Common', N'Obviously the headline must read "Phew! what a scorcher", you could tell, hardly
anyone but Hashers in the car park, where the temperature was around 27 degrees.
Only Vixen was missing from the Hare party, Nikki, Tumble Tot, Prancer, Dancer, and
Cheesy Chips, they had been on the common for a few hours and seemed to be suffering
from
 Boiling Frog Syndrome 
not noticing how hot it had become. 
Runs from Iping frequently attract Hashers from up north, and today was no exception, as we
welcomed Dick Head ("I''m a peripatetic hasher"), Nutcracker, Yellow Peril and Yorkie (Deep Cut),
Chilly Willy (Mostly Haslemere), and Dipstick (Oxford). 
Unfortunately there was time for Cock Burns to educate us about this day in history. Apparently
inspector Morse had sent a dash dot message from Oxford (presumably) to The Invisible Man
who was living in Iping at the time, so he had to dress up in bandages to go to the Post Office
to collect it. It was a parking ticket. 
Out of the kindness of their pounding hearts, the Hares said that the planned trail had been
shortened on account of the heat, but then added that the real reason was that they had run
out of flour. A bit of debate and the fish hook limit was set at five, before we set out in all
directions at first but then getting on the scent East, across the Elsted Road into Steadham
Common on The Serpent Trail. 
A typical weald landscape unfolded in front of us, bits and pieces of various trees, sand lurking
in any trail erosion, bloody gorse roots to trip over, and enough heather to keep you in good
luck for an eternity. We all followed Dickhead, I won''t bang on about it, but we followed Dickhead
for most of this hash, the guy is heat proof. Where was I, yes, we all followed Dickhead as the
trail headed North and we diced with death to cross the busy main road, importantly into
the lovely shade of tall beech and some sweet chestnut to reach the school. 
Chilly Willy and Bambi managed to over run the check here and went a good way along
School Lane with blistering sun above and radiated heat from the tarmac. By the time we
caught up with the pack they were across the lawn from the Hamilton Arms, looking longingly
at a watering hole that would be open in forty minutes. The Hares had scrawled something in
flour on the grass (no wonder they ran out) to the effect "resist the urge to stay here and wait". 
Next bit was a frankly long trawl on hot tarmac North West into Iping, crossing The Rother and
stopping at a regroup under the trees opposite St Mary''s. Cock Burns whipped out her flask
and administered the spiritual revival fluid which had to be downed sharpish lest it evaporate
in the heat. Then the Jelly Baby chasers from Old Faithful. Then the usual Hash chatter, all
about something and nothing in particular. There is an oft heard joke, unfortunately not fit to
repeat here in detail, however it involves Superman flying over Iping and spotting Superwoman
sunning herself in the meadow. His intentions become decidedly dishonourable, so he swoops
down to satisfy a desire. Unfortunately for him things do not go well, and the last line in the
joke is "OOOps sorry I didn''t see you there Invisible Man!". 
We had come this far, we''d started, we would finish, thus it was after some checking around
the church we found flour West through the churchyard and out onto the meadow. Here Yorkie
and Bambi managed, totally innocently, honest guv, to check straight across the meadow and
at first missed the fish hook which was on the perimeter path. This did not go unnoticed alas.
Grudgingly they returned to join Dipstick, The Ref, and Old Faithful. 
The next bit, even though out in the hot open, was good running, just after crossing the
Roman Road, another fish hook for Dick Head, Chilly Willy, Bambi, Dr Blood and
Cheesy Chips. Then the Hammer bridge, and out across a perfectly mown Polo Gallop, with guess who?
way out in front again. …

