(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2026-06-07T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1087 - Arundel Dalloway Road', N'For this run a cool dry day appeared in the midst of a cold wet typical
Britsh "unsettled" period. Now we return to Arundel with recent memories
of local population poison powder panic pervading the FaceBookerSphere.
To head this off at the pass, Cock Burns had pre warned local residents
thus avoiding poison powder panic, and this had a side effect in as much
as two of the residents decided to join us. This would be Talya and Ray,
understandably tremulous but open to try new experiences as long as no
one dies. 
Angel and Bouncer (BH3) also turned up to add to a fair sized turnout
on a grassy verge on the edge of some decent looking suburban housing. 
Goodness me, this day in history came back with a vengeance.
Apparently Henry The Eighth had got all dressed up in gold and set the
slaves free to fight a war against France. A sort of Middle Ages Trump. 
Rain in the wee small hours had washed away the labours of Dean and Cock
Burns so Wayne had kindly stepped up to help relay just in time. In the
kerfuffle a load of chickpea flour had entered the supply chain. For those of
us who are not culinary experts, this flour is a superb binder, thickener and egg
substitute. Consequently the usual trail delivery apparatus, a hole in the top
of a milk container, had been thickened and bound and decidedly constipated,
although Cock Burns described the effect using more colourful language. 
Then we set off West to Priory Lane, following egg substitute, then crossed Torrington
Lane into the mixed woodland of Torrington Common. Here the running is
on narrow paths amongst quite young birch, hazel and beech, moving between checks
being a progression of single file interludes. 
Bouncer was moaning about his knee, which for no apparent reason had been hurting
since getting out of bed. "Must have been something I did in my sleep" he said,
no comment. With more checks than you can shake a stick at, and no horizon,
or particular slope, a sense of direction went up the spout, we were in path lottery mode. 
When we emerged going North on Binsted Lane did we know where we were? ... No!
before continuing West for another dose of follow my leader into Binsted Wood. 
On an historical note, the last time (I think) that we ran in this part was before the flood,
( Run 724 )
when Hairbrush buggered up his knee and we had to carry him to the main
road to meet the ambulance. 
We got to Scotland (top of group in World Cup as I write this, wee ha!) before heading
South all of the way to The Madonna Pond. Who knew that this was here ? This place
has many mystic associations, bottomless, haunted by those that have
disappeared into the dark waters, The Beast of Binsted, a shrine to the virgin Mary,
the list goes on and on (like this write up). 
What a great place to have a Sip Stop, out came the shots, thanks Cock Burns and
the Belly Jabies, thanks Old Faithful. Then a long natter about hauntings and such
like. Angel downs her tot, she says "Ahh! warms the cockles", I say "I didn''t know that
you had cockles", she says "there''s a lot about me that you don''t know", with that
Bouncer interjects "I keep Angel''s cockles, they are safe in two little pouches that
are hanging from my belt, do you want to look?". I thought it wise to not continue
with this conversation, messing around with someone’s cockles could get you into
a lot of trouble. 
Time to get going again, still South, but now out under the sun, suddenly finding
ourselves in waist high Winter Wheat, which was enjoying the changeable weather.
This soon gave way to chest high Rape, now waiting for the pods to ripen. Quite
a tour of the crops this morning. Then East across to New Barn, the path here
having previously been cleared of brambles and nettles by Cock Burns and Dean
using hedge cutters and a strimmer and determination. 
We arrived at Torrington Lane in dribs and drabs, Angel was miles in front, the rest
in twos and threes. All got …

