(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2026-06-21T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1088 - Bordon Hogmoor Inclosure', N'Two handfuls of us made our way up North to Bordon for the Deepcut
Midsummer Hash. To say it was a warm day would be lying, it was in the
"Phew! what a scorcher" league. On a wet day we linger inside the chariot
until the last moment, on this day of the Solstice we should have lingered
in the air con, but didn''t, we deployed the reflective dashboard protector
and bailed out into a furnace. 
Around thirty five masochistic Hashers eventually gathered in the only shade
in the Hogmoor Inclosure car park, on the edge of an ex army tank training
area, perfect for the odd hash or three. 
Golly gosh, Yellow Peril is trying to impart the markings to this multi hash,
group, and we cut him no slack, with many a zany heckle, the "One dot check"
a circle with a spot in the middle draws obvious rather lude comments.
"honi soit qui mal y pense" is what I say. What ?, sorry the heat is giving
me delusions, I''ve gone back to thinking I''m King Edward again when I am only
a knight of the hash. 
We set off, and were elbowed South East through the natural play area, great
for kids of all ages. There is a tall wood totem pole statue of a scrawny bald
masculine looking person with ambiguous genitals, but we can''t linger longer
as we head South into an absolute maze of tracks and paths in the dappled shade
of mixed light woodland. No climbs to speak of, plenty of energy sapping sand,
waist high bracken, brambles, nettles you get the picture. 
Don''t know the other hasher''s names, but Dancer and Bambi were doing a fair
share of trail finding and everyone was perspiring profusely, I''d say Bambi was
"sweating like a pig" which is an old idiom from our iron smelting past and
nothing to do with the farmyard animal, not many people know that, "or care" I
hear you say. 
In any event after much meandering around in this maze we crossed
the tarmac of Templar''s Way, which is a bit "back to the future" considering
our upcoming search for the Holy Grail Hash. Then South through some midge
infested swampy bits to reach Whitehill Village Hall, and now getting even
hotter, even our sweat was sweating, in un-filtered sunshine. 
Very soon then, turning West and back across the tarmac to a quick regroup,
waiting for everyone to negotiate the crossing. We were now tucked away at
bottom end of The Inclosure with no way to go but North, and importantly
back in the shade. I heard the hares congratulating themselves that most of
the trail was in the shade, I would have rushed over and given them a pat
on the back but I didn''t want to get my hand wet, "well done", splash, splash,
splash. Suddenly, a water stop, much needed and greatly appreciated. Many
bottles of your finest spring water got sloshed down, I think a can of beer
was passing around without any takers, yes I know, "I don''t believe it"
as Victor would say. 
As you might imagine we lingered here for a while before continuing our progress
North. One might have been forgiven for thinking that we were on our way back to
the chariots. One of our number was convinced and ran a long long way North without
any trail taking us all along for the ride. "On Back!!!!" from behind, turn around,
slog slog slog back, feet now feeling as heavy as breeze blocks, West and out of
trees and across Hogmoor Road at Warren Close. "Owwwww!!" another dose of Vampire
killing ultra violence, my Reactolite Rapides go dark dark dark. 
"Phew!" relief, back in the trees and into Woolmer Forest which as everyone knows
is the only location in the UK where all 12 of Britain''s native reptile and
amphibian species live and breed, so tread carefully here we wouldn''t want to nipped
by a snake. "Why couldn''t the Viper vipe her nose?", "Because the Adder had her handkerchief"
boom boom. I digress. Not far North here and we find ourselves on a meaningful
climb up through tall spindly pines, then another water stop at the top, well done
Hares for dragging heavy bottles up here. …

