(NEWID(), NEWID(), @kennelId, '2026-07-05T11:00:00+00:00', NULL, 1, 1, 0, NULL, 1, 0, N'Run 1089 - Angmering Spotted Cow', N'The Hash headline reads "Mad dogs go hashing on another scorcher". 
You know those spaghetti westerns where it''s all dust and parched earth
and tumbleweed, well on this definitely sunny Sunday morning on High Down
we might have added another movie to the series "A fistful of flour". 
A meagre turnout, only a few regular "Die Hards" (Oh! thats a different
series of movies), but hold on, an influx of welcome backs and virgins, to
save the day. "Hi!" to BH3 visitor Nicko, welcome back for Sophie and Alex
and "who made you come?" for Ella and Florence. These latter four arrivals
reduced the average age of the pack substantially despite the many years
of experience added by Nicko. 
A last minute dive into the online histrionics by Cock Burns revealed so
many interesting facts about Highdown that all the hasher''s limited brains
started to hurt, Bambi was forced to look away and go "la, la, la" in his head
to cope. Then the Hares made things worse by trying to describe their
markings. Apparently all the markings had a circle, by itself was a regroup,
with a cross inside was a check, with three dots inside, a falsie, it''s
like having Ketchup with everything. 
Without further adooings, we were off and messed about for a while on Cow Lane
before heading East over the wide footbridge across the main road. Looking
back, and noticing the number of new houses, part of a rash which has broken
out all over the South of England. 
Bambi goes North on Ecclesden Lane all by himself and finds a poorly scribbled
scrawl reading "On Inn", what to do?, keep it under his hat along with the
sparse stubble that he likes to call his hair, then go back to catch the pack. 
They are continuing East on the Lane, starting the long slow climb on a narrow
path between high restricting hedges before reaching a climax as the path
suddenly breaks out on to the wide open grassland of High Down, magnificent!.
On the way, two notable falsies North, the first up the path to Ham Spinney
from where the checkers continued by a long cut on the upper path, and a second
up the million steps which Bambi and Nicko struggled up and then down.
Oh yes! nearly forgot a fish hook that caught, Cock Burns, The Ref, Nicko and Bambi. 
Out on the slopes of the Down, under a massive dark blue blue sky the trail
was hard to find, the flour had filtered down into the grass. The main problem
however was the sheer number of rabbit holes, in some areas it was like trying
to run over a large piece of Emmental, not just obvious holes but burrows
collapsing underfoot. Yet, no sign of any roaming bunnies, not a single crepuscular
beastie in sight, one wonders if we managed to squish any ? No don''t think about it. 
Thus with quite a bit of Hare aid, we reached the shade by the tomb of John Oliver,
who ? ... wish I had listened to all that guff at the start. So this nutcase prematurely
erected this tomb 27 years before his death in 1793, at the time he didn''t know it was
27 years before obviously, a plague might have come along the next day then. 
Wayne delved into his rucksack and fished out a bottle of Port and some shot plastics
that had been concealed therein. Too hot a day for a sniffter? not so, Hashers and
sniffters, strawberries and cream, rhubarb and custard, duck and orange, these things
are meant to be. Not to forget the Jelly Babies which with their waxy surface survive
well in the heat. Well done Hares, we had a very pleasant respite in the shade next
to Johnny O. 
At some stage there was a move to continue under the noonday sun, so we wobbled out
blaming the odd stumble or two on the pesky rabbit holes, and found a trail North,
with another fish hook for the same bunch before diving
into the sweet sweet shade of Highdown Copse with its long long path down to Water
Lane, soft underfoot, it was so nice to get the legs into overdrive. Two more fish
hooks on this, same names, but not a problem as we were more or …

